window.RUST_QUESTIONS = {"rust-at-a-glance":{"topic":"rust-at-a-glance","title":"Rust at a glance","quiz":[{"q":"What are the two main things Rust is designed to give you at the same time?","options":["Speed and memory safety","A garbage collector and dynamic typing","Short code and no compiler","Automatic parallelism and a REPL"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rust's whole pitch is programs that are both fast and free of memory bugs, achieved without a garbage collector."},{"q":"How does Rust manage memory safety?","options":["A background garbage collector cleans up while the program runs","You call free() by hand like in C","The compiler checks ownership rules before the program runs and rejects unsafe code","It never frees memory, so programs leak"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust has no garbage collector and no manual free(). The compiler enforces ownership rules at compile time, refusing to build unsafe code."},{"q":"Which single concept is described as the core idea that makes the rest of Rust click?","options":["Macros","Ownership","Async/await","Unsafe blocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ownership is Rust's rule for who is responsible for each value and when it's cleaned up. It's the heart of the language."},{"q":"For a beginner, which topics should you save for later rather than start with?","options":["Variables and functions","Printing and if/else","Macros, unsafe, and async","Reading compiler errors"],"answer":2,"explain":"Macros, unsafe, and async are advanced. Beginners should orient first, then learn basics and ownership before touching them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What two qualities does Rust aim to deliver together?","back":"Speed (like C/C++) and memory safety (like garbage-collected languages) — with no garbage collector."},{"front":"When does Rust check that your memory use is safe?","back":"At compile time. The compiler enforces ownership rules before the program ever runs and refuses to build unsafe code."},{"front":"What does 'Rust is explicit by default' mean?","back":"Rust rarely does hidden things behind your back; you say what you mean, so code tells you the truth about what it does."},{"front":"What is the one big idea to learn slowly, because it makes the rest of Rust make sense?","back":"Ownership — who owns each value and when it gets cleaned up."}]},"install-rust":{"topic":"install-rust","title":"Install Rust","quiz":[{"q":"What is the official, recommended way to install Rust?","options":["rustup, from rustup.rs","Your Linux distro's package manager","Downloading rustc.exe from a random site","Homebrew only"],"answer":0,"explain":"rustup (from rustup.rs) is the official installer. It installs and updates rustc and cargo and can switch toolchains."},{"q":"You just installed Rust but your terminal says 'command not found' for cargo. What's the most likely fix?","options":["Reinstall your operating system","Open a new terminal so the PATH change takes effect","Delete rustup and use apt instead","Nothing, Rust is broken"],"answer":1,"explain":"An already-open terminal doesn't see the new PATH. Opening a fresh terminal (or sourcing ~/.cargo/env) usually fixes it."},{"q":"Which command confirms the compiler is installed and reachable?","options":["rust --check","cargo install","rustc --version","rustup remove"],"answer":2,"explain":"rustc --version prints the compiler's version number, confirming it's installed and on your PATH."},{"q":"How do you update to the latest stable Rust after it's installed?","options":["rustup update","cargo new update","rustc --upgrade","You must reinstall from scratch each time"],"answer":0,"explain":"rustup update fetches the latest stable rustc and cargo and swaps them in. Rust releases roughly every six weeks."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What tool installs and updates Rust, and where do you get it?","back":"rustup, from rustup.rs. It manages rustc and cargo and keeps them updated."},{"front":"After installing Rust, why might 'cargo' still be 'command not found'?","back":"Your open terminal hasn't picked up the new PATH. Open a new terminal (or run: source \"$HOME/.cargo/env\")."},{"front":"How do you verify the install worked?","back":"Run rustc --version and cargo --version — both should print a version number."},{"front":"How do you update Rust to the latest stable version?","back":"rustup update"}]},"cargo-basics":{"topic":"cargo-basics","title":"Cargo basics","quiz":[{"q":"What does `cargo run` do?","options":["Only downloads dependencies","Builds your code and then runs the resulting program","Deletes the target folder","Uploads your crate to crates.io"],"answer":1,"explain":"cargo run compiles your project and immediately runs the built program — two steps in one command."},{"q":"Which file lists your project's dependencies?","options":["src/main.rs","Cargo.lock","Cargo.toml","target/debug"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cargo.toml is the project manifest; dependencies go under its [dependencies] section (cargo add edits it for you)."},{"q":"You want to quickly check that your code compiles, without producing a finished program. Which command is fastest for that?","options":["cargo check","cargo build --release","cargo clean","cargo new"],"answer":0,"explain":"cargo check verifies the code compiles without generating the final executable, so it's faster — ideal while fixing errors."},{"q":"What is the purpose of Cargo.lock?","options":["It stores your source code","It records the exact dependency versions so builds are repeatable","It locks the file so you can't edit main.rs","It is a password file for crates.io"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cargo.lock pins the exact versions of every dependency, so the project builds identically on every machine. Commit it for applications."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which single command builds and runs a Rust project?","back":"cargo run"},{"front":"Where do a project's dependencies get listed?","back":"In Cargo.toml, under [dependencies]. You can add them with `cargo add <crate>`."},{"front":"What does `cargo check` do differently from `cargo build`?","back":"It verifies the code compiles without producing the final executable, so it's faster — great while fixing errors."},{"front":"What is Cargo.lock for, and should you commit it?","back":"It records exact dependency versions for repeatable builds. Commit it for applications."}]},"hello-world":{"topic":"hello-world","title":"Hello, world","quiz":[{"q":"Where does a Rust program start running?","options":["At the first line of the file","At the fn main() function","At whichever function is alphabetically first","Wherever you put println!"],"answer":1,"explain":"Execution always begins at fn main() — it's the entry point, the 'front door' of the program."},{"q":"Why does println! have an exclamation mark?","options":["It makes the text louder","It marks the end of the line","Because println! is a macro, not a regular function","It is optional decoration"],"answer":2,"explain":"The ! tells you it's a macro. If you drop it, the compiler can't find a function named 'println' and errors out."},{"q":"What does {} do inside println!(\"Day {}\", day)?","options":["It prints literal curly braces","It is a placeholder that gets filled with the value of `day`","It starts a new code block","Nothing, it is ignored"],"answer":1,"explain":"{} is a placeholder. Rust substitutes the matching value (here, `day`) into that spot."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the entry point of every Rust executable?","back":"fn main() — the program always starts there."},{"front":"What does the ! in println! mean?","back":"It means println! is a macro (not a normal function). Macros are called with a ! after the name."},{"front":"How do you print the value of a variable x inside text?","back":"Use a {} placeholder: println!(\"value is {}\", x);  or name it inline: println!(\"value is {x}\");"}]},"reading-errors":{"topic":"reading-errors","title":"Reading compiler errors","quiz":[{"q":"When several errors appear at once, which should you usually fix first?","options":["The last one in the list","The first one, then rebuild","The one with the longest message","A random one, they're all independent"],"answer":1,"explain":"One early mistake often causes a cascade of later errors. Fix the first, rebuild, and many others often disappear."},{"q":"In an error message, what does the `--> src/main.rs:4:20` line tell you?","options":["The number of errors found","The file, line, and column where the problem is","The version of the compiler","How long the build took"],"answer":1,"explain":"The --> line is the location: file src/main.rs, line 4, column 20 — where the compiler wants you to look first."},{"q":"What's the difference between a `warning` and an `error`?","options":["Warnings stop the build; errors don't","They are exactly the same thing","A warning still compiles and runs; an error stops the build","Errors are only shown in release mode"],"answer":2,"explain":"A warning compiles and runs but flags something suspicious. Only an error blocks the build."},{"q":"You see a line starting with `help:` suggesting a change. What should you do?","options":["Ignore it and guess your own fix","Try the suggested fix first — it's usually correct","Delete the whole function","Run cargo clean"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust's help: suggestions are right most of the time. Try the suggestion before guessing at other changes."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"When you get many compiler errors, which one do you fix first?","back":"The first one, then rebuild — later errors are often just fallout from the first."},{"front":"What does the `-->` line in a Rust error tell you?","back":"The location of the problem: file, line, and column (e.g. src/main.rs:4:20)."},{"front":"Does a `warning` stop your program from building?","back":"No. A warning still compiles and runs; only an `error` blocks the build."},{"front":"How do you get a longer explanation of an error code like E0382?","back":"Run `rustc --explain E0382`, or check the online error index."}]},"variables-and-mutability":{"topic":"variables-and-mutability","title":"Variables and mutability","quiz":[{"q":"By default, what happens if you try to reassign a variable created with `let`?","options":["It works fine; variables are mutable by default","The compiler errors: cannot assign twice to an immutable variable","It silently creates a new variable","It only fails if the type changes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Variables are immutable by default. Reassigning without `mut` gives 'cannot assign twice to immutable variable'."},{"q":"What is the difference between `mut` and shadowing?","options":["They are two names for the same thing","mut changes a value in place (same type); shadowing makes a new variable that can change type","Shadowing is faster than mut","mut only works on numbers, shadowing only on text"],"answer":1,"explain":"`mut` reassigns the same variable and the type must stay the same. Shadowing (`let x` again) creates a brand-new variable, which may even have a different type."},{"q":"What does `let x = 5; let x = x + 1;` do?","options":["Errors because x is immutable","Changes the original x to 6","Creates a new variable x (shadowing) whose value is 6; the old one is hidden","Creates two variables both visible at once"],"answer":2,"explain":"The second `let x` shadows the first — a new variable reusing the name. The old x is hidden, not mutated."},{"q":"You wrote `let mut n = 3;` but never change `n`. What does Rust do?","options":["Nothing, it's perfectly fine","Refuses to compile","Warns that the variable does not need to be mutable","Automatically removes the mut"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust warns 'variable does not need to be mutable' — a hint that your `mut` and your actual usage disagree."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you make a variable changeable?","back":"Add `mut`: `let mut x = 5;`. Without it, variables are immutable by default."},{"front":"What is shadowing?","back":"Writing `let` again with the same name. It creates a NEW variable (the old one is hidden) and it may have a different type."},{"front":"mut vs shadowing — the one-line difference","back":"mut = same variable changes in place, type stays the same. Shadowing = new variable reusing the name, type can change."},{"front":"What is a `const` in Rust?","back":"A value fixed at compile time, always UPPER_SNAKE_CASE with an explicit type, e.g. `const MAX: u32 = 3;`. Never uses mut."}]},"data-types":{"topic":"data-types","title":"Data types","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `u` in `u32` mean?","options":["Universal — works with any value","Unsigned — zero or positive only, no negatives","Unlimited size","Unicode text"],"answer":1,"explain":"`u` means unsigned (0 or positive). `i` means signed (can be negative). The 32 is the number of bits."},{"q":"Which default type does Rust pick for `let x = 5;`?","options":["u8","f64","i32","usize"],"answer":2,"explain":"With no annotation, an integer literal defaults to `i32`. A decimal literal like 3.14 defaults to `f64`."},{"q":"What is the difference between a tuple and an array?","options":["Tuples can hold different types; arrays hold one type, all the same","Arrays can hold different types; tuples hold one type","They are identical","Tuples can grow; arrays cannot"],"answer":0,"explain":"A tuple groups a fixed number of possibly-different types. An array holds many values of the same type at a fixed length."},{"q":"How do you write a single character value like the letter A?","options":["\"A\" with double quotes","'A' with single quotes","char(A)","`A`"],"answer":1,"explain":"A `char` uses single quotes: 'A'. Double quotes \"A\" make text (a string slice), which is a different type."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Decode the type name `i32`.","back":"`i` = signed (can be negative), `32` = 32 bits. It's the everyday default integer type."},{"front":"Array vs tuple?","back":"Array: many values of the SAME type, fixed length, indexed with [ ]. Tuple: fixed number of possibly-DIFFERENT types, accessed with .0, .1."},{"front":"Single quotes vs double quotes?","back":"Single quotes 'A' = one char. Double quotes \"A\" = text (a string). Different types."},{"front":"I need a list that grows. Array or Vec?","back":"A Vec. Arrays are a fixed length that can't grow or shrink; a growing collection is a Vec."}]},"type-conversion-and-casting":{"topic":"type-conversion-and-casting","title":"Type conversion and casting","quiz":[{"q":"What does `300i64 as u8` produce, and why?","options":["Compile error: value out of range","44, because only the low 8 bits of 300 are kept","255, because it saturates","300, unchanged"],"answer":1,"explain":"`as` between integer types keeps only the bits that fit. 300's low 8 bits equal 44 — no error, no saturation for integer-to-integer casts."},{"q":"What does `-1i32 as u8` evaluate to?","options":["0","-1","255","Compile error"],"answer":2,"explain":"`as` reinterprets the bit pattern rather than clamping. -1i32 is all 1-bits; keeping the low 8 bits as u8 gives 255."},{"q":"How does `as` handle a float that's way out of an integer type's range, e.g. `1e20_f64 as i32`?","options":["Panics at runtime","Produces undefined behavior","Saturates to the target type's MAX or MIN","Wraps around like integer-to-integer casts"],"answer":2,"explain":"Since Rust 1.45, float-to-int `as` casts saturate to the target type's min/max instead of producing UB or garbage."},{"q":"Why does implementing `From<Feet> for Meters` also give you `Feet: Into<Meters>`?","options":["It doesn't — you must implement both traits separately","std provides a blanket impl: any T with `From<T> for U` also gets `Into<U>` for T","Into is a supertrait of From","Rust auto-generates Into via a derive macro"],"answer":1,"explain":"The standard library defines `impl<T, U> Into<U> for T where U: From<T>`, so writing `From` automatically gives you `Into` too."},{"q":"When should you reach for `TryFrom`/`TryInto` instead of `From`/`Into`?","options":["When the conversion always succeeds","When the conversion can fail and you want a Result instead of silent truncation","When converting between two enums specifically","TryFrom is deprecated — never use it"],"answer":1,"explain":"TryFrom/TryInto return `Result<T, E>`, letting you catch and handle out-of-range or invalid conversions explicitly instead of truncating them."},{"q":"What trait powers `\"42\".parse::<i32>()`?","options":["Into","Parse","FromStr","TryInto"],"answer":2,"explain":"`.parse()` calls `FromStr::from_str` for the target type, returning a `Result<T, T::Err>`."},{"q":"What does `70_000i64 as i16` produce?","options":["A compile error, because 70000 doesn't fit in i16","4464 — the low 16 bits of 70000 reinterpreted as i16","i16::MAX, because it saturates","0, because it overflows to zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integer-to-integer `as` casts truncate to the low bits rather than saturating (unlike float-to-int casts); 70000's low 16 bits form 4464 as a signed i16."},{"q":"`fn to_byte(n: i32) -> u8 { u8::try_from(n) }` fails to compile. Why?","options":["u8::try_from doesn't exist","try_from returns a Result<u8, _>, which doesn't match the declared return type u8","n must be declared as unsigned first","try_from requires an explicit turbofish"],"answer":1,"explain":"`TryFrom::try_from` always returns a Result. The function must either return that Result type or unwrap/match it before returning a bare u8."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does `as` never do when casting between numeric types?","back":"It never panics and never checks that the value fits — it silently truncates or reinterprets bits."},{"front":"Widening vs narrowing integer casts — what's the difference?","back":"Widening (small type → big type) always preserves the value; narrowing (big type → small type) keeps only the low bits and can change the value."},{"front":"How do float-to-int `as` casts handle out-of-range values?","back":"They saturate to the target type's MIN/MAX instead of wrapping or producing undefined behavior (since Rust 1.45)."},{"front":"What's the relationship between From and Into?","back":"Implementing `From<T> for U` automatically gives you `Into<U>` for T, via a blanket impl in std."},{"front":"When should you implement From vs TryFrom?","back":"From is for conversions that always succeed; TryFrom is for conversions that can fail, and returns a Result."},{"front":"What trait does `.parse()` use under the hood?","back":"FromStr — `.parse::<T>()` calls `T::from_str`, returning `Result<T, T::Err>`."},{"front":"Does `-1i32 as u8` panic?","back":"No — `as` never panics. It reinterprets the bit pattern, producing 255."},{"front":"Why prefer TryFrom over `as` for a risky conversion?","back":"TryFrom catches out-of-range input as an explicit Err instead of silently truncating or wrapping it."}]},"formatting-with-format":{"topic":"formatting-with-format","title":"Formatting with format!","quiz":[{"q":"Which macro returns a String instead of printing anything?","options":["println!","format!","eprintln!","write!"],"answer":1,"explain":"format! builds and returns a String; the print macros write directly to stdout or stderr."},{"q":"What's the difference between `{}` and `{:?}` in a format string?","options":["{} uses Display, {:?} uses Debug","{} uses Debug, {:?} uses Display","They're identical","{:?} only works on numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"{} calls the Display trait (user-facing text); {:?} calls Debug (developer-facing, usually derived)."},{"q":"Why does `{p:?}` fail to compile if `Point` has no `#[derive(Debug)]`?","options":["It doesn't fail — it just prints nothing","{:?} requires the Debug trait, which the compiler checks at compile time","Debug requires unsafe code","Only tuples support {:?}"],"answer":1,"explain":"{:?} is a real trait bound (Debug). Without an implementation the code fails to compile — it never silently prints blank."},{"q":"What does `println!(\"{n:03}\")` print for `n = 7`?","options":["7","007","7.00","  7"],"answer":1,"explain":"`03` means zero-padded to a minimum width of 3, giving \"007\"."},{"q":"What does precision (`.N`) mean when applied to a string argument, e.g. `{s:.5}`?","options":["Round to 5 decimal places","Truncate the string to at most 5 characters","Repeat the string 5 times","Pad the string to width 5"],"answer":1,"explain":"For strings, precision means \"maximum length\" — the string is truncated to that many characters."},{"q":"Why does `println!(\"{p.score}\")` fail to compile?","options":["Field access isn't allowed anywhere in println!","Captured identifiers in format strings must be plain variable names, not expressions like field access","p.score isn't valid Rust syntax","println! only accepts numeric literals"],"answer":1,"explain":"Implicit argument capture only works for a bare identifier already in scope; field access must be passed as a normal argument, e.g. `println!(\"{}\", p.score)`."},{"q":"What must you do with the Result returned by `write!(buf, \"...\")`?","options":["Nothing — it's always Ok","Handle it (e.g. .unwrap() or ?) — Rust warns on an unused Result","Only handle it when writing to a file, not a String","Call .flush() on it first"],"answer":1,"explain":"write!/writeln! return a Result because the write can fail; leaving it unhandled triggers an \"unused Result that must be used\" warning."},{"q":"When are format string arguments (like `{name}` or `{0}`) checked for correctness?","options":["At runtime, the first time the line executes","At compile time, as part of expanding the macro","Never — invalid references are silently ignored","Only in debug builds"],"answer":1,"explain":"format!/println! expand and validate the format string at compile time, so a typo or missing argument is a build error, not a runtime surprise."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What's the difference between println! and format!?","back":"println! prints to stdout with a trailing newline; format! builds and returns a String without printing."},{"front":"Which macro prints to stderr instead of stdout?","back":"eprintln! (and eprint! without the trailing newline)."},{"front":"What trait does {} use? What about {:?}?","back":"{} uses Display (user-facing); {:?} uses Debug (developer-facing, usually derived)."},{"front":"What does {:#?} do differently from {:?}?","back":"It pretty-prints the Debug output across multiple indented lines instead of one line."},{"front":"What does the format spec `{value:>8.2}` mean?","back":"Right-align (>), minimum width 8, 2 digits of precision (decimal places for a float)."},{"front":"Can you capture a field access like {player.score} directly in a format string?","back":"No — only plain variable names can be captured; expressions must be passed as a regular argument."},{"front":"What's required to implement Display for a custom type?","back":"`impl fmt::Display for T { fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result { write!(f, ...) } }`"},{"front":"When are format string errors caught?","back":"At compile time — invalid syntax or a missing argument fails the build."}]},"functions":{"topic":"functions","title":"Functions","quiz":[{"q":"In a function body, how do you return the last expression's value?","options":["Write it on the last line WITH a semicolon","Write it on the last line WITHOUT a semicolon","You must always use the `return` keyword","Assign it to a variable called `result`"],"answer":1,"explain":"The final expression, written without a trailing semicolon, is the function's return value. No `return` keyword needed."},{"q":"What happens if you add a semicolon after `a + b` in a function that returns i32?","options":["Nothing, it works the same","It returns a + b twice","It throws the value away and returns () — a 'mismatched types' error","It rounds the result"],"answer":2,"explain":"The semicolon turns the expression into a statement, discarding the value. The function then returns () (nothing), causing 'expected i32, found ()'."},{"q":"Which is a correctly written function signature?","options":["fn add(a, b) { a + b }","fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { a + b }","fn add(a: i32, b: i32) { a + b }","fn add -> i32 (a, b) { a + b }"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every parameter needs a type, and a function that returns a value must declare it with `-> Type`. Rust never infers parameter types."},{"q":"When is the `return` keyword actually needed?","options":["Always, on every return","Never; Rust forbids it","To return early, before the end of the function","Only inside loops"],"answer":2,"explain":"The last expression returns implicitly. You use `return` when you want to leave the function early, before reaching the end."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does a Rust function return a value without `return`?","back":"The last expression in the body, written with NO semicolon, is the return value."},{"front":"What does a trailing semicolon do to a return value?","back":"It throws the value away — the expression becomes a statement returning () (nothing). Very common bug."},{"front":"What must every function parameter have?","back":"A type. `fn add(a: i32, b: i32)`. Rust never infers parameter types."},{"front":"How do you declare a function's return type?","back":"With an arrow: `fn name(...) -> ReturnType`. Leave the arrow off if the function returns nothing."}]},"control-flow":{"topic":"control-flow","title":"Control flow","quiz":[{"q":"What must an `if` condition be in Rust?","options":["Any non-zero number is true","A real bool (true or false)","A string that says \"true\"","Anything at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust requires an actual bool. It does not treat numbers as true/false, so `if count` fails — write `if count != 0`."},{"q":"What values does the range `1..4` produce in a for loop?","options":["1, 2, 3, 4","1, 2, 3","2, 3, 4","0, 1, 2, 3"],"answer":1,"explain":"`1..4` excludes the end, giving 1, 2, 3. To include 4, use the inclusive range `1..=4`."},{"q":"Which statement about `if` in Rust is true?","options":["if can only be a statement, never produce a value","if is an expression; you can write `let x = if c { 1 } else { 2 };`","if always returns the number 1 when true","if branches can each return a different type"],"answer":1,"explain":"`if` is an expression and can produce a value, but every branch must return the same type."},{"q":"Which loop is best for walking through each item of a collection?","options":["loop","while","for","if"],"answer":2,"explain":"`for item in collection` hands you each element directly and can't run off the end, making it the safest, most common choice."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Can you write `if count { ... }` where count is a number?","back":"No. Conditions must be a real bool. Write the comparison, e.g. `if count != 0 { ... }`."},{"front":"Difference between `1..4` and `1..=4`?","back":"`1..4` excludes the end → 1,2,3. `1..=4` includes it → 1,2,3,4."},{"front":"Name Rust's three loops and their use.","back":"loop = forever until break; while = while a condition holds; for = walk each item of a collection."},{"front":"Is `if` an expression in Rust?","back":"Yes — it can produce a value: `let x = if c { 1 } else { 2 };`. All branches must share one type."}]},"comments-and-documentation":{"topic":"comments-and-documentation","title":"Comments and documentation","quiz":[{"q":"Which comment style creates documentation that `cargo doc` turns into a website?","options":["// two slashes","/* block comment */","/// three slashes","# hash"],"answer":2,"explain":"`///` is a doc comment that describes the item below it and feeds `cargo doc`. `//` is just an ordinary human note."},{"q":"What is the best thing to explain in a comment?","options":["What each line does, restating the code","Why the code is there — the reasoning or a tricky edge case","The author's name on every line","The type of every variable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Good comments explain WHY, not WHAT. The code already shows what it does; the comment should add reasoning the code can't."},{"q":"Where must a `///` doc comment be placed?","options":["At the end of any line","Directly above the item it documents (a function, struct, etc.)","Anywhere, it doesn't matter","Only inside main"],"answer":1,"explain":"A `///` must sit directly above the item it describes. Floating with nothing below it is an error; use `//!` for inner (module/file) docs."},{"q":"What is special about code examples inside doc comments?","options":["They are ignored entirely","They are run as tests by `cargo test`, so they can't go stale","They slow the program down","They must be written in another language"],"answer":1,"explain":"Doc-comment examples are compiled and run as tests, which keeps the documentation honest and up to date."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"`//` vs `///`?","back":"`//` is an ordinary comment (a human note the compiler ignores). `///` is a doc comment that documents the item below it and feeds `cargo doc`."},{"front":"What should a good comment explain?","back":"The WHY (reasoning, tricky cases), not the WHAT. Don't restate what the code already shows."},{"front":"What does `//!` do?","back":"It documents the thing it's INSIDE (a whole module or file), rather than the item below it. Used at the top of files."},{"front":"What happens to code inside a doc-comment example?","back":"It's run as a test by `cargo test`, so examples stay correct."}]},"modules-and-crates":{"topic":"modules-and-crates","title":"Modules and crates","quiz":[{"q":"What is a crate in Rust?","options":["A single function","The unit Rust compiles — a package, either a runnable binary or a reusable library","A type of variable","A folder on your desktop"],"answer":1,"explain":"A crate is the compilation unit: a binary crate runs (has main), a library crate is used by other crates."},{"q":"By default, an item inside a module is...","options":["Public to everyone","Private — only visible inside its own module","Deleted at compile time","Automatically exported to the internet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Everything is private by default. You add `pub` to expose an item to code outside its module."},{"q":"How do you call a function `parse` from inside a module named `parser`?","options":["parser.parse()","parser->parse()","parser::parse()","parse.parser()"],"answer":2,"explain":"You reach into a module with the `::` path separator: `parser::parse()`. A dot is for method calls and field access."},{"q":"What does the `use` keyword do?","options":["Declares a new module","Brings a name into scope so you can type its short form","Deletes a module","Makes an item public"],"answer":1,"explain":"`use` imports a name so you can refer to it directly, e.g. `use parser::parse;` then just `parse(...)`. It does not create a module."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Binary crate vs library crate?","back":"Binary crate has `main` and runs. Library crate has no main; it offers functions/types for other crates to use."},{"front":"What's the default visibility of items in a module?","back":"Private — visible only inside their own module. Add `pub` to expose them."},{"front":"How do you reach into a module?","back":"With `::` (the path separator): `parser::parse()`. A dot is for methods/fields, not modules."},{"front":"`mod` vs `use`?","back":"`mod` declares a module (creates it or points at its file). `use` just shortens an existing name so you can type less."}]},"visibility-and-privacy":{"topic":"visibility-and-privacy","title":"Visibility and privacy","quiz":[{"q":"What is the default visibility of an item in Rust?","options":["pub","Private, scoped to its defining module and that module's descendants","pub(crate)","Depends on the file name"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every item is private by default — visible only within the module that defines it and that module's descendant modules."},{"q":"What does `pub(crate)` expose that plain `pub` doesn't restrict?","options":["Nothing different — they're identical","Visibility across your whole crate, but not to downstream users of a published crate","Visibility to just the parent module","Visibility only within the same file"],"answer":1,"explain":"pub(crate) is visible everywhere inside the crate, but external crates using this one as a library still can't see it — unlike plain pub."},{"q":"What does `pub(super)` restrict visibility to?","options":["The entire crate","Just the immediate parent module","Only the same module","Every module except the parent"],"answer":1,"explain":"pub(super) exposes an item to the module one level up (the parent), and no further."},{"q":"If `Account` is a `pub` struct, are its fields automatically `pub` too?","options":["Yes, always","No — each field needs its own pub marker","Only the first declared field is pub","Only if the struct derives Debug"],"answer":1,"explain":"A pub struct's fields stay private by default; visibility is decided field by field, independent of the struct's own visibility."},{"q":"What does `pub use` do?","options":["Makes an entire module public","Re-exports an item under a new path, so callers don't need to know your internal module layout","Deletes the original item definition","Only works for functions, not types"],"answer":1,"explain":"pub use re-exports an item (often flattening a deep internal path into a short public one) without moving the original definition."},{"q":"Why can't outside code write `inventory::Item { name: ..., price: ... }` if `Item`'s `name` field is private?","options":["Struct literals never work outside the defining crate","A struct literal must set every field, and outside code can't set a private field","price must also be private for this to matter","Item needs #[derive(Default)] first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Struct literal syntax needs to set every field; a private field can't be set from outside its module, so outside code needs a public constructor function instead."},{"q":"Why is defaulting to the narrowest visibility (rather than always using pub) usually the better choice?","options":["It compiles faster","Every pub item is a permanent promise to callers — widening later is easy, narrowing is a breaking change","Rust limits how many pub items a module can have","pub items use more memory at runtime"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once something is pub, external code may depend on it; narrowing visibility later breaks that code, so it's safer to start narrow and widen only when genuinely needed."},{"q":"Does Rust's privacy system follow file boundaries or module boundaries?","options":["File boundaries — each file is its own privacy scope","Module boundaries — modules can span multiple files or share one file","Both equally","Neither — privacy only applies per-crate"],"answer":1,"explain":"Privacy is entirely about the module tree; how you split modules across files has no effect on visibility rules."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is Rust's default visibility for items?","back":"Private — visible only to the defining module and its descendant modules."},{"front":"What does pub(crate) mean?","back":"Visible anywhere inside the current crate, but invisible to downstream users if this crate is published as a library."},{"front":"What does pub(super) mean?","back":"Visible to just the immediate parent module, one level up — and no further."},{"front":"Does making a struct pub make its fields pub?","back":"No — each field's visibility is set independently of the struct's own visibility."},{"front":"What does pub use do?","back":"Re-exports an item under a new path so callers don't need to know (or depend on) your internal module layout."},{"front":"Why can't you build a struct literal for a type with a private field, from outside its module?","back":"A struct literal must set every field, and private fields can't be set from outside — you need a public constructor instead."},{"front":"What's the risk of marking everything pub \"to be safe\"?","back":"Every pub item becomes a permanent part of your public API — widening later is easy, narrowing is a breaking change."},{"front":"Does Rust privacy follow files or modules?","back":"Modules — file layout has no bearing on visibility rules."}]},"structs":{"topic":"structs","title":"Structs","quiz":[{"q":"What happens if you leave out a field when constructing a struct?","options":["The field defaults to zero","It compiles but the field is empty","A compile error: missing fields","The field is filled with the previous struct's value"],"answer":2,"explain":"You must provide every field in a struct literal (unless you copy the rest with struct update syntax). Missing one is a compile error."},{"q":"How do you make a single field of a struct mutable?","options":["Put `mut` before that field in the definition","You can't — mutability is on the binding: `let mut instance = ...` makes the whole struct mutable","Use `pub mut` on the field","Fields are always mutable"],"answer":1,"explain":"There is no per-field mut. `let mut user = ...` makes the entire struct changeable; without mut, no field can be assigned."},{"q":"How do you read the `name` field of a struct value `user`?","options":["user::name","user->name","user.name","name(user)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fields are accessed with a dot: `user.name`. The `::` is for paths and associated functions, not field access."},{"q":"What is the field init shorthand?","options":["Writing `name` instead of `name: name` when a variable shares the field's name","A way to skip fields entirely","Automatically generating a constructor","Making all fields public"],"answer":0,"explain":"When a variable has the same name as a field, you can write just `name` instead of `name: name`. Rust matches them up."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What must you provide when constructing a struct?","back":"Every field, by name: `User { id: 1, name: ..., active: true }`. Missing a field is a compile error."},{"front":"How do you make a struct's fields changeable?","back":"Make the binding mutable: `let mut user = ...`. There's no per-field mut; it's the whole struct or nothing."},{"front":"What is a tuple struct?","back":"A named type with positional (unnamed) fields: `struct Point(i32, i32);`. Access with .0, .1. Distinct type from a plain tuple."},{"front":"Field init shorthand?","back":"When a variable's name matches the field, write `name` instead of `name: name`."}]},"derive-traits":{"topic":"derive-traits","title":"Derivable traits","quiz":[{"q":"What does `#[derive(Debug)]` generate?","options":["A Display implementation","A Debug implementation that prints the type's name and fields","An equality implementation","A default constructor"],"answer":1,"explain":"It generates a Debug impl usable with {:?}, printing the type's name and every field's value."},{"q":"Why does `Copy` require `Clone`?","options":["It doesn't — they're unrelated traits","Copy is defined as `trait Copy: Clone`, so any Copy type is also a Clone type","Clone requires Copy, not the other way around","It's a historical accident with no real reason"],"answer":1,"explain":"Copy is bounded by Clone (`Copy: Clone`) — a Copy type is always also Clone, since copying is the cheap, implicit version of cloning."},{"q":"Why can't a struct with a `String` field derive `Copy`?","options":["Strings are too large for Copy","String owns heap data and can't be safely duplicated by copying bits; it only implements Clone, not Copy","Copy only works on enums","You need to derive PartialEq first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Copy requires every field to itself be Copy. String owns a heap allocation and only implements Clone, so a struct containing it can't derive Copy."},{"q":"Why can't `f64` derive `Eq` or `Ord`?","options":["Floats are too imprecise for any comparison","NaN != NaN breaks the reflexivity Eq/Ord require, so floats only implement PartialEq/PartialOrd","f64 doesn't support == at all","Eq only works on integer types"],"answer":1,"explain":"Eq promises full reflexivity (a == a is always true), which NaN violates; floats implement PartialEq/PartialOrd but not the stricter Eq/Ord."},{"q":"What determines the sort order for a struct with `#[derive(PartialOrd, Ord)]`?","options":["Alphabetical order of the field names","The order the fields are declared in the struct, most significant first","The field types' sizes in bytes","It's random unless you specify a key"],"answer":1,"explain":"Derived ordering compares fields in declaration order, exactly like comparing a tuple — the first field is the most significant."},{"q":"What happens if you `#[derive(Debug)]` on a struct where one field's type doesn't implement Debug?","options":["That field is skipped in the output","The whole struct fails to compile","It prints \"?\" for that field","It falls back to Display for that field"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deriving requires every field to implement the trait; one field missing Debug fails the entire derive at compile time, not just that field."},{"q":"What does `..Default::default()` do in a struct literal?","options":["Copies every field from a previous instance of the same value","Fills in any fields you didn't explicitly set with their type's Default value","Only works if the struct implements Copy first","Only applies to tuple structs"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's struct update syntax combined with Default — you set the fields you care about, and the rest fall back to Default::default() for their types."},{"q":"When should you hand-write a trait instead of deriving it?","options":["Never — always derive when possible","When the mechanical field-by-field behavior isn't what you actually want, e.g. custom equality that ignores one field","Hand-writing is always required for Debug specifically","Only for enums, never structs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deriving gives you the mechanical field-by-field version; hand-write when semantics should differ, like case-insensitive equality or ordering by a subset of fields."},{"q":"What does `HashMap`/`HashSet` require of its key type?","options":["Only Debug","Both Eq and Hash","Only PartialOrd","Only Clone"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keys need Eq (so equal keys are recognized as the same key) and Hash (so equal keys land in the same bucket)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does #[derive(Debug)] give you?","back":"An implementation of the Debug trait, enabling {:?} printing of the type's name and fields."},{"front":"Clone vs Copy — what's the practical difference?","back":"Clone is an explicit .clone() call (deep copy); Copy makes assignment implicitly duplicate the value instead of moving it."},{"front":"What must be true of every field for a struct to derive Copy?","back":"Every field must itself implement Copy — no String, Vec, Box, or other heap-owning type."},{"front":"Why can't f64 implement Eq?","back":"Because NaN != NaN violates the reflexivity Eq requires; f64 only implements PartialEq."},{"front":"What order does derived PartialOrd/Ord compare struct fields in?","back":"Declaration order, top to bottom — like comparing a tuple, first field most significant."},{"front":"What does Hash + Eq together enable?","back":"Using the type as a key in a HashMap or HashSet."},{"front":"What happens when you derive a trait but one field doesn't implement it?","back":"The whole derive fails to compile — it isn't silently skipped."},{"front":"Give one reason to hand-write PartialEq instead of deriving it.","back":"When equality should ignore some fields (e.g. an internal cache) or use custom logic (e.g. case-insensitive string comparison)."}]},"enums":{"topic":"enums","title":"Enums","quiz":[{"q":"What does an enum represent?","options":["Several values all present together","A value that is exactly one of several named variants","A list that can grow","A number with units"],"answer":1,"explain":"An enum says a value is exactly one of its variants — like a traffic light being red OR yellow OR green, never several at once."},{"q":"Which is true about enum variants in Rust?","options":["Variants can never hold data","All variants must hold the same type of data","Each variant can hold its own, different shape of data (or none)","Variants are just numbers"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust enum variants can each carry different data: nothing, a tuple, or named fields. That's what makes them powerful for modeling."},{"q":"What is `Option` in Rust?","options":["A settings menu","A built-in enum with Some(value) and None, used instead of null","A kind of struct","A loop keyword"],"answer":1,"explain":"Option is an enum: `Some(value)` means there's a value, `None` means there isn't. It replaces null and forces you to handle the missing case."},{"q":"How do you refer to the `North` variant of an enum named `Direction`?","options":["North","Direction.North","Direction::North","North::Direction"],"answer":2,"explain":"You select a variant with `EnumName::Variant`, so `Direction::North`, using the `::` path syntax."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does an enum model?","back":"A value that is exactly ONE of several named variants — 'one of these', not 'all of these together' (that's a struct)."},{"front":"Can enum variants hold data?","back":"Yes, and each variant can hold a different shape: nothing (Quit), a tuple (Write(String)), or named fields (Move { x, y })."},{"front":"What is Option and why does Rust use it?","back":"An enum with Some(value) and None. It replaces null: 'might be missing' is in the type, so you're forced to handle the None case."},{"front":"How do you pick an enum variant?","back":"With `EnumName::Variant`, e.g. `Direction::North` — the `::` path syntax."}]},"pattern-matching":{"topic":"pattern-matching","title":"Pattern matching","quiz":[{"q":"What does it mean that a `match` must be exhaustive?","options":["It must have exactly one arm","It must cover every possible value — handle all cases or add `_`","It must be slow and thorough","It can only match numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust requires a match to handle every possible value. Miss a case and it won't compile — use `_` as a catch-all for the rest."},{"q":"In the arm `Some(n) => ...`, what is `n`?","options":["A comparison against an existing variable n","A new name that captures the value inside Some (destructuring)","The number of the arm","Always zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"`n` is a fresh name that binds the value held inside Some. This is destructuring — the pattern branches and extracts the inner data."},{"q":"When is `if let` a better choice than `match`?","options":["When you need to handle every variant","When you only care about one variant and want to ignore the rest","Never; it's deprecated","Only inside loops"],"answer":1,"explain":"`if let` is the compact form for handling a single pattern. A full match with a throwaway `_ => ()` is clunkier for that case."},{"q":"Where should the catch-all `_` arm go in a match?","options":["First, so it's checked first","Last, because arms are checked top to bottom","It doesn't matter","In the middle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arms are checked top to bottom. A `_` placed early swallows everything below it, making later arms unreachable. Keep it last."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does 'exhaustive match' mean?","back":"A match must cover every possible value. Handle all cases, or add `_` as a catch-all — otherwise it won't compile."},{"front":"What does destructuring do in a match arm?","back":"It mirrors the shape of the data and binds the inner pieces to new names, e.g. `Some(n)` or `Move { x, y }`. Branch and extract in one move."},{"front":"When to use `if let` instead of `match`?","back":"When you only care about one variant and want to ignore the rest — it's the compact single-pattern form."},{"front":"Where do patterns work besides `match`?","back":"In `let` bindings (`let (a, b) = pair`), function arguments, and `if let`. Same 'shapes with names' idea everywhere."}]},"methods-and-impls":{"topic":"methods-and-impls","title":"Methods and impl blocks","quiz":[{"q":"Which receiver should a method use if it only reads the data?","options":["self","&self","&mut self","no receiver"],"answer":1,"explain":"`&self` borrows the value immutably — perfect for reading. Most methods use it."},{"q":"A method needs to change a field. Which receiver does it need?","options":["&self","self","&mut self","static self"],"answer":2,"explain":"`&mut self` borrows mutably, allowing the method to modify fields. Using `&self` would error with 'cannot assign ... behind a & reference'."},{"q":"What is an associated function?","options":["A method that takes &mut self","A function in an impl block with no `self`, often a constructor, called with `Type::func()`","A function outside any impl","A function that returns nothing"],"answer":1,"explain":"An associated function has no self receiver and is called with `::`, e.g. `String::from(...)` or `Rectangle::new(...)`. Constructors are the common case."},{"q":"How do you call a method (with self) versus an associated function (no self)?","options":["Both use a dot","Both use ::","Method uses a dot (value.method()); associated function uses :: (Type::func())","Method uses ::; associated function uses a dot"],"answer":2,"explain":"Methods with a self receiver use a dot: `rect.area()`. Associated functions (no self) use `::`: `Rectangle::new(...)`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"The three method receivers and their meaning?","back":"&self = borrow to read (most common). &mut self = borrow to change a field. self = consume/take ownership (rare)."},{"front":"To call a `&mut self` method, what must the variable be?","back":"`mut`. e.g. `let mut c = ...;` then `c.increment();`. Otherwise the mutable borrow is rejected."},{"front":"What is an associated function?","back":"A function in an impl with no `self`, called with `Type::func()`. Often a constructor like `new`. `String::from` is a familiar example."},{"front":"Dot vs :: for calling?","back":"value.method() (has self) uses a dot. Type::func() (associated function, no self) uses ::."}]},"ownership":{"topic":"ownership","title":"Ownership","quiz":[{"q":"After `let s2 = s1;` where s1 is a String, what happens to s1?","options":["s1 still holds a copy of the string","s1 is moved from and can no longer be used","s1 and s2 share the string and both stay valid","s1 is set to an empty string \"\""],"answer":1,"explain":"For an owning type like String, assignment moves ownership. s1 no longer owns anything, so using it is a compile error."},{"q":"Why can you still use `x` after `let y = x;` when x is an integer?","options":["Integers are special-cased by the compiler","Integers are Copy types, so x is copied, not moved","It's actually a bug and will warn you","Because y is a reference to x"],"answer":1,"explain":"Small stack values like integers implement Copy, so they are duplicated instead of moved. Both x and y remain valid."},{"q":"When is a value's memory freed in Rust?","options":["When a garbage collector decides to run","When you call free() yourself","When its owner goes out of scope","Never, until the program exits"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust drops a value (frees its memory) automatically when the owning variable goes out of scope — no GC, no manual free."},{"q":"You passed a String into a function and now the compiler says it was 'moved'. What's the cheapest correct fix if you still need it afterward?","options":["Call .clone() when passing it, or borrow it with &","Declare the variable twice","Add the `mut` keyword","Wrap it in println!"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passing by value moves the String. Clone makes a separate copy; borrowing with & lends it without moving (and is cheaper)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"State the three ownership rules.","back":"1) Each value has one owner. 2) Only one owner at a time. 3) When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped (freed)."},{"front":"What does 'move' mean in Rust?","back":"Assigning or passing an owning value (String, Vec, ...) transfers ownership to the new place. The original variable can no longer be used."},{"front":"Which types are copied instead of moved?","back":"Copy types: simple fixed-size stack values like integers, bool, and char. `let y = x;` copies them, so x stays usable."},{"front":"How do you use a value without giving up ownership?","back":"Borrow it with a reference (&). That lends access without moving, so the original owner keeps the value. (See the Borrowing lesson.)"}]},"borrowing":{"topic":"borrowing","title":"Borrowing","quiz":[{"q":"What does passing `&s` (instead of `s`) to a function do?","options":["Copies the whole String so the function gets its own","Lends a reference to s without giving up ownership","Moves s into the function permanently","Deletes s after the function runs"],"answer":1,"explain":"`&s` borrows: the function gets a reference and can use the value, but ownership stays with the caller, so s is still usable afterward."},{"q":"You have a read-only borrow `&String` and try to call `.push('!')` on it. What happens?","options":["It works and changes the string","It compiles but does nothing at runtime","A compile error: you can't mutate through a `&` borrow","It makes a copy and mutates that"],"answer":2,"explain":"A plain `&` borrow is read-only. To change the value you need a mutable borrow, `&mut`, and the original variable must be declared `mut`."},{"q":"At one moment, for a single value, which combination of borrows is allowed?","options":["One `&mut` and two `&` at the same time","Two `&mut` at the same time","Either many `&` readers OR one `&mut` writer, never both","Any number of `&mut` writers"],"answer":2,"explain":"The borrowing rule: you may have many read-only borrows, or exactly one mutable borrow, but never a mutable borrow alongside any other borrow."},{"q":"What three things must line up to mutate a value through a function?","options":["The variable is `mut`, you pass `&mut`, the parameter is `&mut`","The variable is `const`, you pass `&`, the parameter is `String`","You call .clone(), pass by value, and return it","Nothing special; any `&` reference can mutate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mutable borrowing needs all three: the original declared `mut`, the value passed as `&mut`, and the parameter typed `&mut`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is borrowing?","back":"Using a value through a reference (&) without taking ownership. The function accesses the value and the original owner keeps it."},{"front":"Difference between `&` and `&mut`?","back":"`&` is a read-only borrow (look, don't change). `&mut` is a mutable borrow (allowed to change the value), and the original must be `mut`."},{"front":"State the core borrowing rule.","back":"At any one time you can have many immutable (&) borrows OR exactly one mutable (&mut) borrow of a value — never both together."},{"front":"Why prefer borrowing over .clone()?","back":"Borrowing moves and copies nothing — it's free. Cloning duplicates the whole value every time. Reach for & before reaching for .clone()."}]},"references-and-dereference":{"topic":"references-and-dereference","title":"References and dereference","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `*` operator do?","options":["Creates a new reference to a value","Follows a reference to reach the value it points at","Multiplies two references together","Frees the memory a reference points at"],"answer":1,"explain":"`*` dereferences: it follows the reference (signpost) back to the actual value. `&` makes a reference; `*` follows one — they are opposites."},{"q":"Given `let r = &x;` where x is an i32, why does `r + 1` fail but `*r + 1` work?","options":["`r` is a reference, not a number; `*r` gets the number so + works","`r + 1` works fine, the question is wrong","You must always write `&` before arithmetic","`*r` copies x into a new variable named r"],"answer":0,"explain":"`r` is a `&i32` (a signpost), and you can't add 1 to a signpost. `*r` follows it to the actual number, then + 1 works."},{"q":"Why can you call `r.len()` without writing `*` when r is a `&String`?","options":["String has a special no-deref rule","The dot operator auto-follows references (deref coercion)","len() secretly takes a reference to a reference","It doesn't work; you must write (*r).len()"],"answer":1,"explain":"The `.` operator automatically follows references as needed to find the method, so method calls rarely need an explicit `*`."},{"q":"How do you add 1 to the value that a `&mut` reference `r` points at?","options":["`r = r + 1;`","`r += 1;`","`*r += 1;`","`&r += 1;`"],"answer":2,"explain":"`*r` follows the mutable reference to the real value, and `+= 1` changes it there. Without `*`, you'd try to change the signpost itself."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a reference, in one image?","back":"A signpost: it points at a value stored elsewhere. It doesn't contain the value itself. You make one with `&`."},{"front":"What does `*` (dereference) do?","back":"Follows a reference back to the value it points at. `&` creates a reference; `*` follows it. They're opposites."},{"front":"When do you need `*` and when not?","back":"Need it for arithmetic and assignment through a reference (`*r + 1`, `*r = 5`). Not for method calls — the dot operator auto-follows references."},{"front":"What is a `&str` compared to `&String`?","back":"A `&str` is a lightweight borrowed view of text. Thanks to deref coercion, a `&String` can be used where a `&str` is expected."}]},"slices":{"topic":"slices","title":"Slices","quiz":[{"q":"What does the slice `&s[0..2]` include?","options":["Indices 0, 1, and 2","Indices 0 and 1 (the end is exclusive)","Just index 2","Indices 1 and 2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ranges are start-inclusive, end-exclusive. `0..2` covers indices 0 and 1 and stops before 2 — the first two elements."},{"q":"What kind of thing is a slice?","options":["A brand-new copy of part of the collection","A borrowed view into part of a collection — it owns nothing","A separate owned String","A number counting the elements"],"answer":1,"explain":"A slice is a reference (a borrow) that views part of an existing collection. It copies nothing and owns nothing, so it can't outlive what it borrows."},{"q":"What is a `&str`?","options":["A string slice — a borrowed view into string text","An owned, growable string on the heap","A single character","A vector of bytes you own"],"answer":0,"explain":"A `&str` is a string slice: a borrowed window into text. Both text literals and slices of a String have this type."},{"q":"You write `let piece = word[0..3];` (no `&`) on a String. Why won't it compile?","options":["The range is invalid","A slice is a borrow, so it needs the `&`","You must use () not []","Strings can't be sliced at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"A slice is a reference, so it needs `&`: `&word[0..3]`. Without it you'd be trying to move a chunk out by value, which isn't allowed."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a slice?","back":"A borrowed view into part of a collection (a string or array). It copies nothing, owns nothing, and can't outlive the value it borrows from."},{"front":"How do you write a slice, and what does the range mean?","back":"`&thing[start..end]`. The range is start-inclusive and end-exclusive, so `[0..2]` is the first two elements (0 and 1)."},{"front":"What is a `&str`?","back":"A string slice — a borrowed window into text. Slicing a String gives a &str, and text literals like \"hi\" are also &str."},{"front":"What's a slice of an array called?","back":"A `&[T]` — a borrowed view over a run of elements of type T. Prefer slice parameters (&[T], &str) for read-only access."}]},"lifetimes":{"topic":"lifetimes","title":"Lifetimes","quiz":[{"q":"What does a lifetime actually track?","options":["How much memory a value uses","How long a reference stays valid (how long its target exists)","How many times a value is cloned","The speed of the program"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lifetime describes how long a reference is valid, so the compiler can forbid references that would outlive the data they point at."},{"q":"Does adding a lifetime annotation like `'a` make data live longer?","options":["Yes, it extends how long the value exists","No, it only describes a relationship the compiler then checks","Yes, it allocates extra memory","Only for String, not for other types"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lifetime annotation describes an existing relationship between references; it never allocates or prolongs anything."},{"q":"When do you usually have to write an explicit lifetime like `'a`?","options":["On every function that uses references","When a function returns a reference and takes more than one reference","Only inside main()","Never — Rust always infers them"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust's elision rules cover the common cases. You mainly need explicit lifetimes when a function returns a reference and takes multiple references, so Rust can't tell which the output borrows from."},{"q":"Why does this fail to compile: a reference `r` set to `&value` inside a block, then used after the block ends?","options":["value is dropped at the block's end, so r would dangle","You can't use & inside a block","r needs to be declared mut","Blocks aren't allowed in main()"],"answer":0,"explain":"The value is dropped when its block ends, so r would point at freed memory. The compiler says 'value does not live long enough' and rejects it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a lifetime?","back":"Rust's tracking of how long a reference stays valid — how long the thing it points at is guaranteed to exist. Used to forbid dangling references."},{"front":"Does `'a` make data live longer?","back":"No. A lifetime annotation only describes a relationship between references so the compiler can check it. It never allocates or prolongs anything."},{"front":"When do you need to write explicit lifetimes?","back":"Mostly when a function returns a reference and takes more than one reference, so Rust can't infer which input the output borrows from."},{"front":"How do you read `'a`?","back":"\"tick-a\" — it's a made-up label for a duration (how long), written in <...>, not a type like i32."}]},"lifetime-elision-and-static":{"topic":"lifetime-elision-and-static","title":"Lifetime elision and 'static","quiz":[{"q":"What does elision rule 1 say?","options":["Every function needs an explicit lifetime","Each elided input reference gets its own distinct lifetime","All references share one lifetime by default","Only the first argument gets a lifetime"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rule 1: every elided (unwritten) lifetime among the input references is treated as its own distinct lifetime parameter."},{"q":"Under elision rule 2, when is the single input lifetime assigned to the output?","options":["Never automatically","When there's exactly one input lifetime","When there are exactly two input lifetimes","Only for methods"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rule 2: if there is exactly one input lifetime (elided or explicit), it's assigned to every elided output lifetime."},{"q":"Under elision rule 3, whose lifetime is assigned to elided outputs?","options":["The first parameter's, always","&self's (or &mut self's) lifetime, when the function is a method","The return type's own lifetime","There is no rule 3"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rule 3: if one of the inputs is &self or &mut self, that lifetime is assigned to all elided output lifetimes — this is why most getter methods need no annotation."},{"q":"Why does `fn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str` need an explicit lifetime?","options":["It doesn't — elision would infer it automatically","There are two input lifetimes and no &self, so neither rule 2 nor rule 3 applies","Only functions named 'longest' need lifetimes","String slices always require explicit lifetimes"],"answer":1,"explain":"With two distinct input references and no &self parameter, none of the three elision rules produce a unique answer, so the compiler requires an explicit annotation."},{"q":"Why does `struct Excerpt { text: &str }` fail to compile?","options":["Structs can't hold references at all","A struct holding a reference needs a lifetime parameter so the compiler knows how long the struct itself can live","str must always be owned, not borrowed","Only tuple structs can hold references"],"answer":1,"explain":"The compiler needs a named lifetime parameter (e.g. Excerpt<'a>) to tie the struct's own validity to the reference it stores."},{"q":"Why does a string literal like \"hello\" have type &'static str?","options":["Because all strings default to 'static","Because it's embedded directly in the compiled binary and is never freed during the program's run","Because the compiler heap-allocates it once at startup","It doesn't — string literals have no lifetime"],"answer":1,"explain":"String literals are baked into the binary at compile time, so they're valid for the entire run of the program — hence 'static."},{"q":"What does a `T: 'static` trait bound actually require?","options":["That every value of T is a compile-time constant","That T contains no references with a lifetime shorter than 'static — i.e. it owns its data or only borrows 'static data","That T must be declared at the top level of the program","That T implements Copy"],"answer":1,"explain":"T: 'static means T doesn't contain any non-'static borrows; an ordinary owned value like a local String satisfies it trivially, even though the variable itself is short-lived."},{"q":"In `fn print_it<T: Debug + 'static>(value: T)`, why does passing an owned `String` compile fine even though the variable is a short-lived local?","options":["It doesn't compile — Strings can never satisfy 'static","The String owns its data outright, so it has no borrows that could expire early, satisfying 'static regardless of the variable's own scope","The String is secretly treated as a string literal","'static only applies to references, never to this kind of bound"],"answer":1,"explain":"'static as a bound is about what a type borrows, not how long the variable lives — an owned String has nothing to expire, so it satisfies T: 'static."},{"q":"Why is reaching for `'static` to fix a lifetime error usually the wrong move?","options":["It's literally impossible to compile","It usually just relocates the bug — now a borrowed value can't be passed in at all where 'static is required","'static bounds were removed in newer Rust editions","It always makes the program slower at runtime"],"answer":1,"explain":"Forcing a 'static bound doesn't fix the underlying ownership issue; it often just makes it impossible to pass in the borrowed value you actually had, pushing you toward cloning or restructuring ownership instead."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"State elision rule 1.","back":"Each elided input reference gets its own distinct lifetime parameter."},{"front":"State elision rule 2.","back":"If there's exactly one input lifetime, it's assigned to every elided output lifetime."},{"front":"State elision rule 3.","back":"If one input is &self/&mut self, its lifetime is assigned to every elided output lifetime."},{"front":"When do you need to write an explicit lifetime by hand?","back":"When none of the three elision rules produce a single answer — typically two or more distinct input references with no &self."},{"front":"Why does a struct holding a reference need a lifetime parameter?","back":"So the compiler can tie the struct's own validity to the lifetime of the data it borrows, preventing the struct from outliving that data."},{"front":"Why are string literals &'static?","back":"They're embedded directly in the compiled binary, so they're valid for the entire program run."},{"front":"What does a `T: 'static` bound actually mean?","back":"T contains no references shorter-lived than 'static — it either owns its data or only borrows 'static data. It does not mean the value itself is global."},{"front":"Does an ordinary local `String` satisfy a `T: 'static` bound?","back":"Yes — it owns its data outright, so it has nothing to expire early, even though the variable itself has a normal, short scope."}]},"traits-basics":{"topic":"traits-basics","title":"Traits","quiz":[{"q":"What does a trait definition actually contain?","options":["Field declarations only","Method signatures (and optionally default bodies) describing behavior a type promises to provide","A list of concrete types allowed to use it","Compiled machine code shared between types"],"answer":1,"explain":"A trait lists method signatures — the shape of the promise. A type keeps that promise by writing an `impl Trait for Type` block."},{"q":"You wrote `trait Greet { fn hello(&self) -> String; }` but never wrote `impl Greet for Dog`. What happens when you call `dog.hello()`?","options":["It compiles and returns an empty string","It compiles using a default implementation","Compile error: no method named 'hello' found for 'Dog'","It panics at runtime"],"answer":2,"explain":"A trait alone does nothing for a type. Without an impl block, Dog never kept the promise, so the compiler can't find the method."},{"q":"In a trait definition, what's the difference between `fn hello(&self) -> String;` and `fn hello(&self) -> String { ... }`?","options":["No difference, both are required methods","The first is a required method (implementers must write it); the second is a default method (implementers get it for free unless they override it)","The first is faster at runtime","The second can only be used with generics"],"answer":1,"explain":"A `;` means no body — every implementer must supply one. A body in the trait itself is a default that implementers inherit automatically."},{"q":"What is the effect of a default method in a trait?","options":["It forces every implementer to override it","It provides behavior automatically to any type that implements the trait, unless that type chooses to override it","It only works for the first type that implements the trait","It removes the need for an impl block entirely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Types that implement the trait get the default method's behavior for free — they only need to write it themselves if they want different behavior."},{"q":"`fn f<T>(x: T) { x.summarize(); }` fails to compile with 'no method named summarize found for type parameter T'. Why?","options":["summarize() doesn't exist anywhere in the program","T has no trait bound, so the compiler has no information that T has a summarize method","Generic functions can never call methods","x needs to be borrowed with &"],"answer":1,"explain":"An unbounded generic type parameter could be literally anything. Without `T: Summary`, the compiler can't assume summarize() exists."},{"q":"When must you use `fn f<T: Trait>(x: T, y: T)` instead of `fn f(x: &impl Trait, y: &impl Trait)`?","options":["Never, they are always interchangeable","When you need to guarantee x and y are the same concrete type","When the trait has default methods","When the function returns a value"],"answer":1,"explain":"`impl Trait` sugar creates an independent anonymous type per parameter, so it can't express 'same T twice.' The generic form with a named T can."},{"q":"What is the purpose of a `where` clause on a generic function?","options":["It changes the runtime behavior of the function","It's purely a readability tool — it expresses the exact same bounds as `<T: ...>`, just moved below the signature","It allows bounds that `<T: Trait>` cannot express","It's required whenever a function has more than one type parameter"],"answer":1,"explain":"`where` clauses are syntactic sugar for the same bounds you could put inline in `<...>`. They exist to keep signatures with multiple/combined bounds readable."},{"q":"Why is it misleading to think of a trait as a 'base class'?","options":["Traits can only be implemented once per program","Traits describe capabilities ('can do'), not an inheritance hierarchy ('is a'), and carry no data of their own","Traits cannot have default methods","Traits are only used for printing"],"answer":1,"explain":"A type can implement many unrelated traits with none of them being a 'parent.' Traits are behavior contracts, not a place to store fields."},{"q":"A trait method has `&self` as its first parameter. What does that mean?","options":["The method takes ownership of the value it's called on","The method borrows the value it's called on, without taking ownership","The method is static and needs no instance","The method can only be called once"],"answer":1,"explain":"`&self` borrows the receiver, the same way any other `&` reference borrows a value — the caller keeps ownership."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a trait?","back":"A named list of method signatures a type promises to provide — a behavior contract, not inheritance."},{"front":"How does a type keep a trait's promise?","back":"By writing `impl TraitName for TypeName { ... }` with real bodies for each required method."},{"front":"Required method vs default method in a trait definition — how do you tell them apart?","back":"Required: signature ends in `;`, no body — implementers must write it. Default: signature has a `{ ... }` body — implementers get it free, can override it."},{"front":"Two equivalent ways to write a trait-bounded function parameter.","back":"`fn f(x: &impl Trait)` (sugar) and `fn f<T: Trait>(x: &T)` (explicit generic) — same meaning for a single parameter."},{"front":"When do you need `<T: Trait>` instead of `impl Trait` sugar?","back":"When the same type parameter must appear more than once and be guaranteed identical each time, e.g. `fn cmp<T: PartialEq>(a: T, b: T)`."},{"front":"What does a `where` clause do that inline `<T: Bound>` doesn't?","back":"Nothing functionally — it's the same bounds, just written below the signature for readability once there are multiple bounds or type parameters."},{"front":"What error do you get calling a trait method on an unbounded generic `T`?","back":"\"no method named '...' found for type parameter 'T'\" — the fix is adding the trait bound, e.g. `<T: Summary>`."},{"front":"\"Can do\" vs \"is a\" — which describes traits?","back":"\"Can do.\" Traits are capabilities a type has, not a parent-child inheritance relationship, and they carry no fields."}]},"trait-objects-and-dyn":{"topic":"trait-objects-and-dyn","title":"Trait objects and dyn","quiz":[{"q":"What happens when you call a generic function `print_area::<Circle>` and `print_area::<Square>` in the same program?","options":["The compiler generates one shared function that checks the type at runtime","The compiler generates two separate monomorphized copies, one per concrete type","It fails to compile — a generic function can only be called with one type per program","Both calls run through a vtable lookup"],"answer":1,"explain":"Monomorphization means the compiler stamps out a dedicated copy of the generic function for each concrete type it's called with. Calls are direct, with no runtime dispatch."},{"q":"Why can't `Vec<T>` hold both a `Circle` and a `Square` even if both implement the same `Shape` trait?","options":["Vec can only ever hold one element","Circle and Square are different concrete types, and every element of Vec<T> must be the same T","Shape must be object-safe first","Vec doesn't support custom structs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vec<T> is generic over one fixed T. Circle and Square are different types, so neither alone satisfies 'every element is T' unless you switch to a trait object like Box<dyn Shape>."},{"q":"Why must `dyn Shape` sit behind a pointer like `Box<dyn Shape>` or `&dyn Shape`?","options":["Because dyn Trait is deprecated syntax","Because dyn Shape is unsized — its size depends on the concrete type behind it, which isn't known at compile time","Because traits cannot be stored in variables at all","Because Box is required for all heap allocation in Rust"],"answer":1,"explain":"Different Shape implementers have different sizes. A pointer is always a fixed size regardless of what it points to, which is what lets dyn Shape be used at all."},{"q":"What extra information does a `Box<dyn Trait>` fat pointer carry compared to a regular pointer?","options":["A copy of the trait definition source code","The address of a vtable — a table of function pointers, one per trait method, for the concrete type","The size of the Vec it lives in","A reference count"],"answer":1,"explain":"A trait object pointer is 'fat': it carries the data address plus a vtable address. Calling a method looks up the right function pointer in that vtable."},{"q":"What makes a trait NOT object-safe (unable to become `dyn Trait`)?","options":["Having more than one method","Having a method with a generic type parameter, or a method returning Self by value","Having a default method","Being implemented for more than one type"],"answer":1,"explain":"A vtable needs one fixed function pointer per method. A generic method would need unboundedly many versions, and a Self-returning method has an unknown size at the call site — both break the fixed-shape requirement."},{"q":"What is the runtime cost of calling a method through `dyn Trait` compared to a generic (static dispatch) call?","options":["Identical — no cost at all","One extra indirection: a vtable lookup to find the right function pointer, then a call through it","It requires garbage collection","It always allocates memory on every call"],"answer":1,"explain":"Static dispatch calls the concrete function directly. Dynamic dispatch adds one vtable lookup before the jump — a small, bounded cost."},{"q":"What does `fn make_adder(n: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32` mean?","options":["The function returns a boxed trait object chosen at runtime","The function returns some fixed, unnamed concrete type implementing Fn(i32) -> i32, resolved entirely at compile time","The function can return a different closure type on each call, chosen by the caller","The function must be generic over its return type"],"answer":1,"explain":"impl Trait in return position names one specific compile-time type without spelling it out. Unlike dyn Trait, every return path must produce the exact same underlying type."},{"q":"A function has two branches, each returning a different concrete closure type, both implementing the same Fn trait. Why does `-> impl Fn(i32) -> i32` fail to compile for this function?","options":["impl Trait return position requires all return paths to resolve to the identical concrete type; use Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32> instead","Closures can never be returned from functions","Fn traits are not object-safe","impl Trait can only be used with structs, not closures"],"answer":0,"explain":"impl Trait in return position is resolved to one hidden concrete type at compile time. If the concrete type genuinely varies by branch, you need dynamic dispatch: Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>."},{"q":"You have `Vec<dyn Shape>` and get 'the size for values of type dyn Shape cannot be known at compilation time'. What's the fix?","options":["Add #[derive(Sized)] to Shape","Change it to Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> so each element is a fixed-size pointer","Remove the Vec entirely, dyn Trait cannot be collected","Add a generic parameter <T: Shape> to main"],"answer":1,"explain":"dyn Shape alone is unsized. Wrapping each element in Box (or &, or Rc) gives Vec a fixed-size element type to store, even though what each Box points to varies in size."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is monomorphization?","back":"The compiler generating a separate, specialized copy of a generic function for each concrete type it's called with — the basis of static dispatch."},{"front":"Static dispatch vs dynamic dispatch — the core trade-off.","back":"Static dispatch (generics): direct calls, no runtime cost, but one fixed type per call site. Dynamic dispatch (dyn Trait): a vtable lookup per call, but lets you mix concrete types behind one interface."},{"front":"Why is `dyn Trait` described as 'unsized'?","back":"Different types implementing the trait have different sizes, so the compiler can't know dyn Trait's size at compile time. It must live behind a pointer (Box, &, Rc)."},{"front":"What is a vtable?","back":"A table of function pointers, one per trait method, generated once per concrete type — what a dyn Trait fat pointer uses to dispatch a method call at runtime."},{"front":"What is a 'fat pointer' in the context of dyn Trait?","back":"A pointer that carries two addresses: the data itself, and the vtable for the concrete type behind it — twice the size of a normal pointer."},{"front":"What breaks a trait's object safety?","back":"A method with a generic type parameter, or a method that returns Self by value — both need information a fixed vtable can't provide."},{"front":"What does `impl Trait` in return position mean, and how does it differ from dyn Trait?","back":"It means 'some fixed concrete type implementing Trait, resolved at compile time' — no vtable, no pointer needed, but every return path must be the same underlying type (unlike dyn Trait, which can vary at runtime)."},{"front":"When should you reach for Box<dyn Trait> instead of generics?","back":"When you need one collection or variable that can hold genuinely different concrete types behind the same trait interface, e.g. Vec<Box<dyn Shape>>."}]},"generics":{"topic":"generics","title":"Generics","quiz":[{"q":"In `fn first<T>(items: &[T]) -> &T`, what is `T`?","options":["A specific type named T defined elsewhere","A placeholder for a type, chosen when the function is called","A variable holding a runtime value","The name of a trait"],"answer":1,"explain":"T is a generic type parameter — a placeholder. The compiler fills it with a concrete type each time you call the function."},{"q":"Why does a generic function often need a trait bound like `<T: PartialOrd>`?","options":["To make the code run faster at runtime","Because T could be any type, so you may only use abilities you promise it has","Bounds are optional decoration with no effect","To turn the generic into a trait object"],"answer":1,"explain":"Inside a generic, T might be anything, so the compiler only allows operations you guarantee via bounds. `PartialOrd` promises T can be compared with `>`."},{"q":"What does the `+` mean in `<T: PartialOrd + Copy>`?","options":["T is either PartialOrd or Copy","Add PartialOrd and Copy together numerically","T must implement both PartialOrd and Copy","It's a syntax error"],"answer":2,"explain":"The `+` combines bounds: T must satisfy PartialOrd AND Copy."},{"q":"Why are Rust generics called 'zero-cost'?","options":["They are free to type on the keyboard","The compiler generates specialized code per concrete type, so there's no runtime penalty","They use a garbage collector to manage memory","They only work with numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"For each concrete type used, the compiler stamps out a specialized version (monomorphization), so generic code runs as fast as hand-written specific code."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a generic type parameter like T?","back":"A placeholder for a type, chosen when the code is used. Declared in angle brackets: fn name<T>(...) or struct Name<T>."},{"front":"Why do you add trait bounds to a generic?","back":"Inside a generic, T could be any type, so you may only use abilities you promise it has. Bounds like <T: PartialOrd> grant those abilities."},{"front":"What does <T: PartialOrd + Copy> mean?","back":"T must implement both PartialOrd (comparable) and Copy (cheaply duplicated). The + means 'and also'."},{"front":"Do generics slow down your program?","back":"No — they are zero-cost. The compiler generates a specialized version per concrete type, so runtime speed matches hand-written code."}]},"vectors":{"topic":"vectors","title":"Vectors","quiz":[{"q":"What's the key difference between an array like `[i32; 3]` and a `Vec<i32>`?","options":["Arrays can hold any type, Vec cannot","Vec's length can change at runtime; an array's length is fixed at compile time","Vec is stored on the stack, arrays are always on the heap","There is no real difference"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vec<T> is a growable, heap-allocated list. An array's size is part of its type and can never change."},{"q":"What's the difference between `Vec::new()` and `vec![1, 2, 3]`?","options":["Vec::new() is faster in every case","Vec::new() starts empty; vec![...] is a macro that builds a Vec already filled with the listed values","vec![...] can only hold strings","They are two names for the exact same function"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vec::new() gives you an empty vector to push into later; the vec! macro is shorthand for building one pre-populated with known values."},{"q":"What does `v.pop()` return?","options":["The popped value directly, panicking if the vector is empty","Option<T> — Some(value) if there was an element, None if the vector was empty","The new length of the vector","A reference to the popped value that stays valid after popping"],"answer":1,"explain":"pop() can't return a bare value because the vector might be empty, so it returns an Option — the same 'don't crash, tell the caller' pattern used elsewhere in Rust."},{"q":"What happens when you evaluate `v[99]` on a vector with only 5 elements?","options":["It returns None","It returns a default value like 0","It panics at runtime with 'index out of bounds'","It silently returns garbage memory"],"answer":2,"explain":"Indexing with [] panics on an out-of-range index rather than reading invalid memory. Use .get(i) if the index might be invalid."},{"q":"What does `v.get(99)` return if index 99 doesn't exist?","options":["It panics, same as v[99]","None — no crash","A reference to the last element instead","It returns 0"],"answer":1,"explain":".get(i) returns Option<&T>: Some(&value) if the index is valid, None otherwise — the crash-free alternative to indexing with []."},{"q":"What's the difference between `for x in &v` and `for x in v`?","options":["No difference at all","for x in &v borrows the vector (still usable afterward); for x in v consumes it (v can't be used after the loop)","for x in v is always faster","for x in &v only works with numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"&v borrows, yielding &T and leaving the vector intact. Iterating v directly takes ownership and yields owned T, consuming the vector."},{"q":"Why doesn't `nums.dedup()` remove all duplicates from `vec![1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 1, 3]`?","options":["dedup() is broken and should never be used","dedup() only removes duplicates that are adjacent to each other, and the two 1-runs aren't adjacent here","dedup() only works on sorted numeric vectors","dedup() requires the elements to implement Copy"],"answer":1,"explain":"dedup() collapses consecutive equal runs only. To remove all duplicates regardless of position, sort() first so equal elements become neighbors, then dedup()."},{"q":"What's the benefit of `Vec::with_capacity(n)` over `Vec::new()` when you know you'll push exactly n elements?","options":["It makes the elements immutable","It reserves space for n elements up front, so those pushes don't trigger any reallocation and copying","It automatically sorts elements as they're pushed","It changes the vector's element type"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without with_capacity, a growing Vec occasionally has to allocate a bigger block and copy every element over. Reserving the space up front avoids that entirely for known sizes."},{"q":"What does `nums.retain(|&n| n % 2 == 0)` do?","options":["Removes elements where the closure returns false, keeping only elements where it returns true, in place","Sorts the vector by evenness","Returns a new vector, leaving nums unchanged","Panics if any element is odd"],"answer":0,"explain":"retain walks the vector and keeps only elements for which the closure returns true, mutating the vector in place."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Vec::new() vs vec![...] — when to use each?","back":"Vec::new() for an empty vector you'll fill later with push(); vec![...] when you already know the starting values."},{"front":"v[i] vs v.get(i) — what's the difference on an invalid index?","back":"v[i] panics (crashes) on an out-of-range index. v.get(i) returns None instead — no crash, use it when the index isn't already known to be valid."},{"front":"What type does Vec::pop() return, and why?","back":"Option<T> — Some(value) or None — because the vector might be empty and there'd be nothing to pop."},{"front":"for x in &v vs for x in v.","back":"&v borrows (v stays usable after the loop, x is &T). Plain v consumes ownership (v is gone after, x is owned T)."},{"front":"sort_by vs sort_by_key.","back":"sort_by takes a closure comparing two full elements and returning Ordering. sort_by_key takes a closure extracting one field to compare by — clearer when sorting by a single field."},{"front":"What does dedup() actually remove?","back":"Only consecutive (adjacent) equal elements. Sort first if you want every duplicate removed regardless of position."},{"front":"Why use Vec::with_capacity(n)?","back":"It reserves room for n elements up front, avoiding the reallocate-and-copy cost that happens when a Vec grows past its current capacity one push at a time."},{"front":"What does v.retain(f) do?","back":"Keeps only the elements for which the closure f returns true, removing the rest, in place."}]},"hashmaps-and-hashsets":{"topic":"hashmaps-and-hashsets","title":"HashMaps and HashSets","quiz":[{"q":"What's the core difference between how Vec and HashMap find things?","options":["Vec finds by key, HashMap finds by position","Vec finds by position (index), HashMap finds by a key you choose","There is no difference, they are interchangeable","HashMap can only store numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vec is ordered and positional (index 0, 1, 2...). HashMap looks values up by an arbitrary key you choose, like a name or id."},{"q":"What happens when you `.insert()` a key into a HashMap that's already present?","options":["It's a compile error","It panics at runtime","The old value is silently overwritten by the new one","Both values are kept in a list under that key"],"answer":2,"explain":"insert() on an existing key replaces its value. A map holds each key exactly once. Check the Option returned by insert() if you need to know whether it overwrote something."},{"q":"What does `counts.entry(word).or_insert(0)` do?","options":["Always inserts 0, overwriting any existing count","Returns a mutable reference to the value for word, inserting 0 first only if the key was missing","Deletes the entry for word if it exists","Returns a copy of the value, not a reference"],"answer":1,"explain":"entry().or_insert() is the one-lookup insert-or-update pattern: it gives you &mut V pointing at the (possibly newly-inserted) slot, so you can modify it directly, e.g. with *count += 1."},{"q":"Why does `HashMap<Point, bool>` fail to compile if Point is a plain struct with no derives?","options":["HashMap values must be primitives","Point needs Eq and Hash implementations so the map can hash it into a bucket and confirm key equality","Structs can never be used as generic parameters","bool cannot be a HashMap value type"],"answer":1,"explain":"A HashMap key must be hashable (Hash) and comparable for equality (Eq) so lookups work. #[derive(PartialEq, Eq, Hash)] adds both automatically."},{"q":"Why does deriving Hash on a struct also require deriving Eq (not just PartialEq)?","options":["It doesn't — PartialEq alone is enough","Eq promises the equality check is total and reflexive, which the map relies on when confirming 'same hash bucket, so same key'","Eq makes hashing faster","Hash and Eq are unrelated and this is a coincidence"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hash + Eq together let the map trust that two values hashing to the same bucket really are the same key when compared. PartialEq alone doesn't give that total-equality guarantee."},{"q":"What does HashSet::insert(value) return, and what's it useful for?","options":["Nothing (unit type)","true if the value was newly added, false if it was already present — useful for 'have I seen this before' checks","The number of elements now in the set","A reference to the inserted value"],"answer":1,"explain":"The boolean return of insert() is what lets you write 'if seen.insert(word) { println!(\"new word\") }' as a one-line first-sighting check."},{"q":"Is HashMap iteration order guaranteed to match insertion order?","options":["Yes, always","No — order is unspecified and can even change between runs of the same program","Only for HashMap<String, _>","Only if you call .sort() first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keys are scattered by hash for fast lookup, with no relation to insertion order, and Rust randomizes the hash seed per process as a DoS defense."},{"q":"Which collection should you reach for when you need sorted iteration over key/value pairs?","options":["HashMap, since it's the default","BTreeMap, which keeps keys in sorted order by design","Vec of tuples, always","HashSet"],"answer":1,"explain":"HashMap makes no order guarantee. BTreeMap is built specifically to keep keys sorted at all times."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Vec vs HashMap — how do they find elements?","back":"Vec finds by position (index). HashMap finds by an arbitrary key you choose."},{"front":"What does map.insert(k, v) do if k already exists?","back":"Overwrites the existing value silently. insert() returns Some(old_value) if it overwrote, None if the key was new."},{"front":"The entry API insert-or-update pattern.","back":"map.entry(key).or_insert(default) returns &mut V to the slot, inserting default first only if missing — one lookup instead of get-then-insert."},{"front":"What traits does a custom struct need to be a HashMap key?","back":"#[derive(PartialEq, Eq, Hash)] — Eq for key equality checks, Hash for placing it in the right bucket."},{"front":"What is a HashSet conceptually?","back":"A HashMap<T, ()> in spirit — tracks which keys/values exist with no attached value, for membership and uniqueness checks."},{"front":"What does HashSet::insert() return, and why is that useful?","back":"true if newly added, false if already present — lets you detect 'first time seeing this value' in one call."},{"front":"Is HashMap iteration order predictable?","back":"No — unspecified, and can differ between runs of the same program due to per-process hash seed randomization."},{"front":"When should you use HashSet::contains vs Vec::contains?","back":"HashSet::contains is O(1) on average; Vec::contains scans linearly, O(n). Prefer HashSet for frequent membership checks."}]},"other-collections":{"topic":"other-collections","title":"BTreeMap, VecDeque, and BinaryHeap","quiz":[{"q":"What's the main advantage BTreeMap has over HashMap?","options":["Faster average-case insert and lookup","Keys are always kept in sorted order, and it supports efficient range queries","It uses less memory in every case","It doesn't require the Ord trait"],"answer":1,"explain":"HashMap is faster on average (O(1) vs BTreeMap's O(log n)) but gives no order guarantee. BTreeMap trades some speed for always-sorted keys and range() queries."},{"q":"What trait must a BTreeMap's key type implement, and why?","options":["Hash, same as HashMap","Ord, so the map can compare keys to decide sort position","Copy, so keys can be duplicated","Default, so missing keys get a placeholder"],"answer":1,"explain":"BTreeMap must always know whether one key is less than, equal to, or greater than another to keep itself sorted — that's exactly what Ord provides."},{"q":"For a custom struct like `struct Version { major: u32, minor: u32 }`, what does `#[derive(PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]` compare first?","options":["The fields in alphabetical order by name","The fields in declaration order — major first, then minor as a tiebreaker","Only the first field, ignoring the rest","It picks a random field each time"],"answer":1,"explain":"Derived Ord compares struct fields in the order they're declared, using each subsequent field as a tiebreaker — exactly the behavior you want for a version number."},{"q":"Why is VecDeque faster than Vec for adding elements to the front?","options":["VecDeque doesn't actually store elements contiguously in memory at all","VecDeque is a ring buffer, so the logical front can move without shifting every other element; Vec::insert(0, x) has to shift all elements","VecDeque elements are always smaller","Vec cannot have a front at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"VecDeque treats its buffer as a ring, so push_front is O(1). Vec's elements are always contiguous starting at index 0, so inserting at the front means shifting everything over — O(n)."},{"q":"When should you prefer plain Vec over VecDeque?","options":["Never, VecDeque is strictly better","When you only ever push and pop from the back — Vec has slightly less overhead for that access pattern","When you need sorted keys","When you need O(1) lookup by key"],"answer":1,"explain":"VecDeque's flexibility (fast at both ends) isn't free, and Vec remains the better default when the front is never touched."},{"q":"By default, what does BinaryHeap::pop() return?","options":["The smallest remaining element","The largest remaining element","A random remaining element","The first element pushed, like a queue"],"answer":1,"explain":"BinaryHeap is a max-heap by default — pop() always hands back the largest element currently in the heap."},{"q":"How do you get min-heap behavior (smallest first) out of BinaryHeap?","options":["Call heap.reverse() before popping","Wrap pushed values in std::cmp::Reverse, then unwrap after popping","BinaryHeap cannot do this; use BTreeSet instead","Pass a comparator argument to BinaryHeap::new()"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reverse(x) flips what 'greater' means for x, so the heap's largest-by-Reverse-ordering value is really the smallest wrapped value. Push Reverse(x), pop and destructure Reverse(smallest)."},{"q":"Does iterating a BinaryHeap with `for x in &heap` visit elements in largest-to-smallest order?","options":["Yes, always","No — only repeated .pop() calls guarantee that order; plain iteration visits elements in unspecified internal order","Only if the heap has fewer than 10 elements","Only for a min-heap built with Reverse"],"answer":1,"explain":"The heap's internal layout is optimized for pop(), not for a sorted walk. Iterating directly does not guarantee any particular order."},{"q":"Which collection would you choose for a sliding window that needs to cheaply drop old items from one end while adding new ones to the other?","options":["BinaryHeap, because it always has the max ready","VecDeque, because push/pop are O(1) at both front and back","BTreeMap, because keys are sorted","HashSet, because membership checks are fast"],"answer":1,"explain":"A sliding window adds at one end and removes at the other repeatedly — exactly VecDeque's O(1)-both-ends strength."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"BTreeMap vs HashMap — the core trade-off.","back":"BTreeMap: keys always sorted, O(log n), supports range queries. HashMap: faster average O(1), but no order guarantee."},{"front":"What trait do BTreeMap/BTreeSet keys need?","back":"Ord (which requires Eq and PartialOrd) — the map needs to compare keys to keep them sorted."},{"front":"How does #[derive(Ord)] order a struct's fields?","back":"By declaration order: the first field is compared first, subsequent fields break ties in the order they're declared."},{"front":"What makes VecDeque different from Vec?","back":"VecDeque is a ring buffer with O(1) push/pop at BOTH ends. Vec is O(1) at the back only — the front requires shifting every element, O(n)."},{"front":"When should you still prefer Vec over VecDeque?","back":"When you only ever push/pop the back — Vec has less overhead for that single-ended access pattern."},{"front":"What does BinaryHeap::pop() return by default?","back":"The largest remaining element — BinaryHeap is a max-heap by default."},{"front":"How do you build a min-heap using BinaryHeap?","back":"Wrap each value in std::cmp::Reverse before pushing; std::cmp::Reverse flips the Ord comparison so the 'largest' wrapped value is the smallest real value."},{"front":"Does BinaryHeap guarantee sorted order when you iterate it directly (not via pop)?","back":"No — only repeated .pop() calls guarantee largest-first order. Direct iteration visits elements in unspecified order."}]},"strings-and-str":{"topic":"strings-and-str","title":"Strings and `str`","quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between `String` and `&str`?","options":["String owns growable text; &str is a borrowed view of existing text","They are identical and interchangeable","&str owns text; String only borrows it","String is for numbers; &str is for letters"],"answer":0,"explain":"String owns a growable heap buffer. &str is a borrowed view into text that lives somewhere else."},{"q":"What type is a string literal like `\"hello\"`?","options":["String","&str","char","Vec<char>"],"answer":1,"explain":"String literals are &str — borrowed views pointing into your compiled program."},{"q":"For a function that only reads text, which parameter type is most flexible?","options":["String, because it owns the data","&str, because both String (via &) and literals can be passed","char, because it's smaller","It doesn't matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"Taking &str lets callers pass a borrowed String or a literal without giving up ownership. Take &str to read, return String to build new text."},{"q":"What does `.len()` return for the string `\"café\"`?","options":["4, the number of characters","5, the number of bytes (é takes 2 bytes in UTF-8)","1, because it's one word","It causes a compile error"],"answer":1,"explain":".len() counts bytes, not characters. 'é' is 2 bytes in UTF-8, so \"café\" is 5 bytes. Use .chars().count() for the character count."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"String vs &str?","back":"String owns growable, heap-allocated text. &str is a borrowed view of text that already exists. String literals are &str."},{"front":"Which text type should a read-only function parameter use?","back":"&str. It accepts both a String (borrowed with &) and a literal, without taking ownership."},{"front":"Why can't you write `some_string[0]` to get a character?","back":"Rust text is UTF-8; byte positions and character positions can differ, so indexing by number is disallowed. Use .chars().nth(i)."},{"front":"Does .len() give the character count?","back":"No — it gives the byte count. Use .chars().count() for the number of characters."}]},"iterator-basics":{"topic":"iterator-basics","title":"Iterator basics","quiz":[{"q":"What does calling `.next()` return once an iterator is exhausted?","options":["None","an empty Vec","a panic","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"`next()` returns `Option<Item>` — `Some(item)` while there's more, and `None` once the iterator has nothing left to give."},{"q":"What does `.iter()` yield when called on a `Vec<T>`?","options":["T (owned)","&T (borrowed)","&mut T (mutable borrow)","Option<T>"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.iter()` hands out read-only references, `&T`, so the original collection is untouched and still usable afterward."},{"q":"What happens to the original collection after you call `.into_iter()` on it and loop over the result?","options":["It's left untouched","It's borrowed immutably","It's consumed — the original can no longer be used","It's cleared but stays usable"],"answer":2,"explain":"`.into_iter()` yields owned items, which means it takes ownership of the collection itself. Once consumed, the original variable is gone."},{"q":"Which method lets you modify every element of a `Vec` in place?","options":[".iter()",".into_iter()",".iter_mut()",".collect()"],"answer":2,"explain":"`.iter_mut()` yields `&mut T` — mutable references you can write through (with `*item = ...`) to change elements without consuming the collection."},{"q":"`for x in &v` desugars to roughly which call?","options":["v.into_iter(), consuming v","v.iter(), borrowing each element","v.iter_mut()","v.clone().into_iter()"],"answer":1,"explain":"Looping over `&v` borrows rather than owns — under the hood it's equivalent to calling `.iter()`, so `v` is still usable after the loop."},{"q":"What happens if you write `nums.iter().map(|n| n * 2);` with no consumer at the end?","options":["It runs immediately and discards the result","Nothing happens — the compiler warns the iterator is unused","It's a hard compile error","It multiplies nums in place"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adaptors are lazy. Without a consumer like `.collect()`, `.sum()`, or a `for` loop, the chain never runs — Rust warns \"unused iterator that must be used.\""},{"q":"Why must an iterator variable be declared `mut` to call `.next()` on it directly?","options":["It doesn't need to be — that's a myth","next() takes &mut self, so it needs mutable access to advance internal state","Rust requires all loop variables to be mutable","next() reallocates memory on every call"],"answer":1,"explain":"`fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>` needs a mutable reference to the iterator so it can update wherever it tracks its position."},{"q":"You need to print every name in a Vec<String> and then still use the Vec afterward. Which loop form is correct?","options":["for name in names","for name in &names","for name in names.into_iter()","Both A and C work equally well"],"answer":1,"explain":"`for name in names` (and its explicit form `names.into_iter()`) consumes the vector. `for name in &names` only borrows, so `names` is still valid afterward."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do .iter(), .iter_mut(), and .into_iter() each yield from a Vec<T>?","back":".iter() -> &T (borrow), .iter_mut() -> &mut T (mutable borrow), .into_iter() -> T (owned, consumes the vector)."},{"front":"What does the Iterator trait's core method look like?","back":"fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> — returns Some(item) while there's more, None when exhausted."},{"front":"What does `for x in v` desugar to?","back":"Roughly: `let mut it = v.into_iter(); while let Some(x) = it.next() { ... }` — it takes ownership of v."},{"front":"What does `for x in &v` desugar to?","back":"Roughly: `let mut it = v.iter(); while let Some(x) = it.next() { ... }` — it borrows v, which stays usable afterward."},{"front":"Is an iterator chain like `v.iter().map(...)` eager or lazy?","back":"Lazy — it builds a plan but does no work until something consumes it (a for loop, .collect(), .next(), etc.)."},{"front":"Why must an iterator variable be `mut` to call .next() on it directly?","back":"next(&mut self) needs a mutable reference to advance the iterator's internal position."},{"front":"You need to double every element of a Vec<i32> in place. Which method?","back":"iter_mut() — it yields &mut T so you can mutate through the reference, e.g. `for n in v.iter_mut() { *n *= 2; }`."},{"front":"After `for x in names` where names: Vec<String>, can you still use `names`?","back":"No — for x in names calls into_iter(), which consumes names. Use `for x in &names` if you need it afterward."}]},"iterator-adaptors":{"topic":"iterator-adaptors","title":"Iterator adaptors","quiz":[{"q":"What does `.map()` do when added to a lazy iterator chain?","options":["Immediately runs and returns a Vec","Describes a transformation of each item; still does nothing until consumed","Filters out items that fail a test","Only works on numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Like all adaptors, `.map()` just adds a step to the plan. Nothing runs until a consumer like `.collect()` or a `for` loop pulls values through."},{"q":"What's the difference between `filter` and `filter_map`?","options":["filter keeps items passing a test; filter_map both filters and transforms in one step, dropping Nones","They are identical","filter_map only works with numbers","filter transforms, filter_map filters"],"answer":0,"explain":"filter_map's closure returns Option<T>: Some(value) keeps the transformed value, None drops the item — one adaptor instead of map + filter + unwrap."},{"q":"What happens when you `.zip()` two iterators of different lengths?","options":["It panics","The result has the length of the longer iterator, padding the shorter with None","The result stops as soon as the shorter iterator is exhausted","Rust refuses to compile"],"answer":2,"explain":"zip pairs items positionally and stops the moment either side runs out, so the output length equals the shorter of the two iterators."},{"q":"Which method pairs each item with its index, starting at 0?","options":["zip","enumerate","fold","take"],"answer":1,"explain":"enumerate() turns an iterator of Item into an iterator of (usize, Item) pairs, with the index starting at 0."},{"q":"What does `.collect::<Vec<i32>>()` do that a bare `.collect()` sometimes can't?","options":["Nothing different","Tells the compiler what type to build, avoiding a 'type annotations needed' error","Makes the chain run eagerly instead of lazily","Reverses the collection"],"answer":1,"explain":"collect() can build many different collections, so the compiler needs to know which one. Turbofish (or a type annotation on the binding) supplies that."},{"q":"What does `nums.iter().fold(0, |acc, n| acc + n)` compute for `[1, 2, 3]`?","options":["0","6","[1, 2, 3]","a panic"],"answer":1,"explain":"fold carries an accumulator through every item, starting at 0: 0+1=1, 1+2=3, 3+3=6. The final accumulator, 6, is returned."},{"q":"Why might a plain for loop be preferred over a 6-step adaptor chain?","options":["for loops are always faster","Long chains can become harder to read and debug than a commented for loop","Adaptors don't work with more than 3 steps","for loops don't need a mut binding"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adaptors are great for straightforward transform/filter/collect work, but a very long chain — or one with side effects — often reads worse than a for loop you can step through line by line."},{"q":"What does `.take(3)` do to an iterator?","options":["Removes the first 3 items and returns the rest","Yields only the first 3 items, ignoring the rest","Skips the first 3 items","Takes ownership of only the first 3 items"],"answer":1,"explain":"take(n) limits the stream to at most the first n items; everything after is never yielded."},{"q":"What's the difference between `.sum()` and `.count()`?","options":["sum() adds up numeric items; count() tells you how many items came through, regardless of value","They do the same thing","count() only works on Strings","sum() returns an Option, count() returns a Result"],"answer":0,"explain":"sum() totals the numeric values in the iterator. count() ignores the values entirely and just tallies how many items were produced."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do adaptors like map, filter, and enumerate have in common?","back":"They're all lazy — each describes a step in the pipeline but runs no code until a consumer pulls values through."},{"front":"What's the difference between filter and filter_map?","back":"filter keeps items where a closure returns true. filter_map runs a closure returning Option<T> per item — Some is kept and unwrapped, None is dropped. One step instead of filter + map + unwrap."},{"front":"How long does .zip() run when the two iterators have different lengths?","back":"It stops as soon as either iterator runs out — the result length is the shorter of the two."},{"front":"How do you tell .collect() what type to build?","back":"Either a type annotation on the binding (`let v: Vec<i32> = ...`) or turbofish syntax (`.collect::<Vec<i32>>()`)."},{"front":"What does fold(init, |acc, item| ...) do?","back":"Builds up a single accumulated value by applying the closure to each item in turn, starting from init. sum() is really a specialized fold."},{"front":"When does a for loop read better than an adaptor chain?","back":"When the chain gets long (roughly 4-5+ adaptors), has side effects like I/O, or needs intermediate debugging — a commented for loop is often clearer."},{"front":"What does enumerate() add to each item?","back":"Its index, yielding (usize, Item) pairs starting at 0."},{"front":"What's the difference between sum() and count()?","back":"sum() adds up the numeric values of items into a total. count() just tallies how many items passed through, regardless of their value."}]},"closures":{"topic":"closures","title":"Closures","quiz":[{"q":"How do you write a closure that takes one parameter `x` and returns `x * 2`?","options":["(x) => x * 2","|x| x * 2","fn(x) x * 2","{x} x * 2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Closure parameters go between pipes: |x| x * 2. A single-expression body needs no braces or return."},{"q":"What can a closure do that a normal top-level `fn` cannot?","options":["Return a value","Take parameters","Capture variables from the surrounding scope","Be called more than once"],"answer":2,"explain":"A closure can capture and use variables from the code around it. A plain fn only sees its own parameters."},{"q":"What does the `move` keyword do in `move || println!(\"{}\", name)`?","options":["Runs the closure immediately","Makes the closure take ownership of the variables it captures","Moves the closure to another thread automatically","Prevents the closure from capturing anything"],"answer":1,"explain":"move forces the closure to own its captured values instead of borrowing them — needed when the closure outlives the current scope, like in a thread."},{"q":"By default, how does a closure capture a variable it uses?","options":["By taking ownership (move)","By borrowing it","By copying the whole program","It doesn't capture unless you use `capture`"],"answer":1,"explain":"By default a closure borrows what it captures, so the original variable stays usable. Add move to take ownership."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a closure and how is it written?","back":"An unnamed inline function: |params| body. A one-expression body needs no braces or return, e.g. |x| x + 1."},{"front":"What is a closure's superpower over a plain fn?","back":"It can capture variables from the surrounding scope and use them without passing them in as arguments."},{"front":"How does a closure capture by default, and how do you change it?","back":"It borrows captured variables by default. Add the `move` keyword to make it take ownership (needed when it outlives the scope, e.g. threads)."},{"front":"How does a function accept a closure as a parameter?","back":"Via the Fn / FnMut / FnOnce trait bounds, e.g. fn apply<F: Fn(i32) -> i32>(f: F) { ... }."}]},"result-and-option":{"topic":"result-and-option","title":"Option and Result","quiz":[{"q":"What are the two shapes of Option<T>?","options":["Ok(v) and Err(e)","Some(v) and None","Value(v) and Empty","True and False"],"answer":1,"explain":"Option<T> is Some(value) when there's a value, or None when there isn't. It's Rust's safe replacement for null."},{"q":"What are the two shapes of Result<T, E>?","options":["Some(v) and None","Ok(v) and Err(e)","Pass and Fail","Value(v) and Error(e)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Result<T, E> is Ok(value) on success or Err(error) on failure, carrying a real error value describing what went wrong."},{"q":"Why does `match` on an Option or Result help catch bugs?","options":["It runs faster than if/else","It's exhaustive — the compiler refuses to build unless every case (including None/Err) is handled","It automatically logs errors to the console","It converts Option into Result for you"],"answer":1,"explain":"match requires every variant to be handled. Forget the None or Err arm and the compiler rejects the code as non-exhaustive, catching a whole class of 'forgot to check' bugs."},{"q":"What happens when you call `.unwrap()` on a `None` or an `Err`?","options":["It returns a default value","It silently does nothing","The program panics and crashes","It returns None again"],"answer":2,"explain":"unwrap() panics (crashes the program) on None/Err. It's convenient in tests and quick scripts, risky in real code a user runs."},{"q":"What must the closure passed to `.and_then()` return?","options":["A plain value of any type","Another Option (or Result) — the same kind of container","Always a String","Nothing — and_then discards the return value"],"answer":1,"explain":"and_then chains another fallible step, so its closure must itself return an Option/Result. If it returns a bare value, you wanted .map() instead."},{"q":"You have `let price: Option<i32> = Some(10);` and want `Option<i32>` back with the value doubled. Which combinator?","options":[".and_then(|p| p * 2)",".map(|p| p * 2)",".unwrap_or(p * 2)",".ok_or(p * 2)"],"answer":1,"explain":".map() transforms the value inside Some/Ok with a plain (infallible) closure and re-wraps the result automatically."},{"q":"What does `.ok_or(err)` do?","options":["Converts a Result into an Option, discarding the error","Converts an Option into a Result, using `err` as the Err value when it's None","Panics if the Option is None","Filters an Option based on a predicate"],"answer":1,"explain":".ok_or(err) turns Option<T> into Result<T, E>: Some(v) becomes Ok(v), and None becomes Err(err)."},{"q":"What's the difference between `.unwrap_or(default)` and `.unwrap_or_else(|| ...)`?","options":["There is no difference, they're aliases","unwrap_or takes a ready-made value; unwrap_or_else takes a closure that only runs (computing the fallback) on the failure path","unwrap_or panics, unwrap_or_else never panics","unwrap_or only works on Result, unwrap_or_else only works on Option"],"answer":1,"explain":"unwrap_or's argument is always evaluated eagerly; unwrap_or_else's closure is only called when there's actually a None/Err, useful when computing the fallback is expensive."},{"q":"`text.parse::<i32>().ok().filter(|&n| n > 0)` — what does `.filter()` do here?","options":["Removes non-digit characters from text before parsing","Converts the Result into an Option","Keeps Some(n) only if the predicate is true; otherwise turns it into None","Runs the closure on every character of the string"],"answer":2,"explain":".filter() on Option keeps Some(value) only when the predicate returns true for the inner value; otherwise it becomes None."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is Option<T> used for, versus Result<T, E>?","back":"Option<T> models 'a value might simply be absent' (no reason needed). Result<T, E> models 'an operation might fail, and here's why' — it carries an error value."},{"front":"Why is unwrap() risky in real code?","back":"It panics (crashes the whole program) on the first None/Err it sees. Fine for tests and quick scripts; risky in anything a user runs, since there's no chance to recover or show a nice message."},{"front":"What does .map() do on an Option/Result?","back":"Transforms the value inside Some/Ok using a plain (non-fallible) closure, leaving None/Err untouched. The result is automatically re-wrapped in Some/Ok."},{"front":"What does .and_then() do, and how is it different from .map()?","back":"and_then chains another fallible step: its closure must itself return an Option/Result (not a bare value). Unlike map, it doesn't re-wrap — this avoids ending up with Option<Option<T>>."},{"front":"What does .ok_or(err) do, and what does .ok() do?","back":".ok_or(err) converts Option -> Result, using err as the Err when it's None. .ok() goes the other way, converting Result -> Option and discarding the error value."},{"front":"unwrap_or(default) vs unwrap_or_else(|| ...) — when would you pick each?","back":"unwrap_or(default) when the fallback is cheap and ready-made. unwrap_or_else(|| ...) when computing the fallback is expensive or needs the error value — its closure only runs on the failure path."},{"front":"What does .filter() do on an Option?","back":"Keeps Some(value) only if a predicate returns true for the inner value; otherwise turns it into None. Useful for adding a validity check after parsing or looking something up."},{"front":"Why does `match` on Option/Result prevent a class of bugs that null checks don't?","back":"match is exhaustive — the compiler refuses to compile unless every variant (Some/None or Ok/Err) is handled, so you literally cannot forget to check the failure case."}]},"the-question-mark-operator":{"topic":"the-question-mark-operator","title":"The ? operator","quiz":[{"q":"What does `expr?` do when `expr` evaluates to an `Err(e)`?","options":["It panics immediately","It returns `Err(e)` (converted if needed) from the enclosing function right away","It silently ignores the error and continues","It logs the error and continues with a default value"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` on an Err returns early from the enclosing function with that error (running it through From::from first). On Ok, it unwraps the value and execution continues."},{"q":"Why does `?` only work inside a function that returns `Result` or `Option`?","options":["It's an arbitrary syntax restriction with no deeper reason","Because `?`'s early return has to return a Result/Option from the enclosing function, and there's nowhere for that to go otherwise","Because Result and Option are the only types with a Debug impl","It actually works in any function, this is a common misconception"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` desugars to an early `return Err(...)` (or `return None`). If the function's return type isn't Result/Option, that return has nowhere valid to go, so the compiler rejects it."},{"q":"What is `expr?` roughly equivalent to, written out as a match?","options":["`match expr { Ok(v) => v, Err(e) => return Err(e.into()) }`","`match expr { Ok(v) => return v, Err(e) => v }`","`expr.unwrap()`","`match expr { Ok(v) => Some(v), Err(e) => None }`"],"answer":0,"explain":"`?` unpacks Ok(v) into v and continues, or returns Err(e) (converted via From) from the function immediately on Err — exactly what that match spells out."},{"q":"What extra step does `?` perform on the error value that a plain `return Err(e)` would not?","options":["It logs the error to stderr automatically","It calls `From::from` on the error, converting it into the function's declared error type if needed","It clones the error","It wraps the error in an Option"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` calls From::from on the error so a function can return one error type while `?`-ing through several different underlying error types, as long as a From conversion exists."},{"q":"A function returns `Result<i32, ParseIntError>`, but you use `?` on something that fails with `std::io::Error`, with no `From<io::Error> for ParseIntError` impl. What happens?","options":["It compiles fine; the io::Error is silently discarded","It compiles and converts using Debug formatting","It's a compile error — there's no way to convert the error into the declared error type","It panics at runtime instead of failing to compile"],"answer":2,"explain":"`?` needs a From conversion path to the function's declared error type. Without one, the compiler rejects the code with a type mismatch on the `?`."},{"q":"What does `fn main() -> Result<(), E>` let you do?","options":["Nothing new — main can never return a value","Use `?` directly inside main, since main itself now returns a Result","Skip writing Ok(()) at the end of main","Automatically catch all panics in the program"],"answer":1,"explain":"Since `?` needs a Result/Option-returning enclosing function, giving main a Result<(), E> return type lets you use `?` at the top level too."},{"q":"In `fn main() -> Result<(), std::num::ParseIntError> { let n: i32 = \"123\".parse()?; Ok(()) }`, what does `Ok(())` mean?","options":["Success, with no meaningful value — () is Rust's empty type","An error containing an empty string","The function is still running","A placeholder that must later be replaced with a real value"],"answer":0,"explain":"`()` (unit) is Rust's 'nothing of interest' type. Ok(()) means the function succeeded but has no value to hand back besides that success."},{"q":"Does `?` work on `Option<T>`, not just `Result<T, E>`?","options":["No, `?` is exclusive to Result","Yes — inside a function returning Option, `?` unwraps Some or returns None immediately","Only in unstable/nightly Rust","Only if the Option contains a Result"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` works the same way on Option: it returns the inner value on Some, or returns None immediately from the enclosing (Option-returning) function."},{"q":"Besides changing the error path, what must you still write explicitly on a function's success path when using `?`?","options":["Nothing, `?` handles both paths","An explicit Ok(value) (or Some(value)) at the end — `?` only rewrites the error/early-return side","A call to .unwrap()","A separate match statement"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` only handles the early-return-on-failure side. The function's normal return still needs to explicitly wrap the successful value in Ok(...) or Some(...)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does `expr?` do on Ok(v) vs Err(e)?","back":"On Ok(v): unwraps to v and execution continues. On Err(e): immediately returns Err(e) (converted via From if needed) from the enclosing function."},{"front":"Why must the enclosing function return Result or Option to use `?`?","back":"`?`'s early-return path returns an Err/None from the enclosing function. If that function's return type isn't Result/Option, there's nowhere valid for the early return to go, so it's a compile error."},{"front":"What does `?` do to the error value that a plain early return wouldn't?","back":"It calls From::from on the error, converting it into whatever error type the function declares — so `?` can auto-convert between different underlying error types."},{"front":"What happens if `?` needs to convert error type A into error type B but no From<A> for B exists?","back":"Compile error — the `?` has no way to perform the conversion. Fix by widening the return type (e.g. to Box<dyn Error>) or writing the From impl yourself."},{"front":"What does `fn main() -> Result<(), E>` enable?","back":"It lets you use `?` directly inside main, since `?` requires the enclosing function to return Result/Option, and now main does."},{"front":"Does `?` still require an explicit Ok(...)/Some(...) on the success path?","back":"Yes. `?` only handles the failure/early-return side. The function's normal (non-early) return still needs to wrap the value: `Ok(value)` or `Some(value)`."},{"front":"Does `?` work on Option<T> as well as Result<T, E>?","back":"Yes — inside a function returning Option<T>, `?` unwraps Some(v) to v, or returns None immediately on None."}]},"custom-error-types":{"topic":"custom-error-types","title":"Custom error types","quiz":[{"q":"Why write a custom error enum instead of just returning `Box<dyn Error>` everywhere?","options":["Box<dyn Error> doesn't compile in stable Rust","A custom enum lets callers match on exactly which failure mode happened; Box<dyn Error> erases that distinction","Custom enums are faster at runtime","Box<dyn Error> can't be printed with Display"],"answer":1,"explain":"Box<dyn Error> type-erases the concrete error, so a caller can only print it. A dedicated enum with one variant per failure mode lets callers `match` and react differently to each case."},{"q":"What's the idiomatic shape of a custom error type for a function with multiple failure modes?","options":["A single struct with a String message field","An enum with one variant per distinct way the operation can fail","A tuple of booleans, one per possible error","Reusing std::io::Error for every kind of failure"],"answer":1,"explain":"One enum variant per failure mode keeps the caller's match meaningful — they can distinguish 'missing' from 'invalid' from any other case."},{"q":"What is `impl std::fmt::Display` for on an error type?","options":["It provides the {:?} Debug representation","It provides the {} representation — a human-readable message","It's required for the type to derive Debug","It converts the error into a Result"],"answer":1,"explain":"Display gives the {} format — the human-facing message a user or log line would show, as opposed to Debug's {:?} which is for programmers."},{"q":"What two traits does `std::error::Error` require as supertraits?","options":["Clone and PartialEq","Debug and Display","Send and Sync","Copy and Default"],"answer":1,"explain":"std::error::Error requires Debug + Display, which is why both need to be implemented (or derived, for Debug) before you can implement Error."},{"q":"What does implementing `impl std::error::Error for MyError {}` (empty body) actually give you?","options":["Nothing, the body must not be empty","It marks MyError as interoperable with the broader error-handling ecosystem — Box<dyn Error>, ? conversions, logging tools","It automatically derives Display for you","It disables the compiler's exhaustiveness checks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Error has sensible defaults (source() returns None by default), so an empty impl block is valid and sufficient to make the type interoperate with code expecting `E: std::error::Error` or Box<dyn Error>."},{"q":"Why implement `From<ParseIntError> for MyError`?","options":["It's required to derive Debug","So `?` can automatically convert a ParseIntError into MyError at any call site inside a function returning Result<_, MyError>","It lets you print the error with println!","It's needed to store the error in a Vec"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` calls From::from on the error it sees. Implementing From<ParseIntError> for MyError once means every `?` on a fallible parse call converts automatically, no .map_err needed."},{"q":"What does the `source()` method on the `Error` trait provide?","options":["The file and line number where the error occurred","A way to walk the underlying cause chain when your error wraps another error","The original input string that caused the error","A timestamp of when the error happened"],"answer":1,"explain":"source() (default None) lets you expose a wrapped error, so callers/logging tools can print a full 'caused by' chain instead of just the top-level message."},{"q":"A function returns `Result<u16, ConfigError>` and calls `text.parse()?` inside it, but there's no `From<ParseIntError> for ConfigError`. What happens?","options":["It compiles, silently discarding the ParseIntError","Compile error — `?` has no way to convert ParseIntError into ConfigError","It compiles and panics at runtime instead","Rust automatically generates the From impl"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without the From impl, `?` cannot convert the error type, so the compiler rejects the code with a type mismatch — the same rule from the ? operator lesson."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What's the idiomatic shape of a custom error type with multiple failure modes?","back":"An enum with one variant per distinct failure mode — this keeps a caller's match meaningful, letting them distinguish and react to each case separately."},{"front":"What does Display give an error type, versus Debug?","back":"Display provides the {} representation — a human-readable message. Debug provides {:?} — a programmer-facing representation, usually just #[derive(Debug)]."},{"front":"What two traits must an error type already implement before it can implement std::error::Error?","back":"Debug and Display — they're required supertraits of std::error::Error."},{"front":"Why implement std::error::Error even with an empty body?","back":"It makes your type interoperate with the broader error ecosystem: Box<dyn Error>, code generic over E: std::error::Error, and ?-conversions that rely on the Error trait."},{"front":"Why implement From<SourceError> for MyError instead of using .map_err() everywhere?","back":"Implement From once per source error type, and every `?` on that error type inside a function returning Result<_, MyError> converts automatically — no repeated .map_err(...) at each call site."},{"front":"What does the source() method on Error do, and what's its default?","back":"It exposes a wrapped/underlying error so callers can walk the cause chain. Its default implementation returns None; override it when your error variant wraps another error."},{"front":"Why is one giant catch-all error variant (e.g. Other(String)) a mistake?","back":"It defeats the purpose of a custom error type — callers can no longer match on which specific thing failed, they're back to just reading a message string."}]},"error-crates-thiserror-and-anyhow":{"topic":"error-crates-thiserror-and-anyhow","title":"thiserror and anyhow","quiz":[{"q":"What problem does `thiserror` solve?","options":["It replaces Result with a faster type","It derives the Display, std::error::Error, and From boilerplate for a custom error enum from attributes","It makes panics recoverable","It removes the need for error types entirely"],"answer":1,"explain":"#[derive(thiserror::Error)] plus #[error(\"...\")] and #[from] attributes generate the same Display/Error/From impls you'd otherwise write by hand."},{"q":"In `#[derive(thiserror::Error, Debug)] enum ConfigError { #[error(\"missing key: {0}\")] Missing(String), ... }`, what does `#[error(\"missing key: {0}\")]` generate?","options":["A From impl for that variant","The matching arm of the Display implementation for that variant","A Debug implementation for that variant","A test case for that variant"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each #[error(\"...\")] attribute becomes the Display arm for that variant, with {0} referring to the variant's first field, similar to format!."},{"q":"What does `#[from]` on a variant's field do in a thiserror enum?","options":["Nothing, it's just documentation","Generates a From<SourceType> for MyError impl, so `?` still auto-converts","Forces the field to be public","Converts the field to a String automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"#[from] generates the From impl for that field's type, exactly matching what you'd write by hand — that's why ? keeps working without extra code."},{"q":"What is `anyhow::Result<T>` shorthand for?","options":["Option<T>","Result<T, anyhow::Error>","Result<T, String>","Box<T>"],"answer":1,"explain":"anyhow::Result<T> is defined as Result<T, anyhow::Error> — a catch-all error type that any std::error::Error-implementing type converts into via ?."},{"q":"Why can't you `match` on the specific error variant behind an `anyhow::Error`?","options":["You actually can, anyhow preserves the concrete type","anyhow::Error intentionally type-erases the concrete error — it's built to be propagated and logged, not matched on","anyhow::Error only stores a String, no structured data at all","match doesn't work on any Result type"],"answer":1,"explain":"anyhow::Error erases the concrete type by design, which is exactly why it's meant for application code that just propagates/logs errors rather than library code whose callers need to distinguish cases."},{"q":"What must be in scope to call `.context(\"...\")` on a Result?","options":["Nothing extra, it's always available on Result","`use anyhow::Context;` — context is a trait method, not an inherent Result method","The thiserror crate","A #[derive(Context)] on the error type"],"answer":1,"explain":"Context is a trait anyhow provides; without importing it, `.context(...)` doesn't exist on Result and the compiler reports 'no method named context found'."},{"q":"What's the difference between `.context(\"msg\")` and `.with_context(|| \"msg\")`?","options":["They're identical, with_context is just deprecated","with_context's closure only runs (building the message) on the failure path, useful when the message is expensive to construct","context works on Option, with_context only works on Result","with_context discards the original error, context keeps it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same relationship as unwrap_or vs unwrap_or_else: .context(...) always evaluates its argument, .with_context(|| ...) only runs the closure when there's actually an error to attach it to."},{"q":"What does `anyhow::ensure!(port <= 65535, \"port {port} is out of range\")` do?","options":["Always returns an error","If the condition is false, returns early with a formatted error — like a fallible assert!","Panics if the condition is false","Logs a warning but continues either way"],"answer":1,"explain":"ensure!(cond, \"msg\") is equivalent to `if !cond { bail!(\"msg\") }` — it bails out with the given error only when the condition fails."},{"q":"What's the recommended rule of thumb for choosing between thiserror and anyhow?","options":["Always use anyhow, everywhere, for everything","thiserror for libraries (callers need to match specific variants), anyhow for binaries/applications (callers just log or exit)","thiserror for applications, anyhow for libraries","Use both crates in every function for maximum flexibility"],"answer":1,"explain":"Libraries should expose precise, match-able errors (thiserror); applications typically just want to propagate and report failures with context (anyhow)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does #[derive(thiserror::Error)] generate for an enum?","back":"It generates the std::error::Error implementation, and combined with #[error(\"...\")] on each variant, the matching Display implementation too."},{"front":"What does #[from] on a thiserror variant's field do?","back":"Generates a From<SourceType> for YourErrorType impl for that field's type, so ? still auto-converts without you writing the impl by hand."},{"front":"What is anyhow::Result<T>?","back":"Shorthand for Result<T, anyhow::Error>. Any type implementing std::error::Error (or a plain string) converts into anyhow::Error via ?, making it a fast catch-all for application code."},{"front":"Why can't you match on the concrete error behind anyhow::Error?","back":"anyhow::Error intentionally type-erases the original error — it's designed for propagating and logging, not for distinguishing cases. Use thiserror instead if callers need to match."},{"front":"What do you need in scope to use .context(\"...\") on a Result?","back":"use anyhow::Context; — .context() is a trait method from anyhow::Context, not an inherent method on Result, so it won't compile without the import."},{"front":"context(\"msg\") vs with_context(|| \"msg\") — when to use which?","back":"context(\"msg\") always builds the message eagerly. with_context(|| \"msg\") only runs the closure on the failure path — use it when the message is expensive to construct or needs computed data."},{"front":"What do anyhow::bail! and anyhow::ensure! do?","back":"bail!(\"msg\") returns early with a formatted error immediately. ensure!(cond, \"msg\") is a fallible assert!: bails with that error only if cond is false."},{"front":"Rule of thumb: thiserror vs anyhow?","back":"thiserror for libraries — callers need to match on specific error variants. anyhow for binaries/application code — callers just want to propagate, log, or exit on any failure."}]},"builder-pattern":{"topic":"builder-pattern","title":"The builder pattern","quiz":[{"q":"Why does Rust lean on the builder pattern instead of what many other languages use for optional constructor arguments?","options":["Rust has no constructor overloading or named/optional function arguments","Rust structs cannot have more than 3 fields","Builders are required by the borrow checker","Rust doesn't allow default values anywhere"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without overloaded constructors or named/optional arguments, a struct with many optional fields needs another way to be built readably — that's what the builder pattern provides."},{"q":"What must a builder setter method return to support method chaining like `.name(\"x\").port(8080)`?","options":["()","&str","Self","bool"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each setter takes self (consuming the builder) and must return Self so the next .method() call has something of the right type to call on."},{"q":"A setter is written as `fn port(mut self, port: u16) { self.port = port; }` (no return type). What happens?","options":["It fails to compile at the setter itself with a clear error","It compiles, but the chain breaks at the NEXT method call because port() returns ()","It silently ignores the port value","It automatically infers -> Self"],"answer":1,"explain":"A function with no declared return type implicitly returns (). The setter itself compiles fine; the error surfaces one call later, when you try to call a method on the () it returned."},{"q":"Why do required fields in a builder often get stored as `Option<T>` rather than `T`?","options":["Option<T> is required by the derive(Default) macro","So .build() can check whether the field was actually set and return an Err if not","Option<T> makes the field mutable","It has no real purpose, it's just convention"],"answer":1,"explain":"Storing a required field as Option<T> lets .build() distinguish 'never set' (None) from 'set', and use .ok_or(...)? to turn a missing value into a proper Err instead of guessing a default."},{"q":"What is `.build()` typically responsible for that a plain struct literal isn't?","options":["Serializing the struct to JSON","Validating that required fields were provided, often returning a Result","Deleting the builder from memory immediately","Converting all fields to strings"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.build()` is the natural place to check invariants (required fields present, values in valid ranges) and report failure via Result, since it's the single point where construction actually finishes."},{"q":"For a struct with only 2-3 non-optional fields, what does the lesson recommend over a full builder?","options":["A global mutable variable","Default plus struct-update syntax (..Default::default())","Always still use a builder, no exceptions","Multiple overloaded new() functions"],"answer":1,"explain":"A builder is overhead for small structs. `Point { x: 10, ..Default::default() }` is simpler and does the same job when there's little real optionality."},{"q":"Why does a setter take `self` by value instead of `&self`?","options":["&self would be a syntax error in impl blocks","Taking self by value lets the method consume and return an updated builder, enabling the chain","Rust requires all methods to take self by value","It has no effect on chaining either way"],"answer":1,"explain":"Consuming self (mut self) means the method owns the builder for its body and can move a modified version back out via the return value — exactly what chaining `.method().method()` needs."},{"q":"What's a real-world example of the builder pattern mentioned in the lesson?","options":["std::process::Command","std::vec::Vec::new()","println!","std::mem::swap"],"answer":0,"explain":"std::process::Command is a classic standard-library builder: .arg(), .env(), .current_dir() all chain and return Self, ending in .spawn() or .output()."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why doesn't Rust use constructor overloading for optional struct fields?","back":"Rust has no constructor overloading and no named/optional function arguments, so a struct with several optional fields needs another idiomatic way to be constructed readably — the builder pattern."},{"front":"What must every setter method in a builder chain return?","back":"Self. Each setter takes self (or &mut self) and returns Self (or &mut Self) so the next .method() call in the chain has something valid to call on."},{"front":"Where does validation of required fields typically happen in the builder pattern?","back":"In .build(), which checks that required Option<T> fields were set (often with .ok_or(...)?) and returns a Result so missing data becomes a proper Err instead of a silent bug."},{"front":"When is a builder overkill?","back":"For a struct with only 2-3 fields and little real optionality — use Default plus struct-update syntax (..Default::default()) instead."},{"front":"What error symptom shows up when a setter forgets to return Self?","back":"The setter itself compiles fine (it implicitly returns ()), but the NEXT method call in the chain fails with something like 'no method named `X` found for type `()`'."},{"front":"Give two real Rust ecosystem examples of the builder pattern.","back":"reqwest::Client::builder() and std::process::Command — both use chainable setters returning Self, ending in a final consuming call (.build() or .spawn()/.output())."},{"front":"What does #[derive(Default)] give a builder struct?","back":"A default() constructor that sets every field to its type's zero value (0, String::new(), false, None, ...), giving you a clean starting point without writing new() by hand."}]},"newtype-pattern":{"topic":"newtype-pattern","title":"The newtype pattern","quiz":[{"q":"What does `struct Meters(f64);` create?","options":["An alias for f64 that's interchangeable with it","A brand new type, distinct from f64, at zero runtime cost","A struct with a field named 'f64'","A trait that f64 must implement"],"answer":1,"explain":"A single-field tuple struct wraps f64 but is a genuinely different type to the compiler, while being identical to a bare f64 in memory at runtime — a zero-cost abstraction."},{"q":"Why does wrapping UserId(u64) and OrderId(u64) separately prevent a real bug?","options":["It makes both types faster","It stops the compiler from allowing a UserId to be passed where an OrderId is expected, even though both are u64 underneath","It automatically validates the ID values are positive","It converts the IDs to strings for safety"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without the wrapper, both parameters are just u64 and can be swapped by accident with no compiler warning. With distinct newtypes, swapping them is a compile error."},{"q":"What is the orphan rule?","options":["A rule that all structs must have at least one field","A rule blocking impl ForeignTrait for ForeignType when you own neither the trait nor the type","A rule that prevents recursive struct definitions","A rule about unused variables"],"answer":1,"explain":"The orphan rule exists so two different crates can't both implement the same foreign trait for the same foreign type in conflicting ways. You must own the trait or the type (or both)."},{"q":"How does the newtype pattern work around the orphan rule?","options":["It doesn't — the orphan rule cannot be avoided","By wrapping the foreign type in a new type you define, so you now own the type in the impl slot","By renaming the foreign trait","By using unsafe code to bypass type checking"],"answer":1,"explain":"impl ForeignTrait for YourWrapper(ForeignType) is allowed because YourWrapper is defined in your own crate — you own the type even though the trait is still foreign."},{"q":"How do you access the inner value of `let d = Meters(42.0);`?","options":["d.inner()","d.value","d.0","*d"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tuple structs index their fields positionally starting at 0, so the single wrapped value is accessed with .0."},{"q":"Does `struct Meters(f64)` automatically inherit f64's methods like sqrt()?","options":["Yes, all inner type methods are inherited automatically","No — you need .0 to reach the inner value, or a Deref impl to forward method calls","Only arithmetic operators are inherited, not other methods","Only if the struct derives Copy"],"answer":1,"explain":"A newtype is a brand new type with no methods of its own by default. You either unwrap with .0 or implement Deref (or forwarding methods) to make it feel like the inner type."},{"q":"Why does `println!(\"{}\", Meters(42.0))` fail to compile?","options":["println! only works with integers","Meters doesn't implement Display — wrapping a printable f64 doesn't make the wrapper itself printable","You must always use {:?} for structs","Tuple structs cannot be printed at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Meters is a new type with no trait implementations by default. You need m.0 to print the inner f64, or implement Display/derive Debug on Meters yourself."},{"q":"What's a downside of overusing the newtype pattern?","options":["It makes the program slower at runtime","Wrapping every primitive adds .0 noise everywhere and slows readers down","It's not allowed by the Rust compiler for more than 5 types","It disables pattern matching entirely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Newtypes are best reserved for cases where mixing values up would be a real bug (IDs, units). Wrapping everything by default adds unwrapping overhead to reading and writing code."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the newtype pattern look like syntactically?","back":"A single-field tuple struct, e.g. struct Meters(f64); — wraps an existing type to create a new, distinct type at zero runtime cost."},{"front":"What real bug does wrapping UserId(u64) and OrderId(u64) separately prevent?","back":"Accidentally passing one where the other is expected. Both are u64 underneath, but as distinct newtypes the compiler catches the swap at the call site."},{"front":"State the orphan rule in one sentence.","back":"You cannot impl ForeignTrait for ForeignType unless you own the trait or the type (or both) — this prevents conflicting trait impls across crates."},{"front":"How does wrapping a foreign type sidestep the orphan rule?","back":"Define your own newtype around the foreign type (e.g. struct Names(Vec<String>);). You now own the type in the impl slot, so impl ForeignTrait for Names is allowed."},{"front":"How do you get the inner value out of a newtype?","back":"With .0 (tuple structs index fields positionally), or by implementing Deref/From for more ergonomic access."},{"front":"Does a newtype inherit the wrapped type's methods automatically?","back":"No. struct Meters(f64) does not gain f64's methods for free — you need .0 to reach the inner value, or explicit Deref/forwarding impls."},{"front":"When should you reach for a newtype?","back":"When mixing up two values of the same underlying type would be a real bug (IDs, units, currencies) — not reflexively for every primitive, since that adds .0 noise everywhere."}]},"operator-overloading":{"topic":"operator-overloading","title":"Operator overloading","quiz":[{"q":"What does `a + b` actually desugar to in Rust?","options":["A special compiler intrinsic with no trait involved","Add::add(a, b)","a.plus(b)","std::ops::plus(a, b)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every operator in Rust is shorthand for a trait method call. `+` specifically maps to the Add trait's add method."},{"q":"Which trait must you implement to make `p1 + p2` work for a custom struct Point?","options":["std::ops::Add","std::ops::Index","std::cmp::PartialEq","std::fmt::Display"],"answer":0,"explain":"Add is the trait behind the + operator. It has an associated Output type and an add(self, rhs) -> Output method."},{"q":"What does implementing Index<(usize, usize)> for a Grid type enable?","options":["grid.get(row, col)","grid[(row, col)] subscript syntax","Automatic sorting of grid cells","grid == grid comparisons"],"answer":1,"explain":"Index (and IndexMut for read-write) is the trait behind [] syntax — implementing it for your own type lets container[key] work."},{"q":"A type implements Add but a program tries to use `p += other`. What happens?","options":["It works automatically since += is just repeated +","It fails to compile — AddAssign is a separate trait from Add and must be implemented separately","It compiles but silently does nothing","It only works if the type is Copy"],"answer":1,"explain":"+= is backed by the AddAssign trait, distinct from Add. Implementing Add does not give you += for free — you must implement AddAssign too."},{"q":"Why does `impl Add for Point` typically require Point to be Copy (or otherwise handle ownership explicitly)?","options":["Because Add requires Copy by definition and won't compile otherwise","Because Add::add takes self by value, which moves (or copies, for Copy types) the left-hand operand","Because Rust requires all operator traits to only work on Copy types","It has nothing to do with ownership at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Most std::ops traits consume self by value. For a Copy type, p1 + p2 copies p1 instead of moving it, so p1 remains usable afterward. For non-Copy types you'd need to implement Add for references or accept the move."},{"q":"When is operator overloading a GOOD idea according to the lesson's judgment call?","options":["Whenever it saves a few characters of typing","When the operator's meaning is exactly what a reader would expect from ordinary math or collection syntax","Only for numeric types built into std","Never — named methods are always better"],"answer":1,"explain":"Point + Point reading as vector addition, or grid[(row, col)] reading as indexing, are cases where the operator's meaning is obvious and unsurprising — that's when overloading helps rather than confuses."},{"q":"Why is `+` with side effects (like writing to a file) considered a mistake?","options":["Because it would cause a compile error","Because readers bring assumptions to + from ordinary math and don't expect side effects or mutation","Because + can only be implemented for numeric types","It isn't a mistake, it's fine practice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Operators carry implicit expectations. A + that mutates or has side effects violates those expectations — a named method (like log.append(entry)) communicates intent far more clearly."},{"q":"What does Index::index return, and how does `grid[(1, 2)]` end up as a value instead of a reference?","options":["index returns a value directly; no dereferencing happens","index returns a reference (&Output), and the [] syntax automatically dereferences it","index always panics and grid[] never actually calls it","grid[(1,2)] calls .clone() internally on Output"],"answer":1,"explain":"Index::index returns &Output. The compiler's desugaring of container[key] automatically dereferences that reference, so you see the value directly."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does `a + b` desugar to in Rust?","back":"Add::add(a, b). Every operator is a trait method call under familiar symbolic syntax."},{"front":"Which trait/method pair implements the + operator for a custom type?","back":"std::ops::Add, with an associated type Output and a method fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self::Output."},{"front":"Which trait powers container[key] syntax?","back":"std::ops::Index (read) and std::ops::IndexMut (read-write). index() returns &Output, which [] auto-dereferences."},{"front":"Does implementing Add give you += for free?","back":"No. += is backed by a separate trait, AddAssign, with its own add_assign(&mut self, rhs) method. You must implement it separately."},{"front":"Why does Point usually derive Copy when implementing Add?","back":"Most std::ops traits take self by value, which moves the operand. Deriving Copy means p1 + p2 copies p1 instead of consuming it, so p1 stays usable afterward."},{"front":"When should you overload an operator vs use a named method?","back":"Overload when the meaning is unambiguous and matches what a reader expects from ordinary math/collection syntax (Point + Point, grid[(r,c)]). Use a named method when the operator's meaning would surprise the reader (e.g. a + with side effects)."},{"front":"Name three operators and their backing std::ops traits.","back":"+ -> Add, - -> Sub, * -> Mul (also += -> AddAssign, -= -> SubAssign, [] -> Index/IndexMut)."}]},"unit-testing":{"topic":"unit-testing","title":"Unit testing","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `#[test]` attribute do?","options":["Marks a function as a test so `cargo test` discovers and runs it","Makes a function run automatically at program startup","Tells the compiler to optimize the function more aggressively","Marks a function as deprecated"],"answer":0,"explain":"`#[test]` is what `cargo test` looks for. A function without it is treated as ordinary code and never gets run as a test."},{"q":"Which macro fails the test when its two arguments are equal?","options":["assert!","assert_eq!","assert_ne!","assert_panic!"],"answer":2,"explain":"`assert_ne!(a, b)` panics (failing the test) if `a` and `b` ARE equal — the 'these must differ' check."},{"q":"What does `#[cfg(test)]` on `mod tests` actually do?","options":["It hides the module's compile warnings","It compiles the module only when running `cargo test`, so it's absent from release builds entirely","It runs the tests in a separate thread","It disables the tests without deleting them"],"answer":1,"explain":"`#[cfg(test)]` is a compile-time switch: in a normal `cargo build`, that module isn't compiled at all, so it adds zero size or dependencies to your shipped binary."},{"q":"Inside `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { ... }`, what does `use super::*;` do?","options":["Imports every crate in the Cargo registry","Brings items from the parent module (including private ones) into scope","Runs all tests in the parent module first","Marks the module as a superset of another module"],"answer":1,"explain":"`super` refers to the enclosing module. `use super::*` pulls in everything defined there, including private functions — this is why unit tests can reach private internals."},{"q":"What's the difference between `#[should_panic]` and `#[should_panic(expected = \"msg\")]`?","options":["There is no difference, `expected` is just documentation","`expected` makes the test run faster","Plain `#[should_panic]` passes on any panic; `expected` also checks the panic message contains that substring","`expected` disables the panic check entirely"],"answer":2,"explain":"Without `expected`, the test passes if the code panics for ANY reason, even an unrelated bug. Adding `expected = \"...\"` verifies the panic message actually matches, so you know you're panicking for the right reason."},{"q":"How do you run only the tests whose name contains \"parse\"?","options":["cargo test --only parse","cargo test parse","cargo run --test parse","cargo test --filter=parse"],"answer":1,"explain":"`cargo test <substring>` runs only tests whose full path (module + function name) contains that substring."},{"q":"A `#[test]` function has no `assert!`/`assert_eq!`/`assert_ne!` calls at all. What happens when you run it?","options":["It fails to compile","It always fails","It always passes, because nothing inside it can fail","cargo test skips it with a warning"],"answer":2,"explain":"A test with no assertion has nothing that can panic, so it always passes — and checks absolutely nothing. Every real test needs at least one assertion."},{"q":"Why doesn't `#[cfg(test)]` test code increase the size of your shipped release binary?","options":["The linker strips it out after compiling","Rust compresses test code separately","It's never compiled in the first place outside of `cargo test`","Test code is stored in a separate file automatically"],"answer":2,"explain":"`#[cfg(test)]` is evaluated at compile time — the annotated module simply isn't part of the source the compiler processes for a normal `cargo build`, so it can't contribute to binary size."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What three things does every test need?","back":"A `#[test]` attribute, at least one assertion, and a descriptive name so a failure tells you what broke."},{"front":"What's the difference between `assert!`, `assert_eq!`, and `assert_ne!`?","back":"`assert!(cond)` fails if cond is false. `assert_eq!(a, b)` fails if a != b (and prints both values). `assert_ne!(a, b)` fails if a == b."},{"front":"Where do unit tests conventionally live, and why?","back":"In a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { use super::*; ... }` block in the same file as the code — `use super::*` gives access to private items, and `#[cfg(test)]` excludes the whole module from non-test builds."},{"front":"What does `#[cfg(test)]` guarantee that just 'not calling the function' doesn't?","back":"It's a compile-time exclusion — the module isn't compiled at all outside `cargo test`, so it can safely use dev-dependencies and adds zero size to release builds."},{"front":"When should you use `#[should_panic(expected = \"...\")]` instead of plain `#[should_panic]`?","back":"Whenever you want to make sure the code panicked for the RIGHT reason. Plain `#[should_panic]` passes on any panic, even from an unrelated bug."},{"front":"How do you run just one test, or a group of tests matching a name?","back":"`cargo test <substring>` — it matches against the full test path (module::function_name)."},{"front":"Why is a test with no assertions dangerous?","back":"It always passes, giving false confidence — it looks like coverage but checks nothing."},{"front":"What convention does `assert_eq!(a, b)` follow, and what happens if you swap the order?","back":"Convention is `assert_eq!(actual, expected)`. Swapping still works correctly (it still fails on mismatch), it just flips which value the panic message labels 'left' vs 'right'."}]},"integration-testing":{"topic":"integration-testing","title":"Integration testing","quiz":[{"q":"How does Cargo treat each `.rs` file placed directly under `tests/`?","options":["As a module inside your library crate","As its own separate crate, compiled and linked against your library","As a script that's ignored unless named `main.rs`","As documentation, not code"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every file directly under `tests/` is compiled as its own independent binary crate that depends on your library, the same way an external project would."},{"q":"Why does `tests/api.rs` write `use my_crate::add;` instead of `use super::*;`?","options":["It's just a style preference, both work identically","Because it's a separate crate, not a child module of your library — there is no `super` to reach through","`use super::*` is deprecated","Because integration tests can't use the `use` keyword"],"answer":1,"explain":"`tests/api.rs` is compiled as its own crate, so it imports your library the way any external dependency would — by crate name, not by `super`."},{"q":"What can an integration test in `tests/` NOT see that a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` unit test CAN?","options":["Public functions","Private (non-pub) items","The standard library","println! output"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integration tests are compiled as a separate crate and only see your `pub` API, just like any real external user of your library. Unit tests live inside the crate and can see everything."},{"q":"Why do integration tests only work for library crates?","options":["Binary crates don't support the `tests/` folder syntax","A binary crate produces no importable library artifact for a tests/*.rs file to `use`","Cargo forbids running `cargo test` on binaries","Integration tests require async code"],"answer":1,"explain":"`use my_crate::...;` needs a compiled library (an rlib) to depend on. A `src/main.rs`-only project has no such library target."},{"q":"What's wrong with naming a shared test helper file `tests/common.rs`?","options":["Nothing, it's the recommended approach","Cargo treats it as its own separate test crate, so it runs (with zero tests) and shows up in the output","It causes a compile error","It's only a problem on Windows"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cargo auto-discovers every direct child of `tests/` as its own test crate. `common.rs` gets run as a (nearly empty) test binary — harmless, but noisy and not what was intended."},{"q":"How do you correctly share setup helpers across multiple integration test files?","options":["Put them in `tests/common.rs` and import with `use`","Put them in `tests/common/mod.rs` and declare `mod common;` in each file that needs them","Copy-paste the helper code into every test file","Put them in `src/lib.rs` marked private"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cargo only auto-discovers files directly inside `tests/`, not ones nested in a subdirectory — so `tests/common/mod.rs` is never treated as its own test crate. Each file opts in explicitly with `mod common;`."},{"q":"A private helper function inside your library needs a direct test. Where should that test live?","options":["In `tests/api.rs`, since integration tests are more thorough","It can't be tested at all","In a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` unit test inside the same file, which can see private items","In `tests/common/mod.rs`"],"answer":2,"explain":"Integration tests only see `pub` items. A private helper needs a unit test inside the crate, where `use super::*` gives access to private internals."},{"q":"What's a real cost of a large integration test suite?","options":["It makes your release binary larger","Each file in `tests/` triggers its own full compile of your library, which can noticeably slow `cargo test`","It disables unit tests","It requires nightly Rust"],"answer":1,"explain":"Because each `tests/*.rs` file is a separate crate, Cargo compiles your library against each one independently — many files means many compiles."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does Cargo compile files inside `tests/`?","back":"Every `.rs` file directly under `tests/` is compiled as its own separate crate, linked against your library exactly like an external dependency would be."},{"front":"What can integration tests see of your crate, and why?","back":"Only `pub` items — because each test file is a genuinely separate crate, it goes through the same visibility rules as any outside consumer of your library."},{"front":"Why do integration tests only apply to library crates?","back":"`use my_crate::...;` requires a compiled library artifact to depend on. A binary-only crate (no `src/lib.rs`) has no importable public API."},{"front":"What's wrong with `tests/common.rs` for shared helpers?","back":"Cargo auto-discovers it as its own test crate (with no tests in it), which is noisy and not intended — it shows up in `cargo test` output as a nearly-empty test run."},{"front":"What's the idiomatic way to share setup code across integration test files?","back":"`tests/common/mod.rs`, declared via `mod common;` in each file that needs it — files nested in a subdirectory of `tests/` aren't auto-discovered as their own test crates."},{"front":"Unit test or integration test: checking a private helper function directly?","back":"Unit test (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests` inside the file) — integration tests can't see private items at all."},{"front":"What real cost scales with the number of files in `tests/`?","back":"Compile time — each file is its own crate, so Cargo recompiles your library against every one of them."},{"front":"How would you fix a binary-only project (`src/main.rs`, no `src/lib.rs`) that wants integration tests?","back":"Move the logic you want tested into `src/lib.rs`, and make `main.rs` a thin wrapper that calls into the library."}]},"doc-tests-and-benchmarks":{"topic":"doc-tests-and-benchmarks","title":"Doc tests and benchmarks","quiz":[{"q":"What happens to a fenced code block inside a `///` doc comment when you run `cargo test`?","options":["Nothing, it's purely documentation","It's spell-checked but not compiled","It's compiled and executed as its own test","It's only checked when you run `cargo doc`"],"answer":2,"explain":"A bare ` ``` ` block inside a doc comment defaults to Rust and is compiled AND run by `cargo test` — a wrong example fails the build, not just the docs."},{"q":"Why can a doc-test only call `pub` items of the crate it documents?","options":["rustdoc doesn't support private items in any context","The example is compiled as if it were external code depending on the crate, the same restriction integration tests have","Because doc-tests run before compilation finishes","It's an arbitrary rustdoc limitation with no deeper reason"],"answer":1,"explain":"Doc-tests are compiled as standalone programs calling into your crate from the outside, so — like `tests/` integration tests — they only see the public API."},{"q":"What does prefixing a doc-test line with `# ` (hash, space) do?","options":["Comments the line out so it's never compiled","Marks the line as a TODO","Compiles and runs the line, but hides it from the rendered documentation","Turns the line into a doc-test title"],"answer":2,"explain":"`# `-prefixed lines are still compiled and executed by `cargo test`, they just don't appear in the example a reader sees via `cargo doc`. Handy for hiding setup/imports."},{"q":"What does `std::time::Instant::now()` capture?","options":["The current wall-clock date and time","A monotonic timestamp unaffected by system clock adjustments, meant for measuring elapsed durations","A random benchmark seed","The number of CPU cycles used so far"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Instant` is designed for measuring elapsed time between two points — it only moves forward, so it isn't thrown off by the system clock being changed."},{"q":"Why shouldn't you trust a single `Instant::now()` / `.elapsed()` measurement as a real benchmark result?","options":["Instant is inaccurate by design","It only measures in whole seconds","A single run is noisy — CPU scaling, other processes, and cold caches can swing it significantly","elapsed() requires unsafe code"],"answer":2,"explain":"One measurement captures whatever noise happened to be present during that run. It's fine for a rough sanity check, but not for concluding a change made code faster or slower."},{"q":"Does stable Rust have a built-in `cargo bench`?","options":["Yes, identical to `cargo test`","No — the `#[bench]`/`cargo bench` combo is part of the unstable `test` crate, nightly-only","Yes, but only for library crates","No, benchmarking isn't possible in Rust at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Built-in benchmarking requires nightly Rust. On stable, the ecosystem standard is the `criterion` crate."},{"q":"What does `criterion` give you that a single `Instant::elapsed()` timing doesn't?","options":["Automatic code optimization","Statistical analysis across many runs, including comparison against the previous run with a confidence interval","A GUI for editing benchmarks","The ability to benchmark without compiling the code"],"answer":1,"explain":"`criterion` runs your function many times, filters noise statistically, and reports whether a change is a real regression/improvement or just noise — not just a single raw number."},{"q":"What does wrapping a value in `black_box(...)` prevent in a criterion benchmark?","options":["It prevents the value from being printed","It prevents the compiler from optimizing away the computation because it can see the result is unused","It hides the value from the debugger","It slows down the benchmark deliberately for accuracy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without `black_box`, the optimizer can notice a computed value is never observably used and delete the whole computation, making the benchmark measure nothing."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What happens to a fenced code block inside a `///` doc comment when `cargo test` runs?","back":"It's compiled and executed as its own test — a wrong or stale example fails the build, not just the rendered docs."},{"front":"Why can doc-tests only call `pub` items?","back":"The example is compiled as if it were external code depending on the crate, the same visibility restriction integration tests in `tests/` have."},{"front":"What does a `# `-prefixed line inside a doc-test do?","back":"It's compiled and run like any other line, but hidden from the rendered documentation — used for setup/imports that would clutter the visible example."},{"front":"What does `Instant::now()` + `.elapsed()` give you?","back":"Rough, noisy manual timing using std alone — a monotonic timestamp and the Duration since it. Fine for 'is this obviously slow,' not for real comparisons."},{"front":"Does stable Rust have a built-in `cargo bench`?","back":"No — the `#[bench]`/`cargo bench` pair is part of the unstable `test` crate (nightly-only). Stable Rust uses the `criterion` crate instead."},{"front":"What makes `criterion` more trustworthy than a single `Instant` measurement?","back":"It runs the function many times, applies statistical analysis to filter noise, and compares each run to the previous one with a confidence interval."},{"front":"What does `black_box` do in a criterion benchmark, and why is it needed?","back":"It stops the compiler from optimizing away a computation whose result is never observably used — without it you might benchmark nothing at all."},{"front":"How is a `benches/*.rs` criterion file structured, and what does `criterion_main!` do?","back":"It's its own compiled binary (set via `[[bench]] harness = false` in Cargo.toml); `criterion_group!`/`criterion_main!` generate the `fn main` that runs the registered benchmarks."}]},"file-io":{"topic":"file-io","title":"File I/O","quiz":[{"q":"Why do almost all `std::fs` functions return a `Result`?","options":["Because Rust dislikes files","Because file access can always fail (missing file, no permission, full disk)","So they run faster","To make the code longer on purpose"],"answer":1,"explain":"The filesystem is the unreliable outside world. Any read or write can fail, so the API returns a Result and forces you to handle the failure case."},{"q":"What does the `?` operator do on a line like `let t = fs::read_to_string(p)?;`?","options":["Prints the error and continues","Ignores any error silently","Unwraps the Ok value, or returns the Err from the current function","Retries the read three times"],"answer":2,"explain":"`?` gives you the value on success, or immediately returns the error to the caller on failure — but only inside a function that itself returns Result or Option."},{"q":"What does `fs::write(\"log.txt\", data)` do if the file already exists?","options":["Appends the data to the end","Overwrites the file completely, erasing the old contents","Returns an error and does nothing","Creates log.txt.bak first"],"answer":1,"explain":"`fs::write` replaces the entire file. To add to an existing file instead, open it with `OpenOptions::new().append(true)`."},{"q":"Which requirement must a function meet to use the `?` operator inside it?","options":["It must be named main","It must return `Result` (or `Option`)","It must be marked `unsafe`","It must take no arguments"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` needs somewhere to send an error, so the enclosing function must return a Result or Option. That's why file-reading helpers usually return `Result<String, io::Error>`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Read an entire text file into a String?","back":"`std::fs::read_to_string(path)` — returns a `Result<String, io::Error>`."},{"front":"Write a string to a file (creating/overwriting it)?","back":"`std::fs::write(path, data)` — returns a Result; it overwrites any existing file."},{"front":"Add to a file without erasing it?","back":"Open it with `OpenOptions::new().create(true).append(true).open(path)`, then write to it."},{"front":"Why prefer `?` over `.unwrap()` in real programs?","back":"`.unwrap()`/`.expect()` crash the whole program on the first error; `?` propagates the error so it can be handled gracefully."}]},"environment-and-config":{"topic":"environment-and-config","title":"Environment variables and config","quiz":[{"q":"What does `std::env::var(\"KEY\")` return when KEY isn't set?","options":["An empty String","Err(VarError::NotPresent)","It panics immediately","None"],"answer":1,"explain":"env::var returns Result<String, VarError>. A missing variable is an Err, never a silent empty string — Rust won't let 'missing' and 'set to empty' look the same."},{"q":"Why shouldn't real secrets live in a committed `.env` file?","options":["Rust refuses to compile projects with .env files",".env files only work in test builds","Once a secret is in git history, it's compromised — rotating it is the only real fix","dotenvy encrypts the file automatically, so it's fine either way"],"answer":2,"explain":"A committed .env eventually ends up in every clone and fork forever. Treat it as local-dev convenience only; real secrets belong in the platform's secret manager."},{"q":"When should `dotenvy::dotenv()` be called?","options":["At the very end of main, after all env::var calls","Once, early in main, before you read anything with env::var","Inside every function that needs an env var","It's called automatically by Cargo before main runs"],"answer":1,"explain":"It has to run before the reads that depend on it — calling it late means the vars it would have loaded aren't there yet."},{"q":"Why deserialize config into a `#[derive(Deserialize)]` struct instead of many loose `env::var` calls?","options":["It's the only way to read a file in Rust","Field types are checked, and a missing or wrong field gives one clear error instead of scattered bugs","It removes the need for a Cargo.toml file","env::var can't return numeric values at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once you have more than a couple of related settings, a typed struct catches problems at startup with one clear message instead of tracking down a bug three functions later."},{"q":"What's the problem with `env::var(\"KEY\").unwrap_or_default()` for a setting that must be configured correctly?","options":["It doesn't compile","It silently treats 'missing' the same as 'set to empty on purpose,' hiding real misconfiguration","It always panics on success","unwrap_or_default() doesn't exist on Result"],"answer":1,"explain":"Falling back to a default on error can be fine for optional settings, but it can also mask a real problem — a required var that's simply not set."},{"q":"Which crate does this lesson use to load a `.env` file into the process environment?","options":["serde","toml","dotenvy","clap"],"answer":2,"explain":"dotenvy::dotenv() reads a .env file and copies its key-value pairs into the process environment, so env::var finds them normally afterward."},{"q":"What should a committed `.env.example` file contain?","options":["Real production secrets","Nothing — it should be an empty file","The key names with fake/placeholder values, no real secrets","A copy of Cargo.lock"],"answer":2,"explain":".env.example documents which variables a project needs without leaking any actual secret values."},{"q":"What is the return type of `env::var(\"KEY\")`?","options":["String","Option<String>","Result<String, VarError>","&str"],"answer":2,"explain":"It's a Result, not a String — reading the environment can fail (the variable might not exist), so the type says so."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does std::env::var(\"KEY\") return?","back":"Result<String, VarError> — Ok(value) if set, Err if missing. Never a silent empty string."},{"front":"Where should real secrets live for production?","back":"In the platform's real secret manager — never in a committed .env file. .env is for local dev convenience only."},{"front":"When should dotenvy::dotenv() be called?","back":"Once, at the very top of main, before any env::var reads that depend on it."},{"front":"What's the safer alternative to committing a real .env file?","back":"Add .env to .gitignore and commit a .env.example with key names but no real values."},{"front":"When should you switch from loose env::var calls to a config struct?","back":"Once you have more than a couple of related settings — deserialize a TOML/JSON file into a #[derive(Deserialize)] struct instead."},{"front":"Why prefer .expect(\"message\") over bare .unwrap() on a required env var?","back":".expect gives a clear, custom message about exactly what's missing; bare .unwrap() just says 'called unwrap on an Err value.'"},{"front":"What crate/function deserializes a TOML file into a struct?","back":"toml::from_str, combined with #[derive(Deserialize)] from serde on the target struct."}]},"process-and-command":{"topic":"process-and-command","title":"Args, exit codes, and subprocesses","quiz":[{"q":"What is the first element yielded by `std::env::args()`?","options":["The first CLI argument the user typed","The program's own name/path","Always an empty string","The current username"],"answer":1,"explain":"Just like argv[0] in C, the first element is the program itself — skip it with .skip(1) before parsing real arguments."},{"q":"What's the key difference between returning `ExitCode` from `main` and calling `std::process::exit()`?","options":["No real difference, they're interchangeable","exit() runs Drop for everything on the stack, returning ExitCode doesn't","Returning ExitCode runs Drop normally; process::exit() skips Drop entirely","ExitCode can only ever be 0 or 1"],"answer":2,"explain":"Returning from main is a normal function return, so destructors run. process::exit() terminates immediately and skips Drop for anything still on the stack."},{"q":"What does `Command::output()` give you?","options":["Nothing — it just runs the subprocess silently with no way to inspect it","Captured stdout/stderr as bytes, plus the exit status — nothing prints automatically","Direct terminal output only, with no way to capture it","A live streaming iterator of the subprocess's output"],"answer":1,"explain":"output() buffers stdout and stderr into memory as Vec<u8> and returns them alongside the exit status. You have to print them yourself if you want them visible."},{"q":"How does `Command::status()` behave differently from `.output()`?","options":["It doesn't actually run the subprocess","It inherits the parent's stdin/stdout/stderr directly and only returns the exit status","It captures stdout but not stderr","It behaves identically to .output()"],"answer":1,"explain":"status() lets the subprocess's output go straight to the real terminal, exactly as if you'd run it yourself, and hands back only the exit status."},{"q":"Why does a bare `return;` fail to compile inside `fn main() -> ExitCode`?","options":["return is never allowed anywhere in Rust","return; produces (), which doesn't match the required ExitCode return type","ExitCode requires an async main function","main is never allowed to return early"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every path out of a function must match its declared return type. A bare return; yields (), a type mismatch against ExitCode."},{"q":"Does a subprocess spawning without error guarantee it succeeded at its job?","options":["Yes — if Command runs without error, the subprocess always succeeded","No — you must check the exit status separately, since the subprocess can run and still fail its own task","Yes, but only when using .status()","No — spawning itself can never succeed"],"answer":1,"explain":"Spawning and running are separate from the subprocess's own result. Always check output.status.success() (or status.success())."},{"q":"Why prefer returning `ExitCode` from `main` over calling `process::exit` mid-function?","options":["process::exit is deprecated in current Rust","process::exit is measurably slower to execute","process::exit skips Drop for values still on the stack, so cleanup like flushing or releasing locks may not run","ExitCode is required by Cargo's build system"],"answer":2,"explain":"If anything on the stack needs cleanup when the program ends, returning ExitCode lets that cleanup run; process::exit cuts straight through it."},{"q":"What Rust type holds a subprocess's captured result from `.output()`?","options":["A plain String","ExitCode","Output, with stdout/stderr as Vec<u8> fields and a status field","Command itself"],"answer":2,"explain":"output() returns io::Result<Output>, where Output bundles captured stdout, stderr, and the exit status together."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What's always the first element of std::env::args()?","back":"The program's own name/path — skip it with .skip(1) before parsing real arguments."},{"front":"What does std::process::exit() skip that returning normally doesn't?","back":"It skips running Drop for everything still on the stack — no destructors run on the way out."},{"front":"When should you prefer ExitCode over process::exit()?","back":"Whenever something on the stack needs cleanup (flushing a file, releasing a lock) — returning ExitCode lets Drop run normally."},{"front":"What does Command::output() capture?","back":"The subprocess's stdout and stderr as bytes (Vec<u8>), plus its exit status — nothing prints to your terminal automatically."},{"front":"What does Command::status() do with the subprocess's streams?","back":"Lets them inherit the parent's stdin/stdout/stderr directly, so output appears on the real terminal; you only get back the exit status."},{"front":"How do you check whether a subprocess actually succeeded, not just spawned?","back":"Check output.status.success() (or status.success()) — spawning without error doesn't mean the subprocess's own job succeeded."},{"front":"What value does a bare `return;` produce?","back":"() — the empty tuple. In a function that must return ExitCode, that's a type mismatch."}]},"threads-and-spawn":{"topic":"threads-and-spawn","title":"Threads and spawn","quiz":[{"q":"What does `thread::spawn(closure)` return?","options":["The closure's return value immediately","A `JoinHandle` you can later `.join()` to get the result","Nothing — it blocks until the closure finishes","A `Result<(), Error>`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`thread::spawn` starts the closure running on a new OS thread and returns right away with a `JoinHandle`, which you trade in later via `.join()`."},{"q":"Why does a closure passed to `thread::spawn` usually need to be `move`?","options":["It makes the thread start faster","It gives the thread ownership of captured values, since the compiler can't prove borrowed data outlives the new thread","It's required syntax with no real meaning","It pins the thread to a specific CPU core"],"answer":1,"explain":"`thread::spawn` needs a `'static` closure — one that might outlive the current stack frame. `move` transfers ownership in, so there's nothing left that could dangle."},{"q":"What type does `handle.join()` return?","options":["The closure's return value directly, panicking if the thread failed","`Result<T, Box<dyn Any + Send>>` — `Ok(value)` on success, `Err(payload)` if the thread panicked","`()`, always","Another `JoinHandle`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.join()` gives back a `Result`, not the bare value. You need `.unwrap()` or a `match` to reach the `T` inside `Ok`."},{"q":"A spawned thread panics (say, an out-of-bounds index). What happens to the rest of the program?","options":["The whole process exits immediately","Only that thread unwinds; `.join()` returns `Err`, and other threads (including `main`) keep running","The panic is silently swallowed and never reported","`main` automatically restarts the failed thread"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust unwinds just the panicking thread's stack. The panic surfaces as `Err` from `.join()` — it does not bring down threads that didn't panic."},{"q":"You call `handle.join().unwrap()` and the spawned thread had panicked. What happens?","options":["It silently returns a default value","It panics on the thread that called `.join()`, propagating the failure","It restarts the spawned thread automatically","It converts the `Err` into `Ok(())`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Result::unwrap()` panics when given an `Err`. So `.join().unwrap()` re-raises the worker's panic, but now on the joining thread."},{"q":"In `thread::spawn(|| { ...; last_expr })`, what happens to `last_expr`?","options":["It's ignored entirely","It becomes the value wrapped in `Ok(...)` inside `handle.join()`'s result","It's printed to stdout automatically","It's a compile error unless you write `return` explicitly"],"answer":1,"explain":"Like any Rust function or closure, the last expression is the return value — here, it becomes the `T` in the thread's `JoinHandle<T>`."},{"q":"What happens if `main` finishes before a spawned thread you never `.join()`-ed?","options":["Rust automatically waits for every spawned thread before exiting","The process exits, and the unjoined thread is cut off mid-work","The compiler refuses to build the program","The spawned thread is promoted to run as the new `main`"],"answer":1,"explain":"When `main` returns, the process exits — any spawned threads still running are simply cut off. `.join()` is what makes `main` wait for them."},{"q":"Why is spawning a brand-new OS thread for every tiny unit of work often a bad idea?","options":["Threads in Rust can't return values","Each OS thread has real overhead (its own stack, kernel bookkeeping) — a thread pool is cheaper for lots of small tasks","It's not actually possible to spawn more than a few threads in Rust","OS threads share memory unsafely by default"],"answer":1,"explain":"OS threads aren't free — each one costs memory and scheduling overhead. For many short-lived jobs, a thread pool (e.g. `rayon`) reuses a fixed set of threads instead."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Start work on a new thread?","back":"`thread::spawn(move || { ... })` — returns a `JoinHandle<T>` immediately; the closure runs concurrently."},{"front":"Why `move` on the closure?","back":"The closure might outlive the current stack frame, so it must own (not borrow) the values it captures — `move` transfers ownership in."},{"front":"What does `.join()` return?","back":"`Result<T, Box<dyn Any + Send>>` — `Ok(value)` is the closure's return value; `Err(payload)` means the thread panicked."},{"front":"Does a panic in a spawned thread crash the whole program?","back":"No. Only that thread unwinds; `.join()` reports it as `Err`, and every other thread (including `main`) keeps running."},{"front":"What does `.join().unwrap()` do if the thread panicked?","back":"It re-panics — but now on the thread that called `.join()`. Match the `Err` explicitly if you want to contain the failure instead."},{"front":"What happens if you never call `.join()` on a handle?","back":"If `main` exits first, the spawned thread is simply cut off mid-work — the process doesn't wait for it automatically."},{"front":"When should you avoid spawning a thread per task?","back":"When you have many small, short-lived tasks — OS threads have real overhead; use a thread pool (e.g. `rayon`) instead."}]},"channels-mpsc":{"topic":"channels-mpsc","title":"Channels (mpsc)","quiz":[{"q":"What does Rust's concurrency motto \"share memory by communicating\" mean in practice?","options":["Threads should share a global mutable variable","Threads pass ownership of values through a channel instead of reaching into shared memory","Only `main` is allowed to hold any data","Every value must be wrapped in `unsafe`"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instead of multiple threads touching the same memory, one thread sends a value down a channel and the other receives full ownership of it — no shared mutable state involved."},{"q":"What does `mpsc` stand for, and what does it mean for `Receiver`?","options":["Multi-producer, single-consumer — there's only ever one `Receiver`","Multi-process shared cache — receivers can be cloned freely","Message-passing sync channel — it's always bounded","Multi-consumer, single-producer — only one thread may ever send"],"answer":0,"explain":"`mpsc::channel()` supports many producers (clone `Sender` as needed) but exactly one consumer — `Receiver` is not `Clone`."},{"q":"After `tx.send(value)`, what can the sending thread still do with `value`?","options":["Read it, but not mutate it","Nothing — `send` takes ownership, so `value` was moved","Clone it for free since channels borrow internally","Send it again on the same channel"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Sender::send(&self, t: T)` takes `T` by value. Once sent, the value belongs to the receiving thread; using it again on the sender's side is a use-after-move error."},{"q":"You need three threads to all send messages into one channel. What do you do?","options":["Clone the `Receiver` three times","Clone the `Sender` once per thread — each clone is a handle to the same pipe","Create three separate `mpsc::channel()` pairs and merge them manually","Wrap `tx` in a `Mutex` so threads take turns"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Sender` implements `Clone` for exactly this reason. Each clone points at the same channel, so many threads can send independently."},{"q":"When does a `for msg in rx` loop finish?","options":["After exactly one message is received","Once every `Sender` (the original plus all clones) has been dropped","As soon as the receiving thread calls `.stop()`","It never finishes automatically — you must `break` manually"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Receiver` iterates until it's certain no more messages can arrive, which is exactly when every `Sender` handle has gone out of scope."},{"q":"A `for msg in rx` loop hangs forever even though every worker thread finished. What's the likely cause?","options":["The channel is bounded and full","A `Sender` clone (maybe the original `tx`, unused after cloning) is still alive somewhere and was never dropped","`Receiver` requires an explicit `.close()` call","The messages are the wrong type"],"answer":1,"explain":"As long as any `Sender` handle exists — even one nobody is actively using — the channel isn't considered closed, so the receiving loop keeps waiting."},{"q":"Why might `.send(value).unwrap()` panic in a long-running producer thread?","options":["`send` always fails on the first call","`send` returns `Err` once the `Receiver` has been dropped, and there's nothing left to receive the value","Only `.recv()` can fail, never `.send()`","Sending more than one value always errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"If the reader gave up and dropped `rx`, `send` returns `Err(SendError(value))` handing the value back. Unwrapping that panics — worth handling explicitly in long-lived producers."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Create a channel?","back":"`let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();` — `tx` is the `Sender`, `rx` is the `Receiver`."},{"front":"What does `mpsc` stand for?","back":"Multi-producer, single-consumer: many `Sender`s (via `.clone()`), exactly one `Receiver`."},{"front":"What does `.send(value)` do to `value`?","back":"Moves it across the channel — the receiving thread gets ownership, not a reference."},{"front":"How do multiple threads send into one channel?","back":"Clone the `Sender` (`tx.clone()`) once per thread — each clone is a handle to the same underlying pipe."},{"front":"When does `for msg in rx` stop looping?","back":"Automatically, once every `Sender` (original and all clones) has been dropped — no manual close needed."},{"front":"Why does an unused `Sender` clone left alive cause a hang?","back":"The channel only closes once ALL senders are gone. One forgotten clone (even idle) keeps the receiver waiting forever."},{"front":"Can you clone a `Receiver`?","back":"No — only `Sender` is `Clone`. `std::sync::mpsc` always has exactly one consumer."}]},"shared-state-mutex-and-arc":{"topic":"shared-state-mutex-and-arc","title":"Shared state: Arc and Mutex","quiz":[{"q":"Why can't you use `Rc<RefCell<T>>` to share mutable state across threads?","options":["`Rc` and `RefCell` are deprecated","`Rc`'s reference count isn't updated atomically, so the compiler marks it unsafe to send across threads","`RefCell` only works with numbers","They work fine, it's just slower than `Arc<Mutex<T>>`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Rc`'s internal counter can be corrupted if two threads bump it at once. The compiler catches this at compile time by not implementing `Send` for `Rc`."},{"q":"What does `Arc::clone(&data)` actually do?","options":["Deep-copies the value inside the `Arc`","Bumps an atomic reference count and returns another pointer to the same value","Creates a brand-new independent `Mutex`","Moves the value to the cloning thread, invalidating the original"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Arc` clones are cheap by design — they share the same underlying allocation and just increment a counter, not copy the data."},{"q":"What does `mutex.lock()` return?","options":["`&mut T` directly","`Result<MutexGuard<T>, PoisonError<...>>` — `Ok` holds a guard that derefs to `&mut T`","`bool` — whether the lock succeeded","Nothing; it just blocks and returns `()`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.lock()` blocks until available, then hands back a `Result` wrapping a `MutexGuard`. The `Err` case only occurs if the mutex was poisoned by a panic while locked."},{"q":"When does a `MutexGuard` release its lock?","options":["You must call `.unlock()` explicitly","Automatically, when the guard is dropped (goes out of scope)","Only when the program exits","As soon as you read the value once"],"answer":1,"explain":"`MutexGuard` implements `Drop`, which releases the lock. There's no manual unlock call — scoping the guard tightly is how you control lock duration."},{"q":"Five threads each need to increment one shared counter. What's the idiomatic pattern?","options":["Pass a `&mut i32` to each thread","`Arc<Mutex<i32>>` — clone the `Arc` for each thread, `.lock()` before mutating","Give each thread its own separate counter and sum them with a channel","Wrap the counter in `Rc<Cell<i32>>`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Arc` provides thread-safe shared ownership; `Mutex` provides exclusive access. Together they're the standard way to mutate shared state safely across threads."},{"q":"What causes the classic two-mutex deadlock?","options":["Using `Arc` instead of `Rc`","Two threads locking the same two mutexes in different orders, so each ends up waiting on a lock the other holds","Forgetting to call `Arc::clone`","A `Mutex` that contains a `String` instead of a number"],"answer":1,"explain":"If thread A locks mutex 1 then mutex 2, while thread B locks mutex 2 then mutex 1, an unlucky interleaving leaves each thread holding one lock and waiting forever for the other."},{"q":"How do you avoid the two-mutex deadlock described above?","options":["Never use more than one `Mutex` in a program","Always lock the mutexes in the same global order across every code path","Wrap every `Mutex` in an `Arc` twice","Call `.lock().unwrap()` instead of `.lock()`"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deadlocks from lock ordering disappear if every thread acquires shared locks in a consistent order — then the circular-wait interleaving simply can't occur."},{"q":"What does the compiler do if you try to share a plain (non-`Arc`, non-`Mutex`) mutable reference across threads?","options":["Allows it, but warns you","Refuses to compile — the type isn't `Send`/`Sync` for that kind of sharing","Silently clones the value per thread","Automatically wraps it in a `Mutex` for you"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust's `Send`/`Sync` traits are checked at compile time. A bare shared mutable reference across threads fails to compile rather than risking a data race at runtime."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Share ownership of a value across threads?","back":"`Arc<T>` — `Arc::clone(&x)` bumps an atomic refcount (cheap); the data itself is shared, not copied."},{"front":"Mutate shared data safely across threads?","back":"`Mutex<T>` — `.lock()` blocks until free, returns a `MutexGuard` that derefs to `&mut T`."},{"front":"When does a `MutexGuard` unlock?","back":"Automatically when it's dropped (goes out of scope) — no manual unlock call needed."},{"front":"Standard pattern for shared mutable state across threads?","back":"`Arc<Mutex<T>>` — `Arc` for shared ownership, `Mutex` for exclusive one-at-a-time access."},{"front":"Why not `Rc<RefCell<T>>` across threads?","back":"`Rc`'s refcount isn't atomic and isn't `Send` — the compiler refuses to let it cross a thread boundary. Use `Arc<Mutex<T>>` instead."},{"front":"What causes a two-mutex deadlock?","back":"Two threads locking the same two mutexes in different orders — each ends up holding one lock while waiting on the other, forever."},{"front":"How do you prevent lock-ordering deadlocks?","back":"Always acquire shared mutexes in the same global order, on every code path, everywhere in the program."},{"front":"What does `.lock()` return, exactly?","back":"`Result<MutexGuard<T>, PoisonError<...>>` — `Err` only if another thread panicked while holding the lock (a 'poisoned' mutex)."}]},"async-basics":{"topic":"async-basics","title":"Async and await basics","quiz":[{"q":"What actually happens when you call an `async fn`?","options":["The function body runs immediately, like a normal function","Rust returns a `Future` without running the body","Rust spawns a new OS thread to run it","The call blocks until the result is ready"],"answer":1,"explain":"Calling an async fn only builds a Future — an inert state machine sitting at step zero. The body doesn't execute until something polls/awaits it."},{"q":"An `async fn` has a `println!` as its first line. You call the function but never `.await` it. Does the `println!` run?","options":["Yes, calling always runs the first line","No — calling an async fn only constructs a Future; nothing inside runs until it's driven","Only if the function has no arguments","Only in debug builds"],"answer":1,"explain":"The function body is completely inert until the Future is polled by an .await or an executor. Calling it alone never executes a single line inside."},{"q":"Where is `.await` legal to write?","options":["Anywhere in any function","Only inside an `async fn` or `async` block","Only inside `fn main`","Only inside a closure"],"answer":1,"explain":".await can only appear inside async code, because only that code has been rewritten by the compiler into a pollable state machine that knows how to pause and resume."},{"q":"What compile error do you get from writing `value.await` inside a plain (non-async) `fn main()`?","options":["E0502 — cannot borrow as mutable","E0308 — mismatched types","E0728 — `await` is only allowed inside `async` functions and blocks","No error — it's just a warning"],"answer":2,"explain":"The compiler rejects .await outside async code with error E0728, since only async functions and blocks are compiled to support pausing and resuming."},{"q":"What's the key behavioral difference between `.await` (waiting on I/O) and a blocking call like `std::thread::sleep`?","options":["There's no real difference — both freeze the thread the same way","`.await` yields control back so other work can run on the same thread while waiting; `std::thread::sleep` freezes the whole thread","`.await` always runs on a different CPU core automatically","`std::thread::sleep` is always faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"When a future awaited via .await isn't ready, it returns control to the executor instead of the CPU sitting idle — other work can proceed. A real blocking call has no such courtesy."},{"q":"What does Rust's standard library provide for async, and what is it missing?","options":["It provides both the async/.await syntax and a built-in executor","It provides the async/.await syntax and the Future trait, but ships no executor","It provides an executor, but no async/.await syntax","It provides neither — both come from an external crate"],"answer":1,"explain":"async/.await and the Future trait are language/std features. Actually running a future to completion needs an executor/runtime, which Rust deliberately doesn't ship — you pick one, like Tokio."},{"q":"You call an async fn but never `.await` or spawn the resulting value. What does the compiler tell you?","options":["`unused implementer of \\`Future\\` that must be used`","`unreachable code`","`this function is deprecated`","Nothing — no warning is produced"],"answer":0,"explain":"The compiler's must_use lint on Future fires with 'unused implementer of Future that must be used', noting that futures do nothing unless you .await or poll them."},{"q":"Why doesn't wrapping a purely CPU-bound loop (no I/O waiting at all) in `async` make it faster?","options":["Async always makes every program slower, period","Async's benefit comes from yielding control during I/O waits; with nothing to wait on, there's no gap to fill and only bookkeeping overhead is added","CPU-bound code cannot legally be written inside an async fn","async fn only works for functions that use the network"],"answer":1,"explain":"Async concurrency comes from overlapping waiting time. A tight computation with no waiting gains nothing from being async and just carries the state-machine overhead."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does calling an `async fn` return?","back":"A `Future` — an inert value representing the work to be done. The function body has not run yet; nothing happens until the future is polled."},{"front":"What does `.await` actually do?","back":"Drives a future forward by polling it. While it's waiting on something, it yields control back to whatever is running it instead of blocking the thread."},{"front":"Where can `.await` legally appear?","back":"Only inside `async` code — an `async fn` body or an `async` block. Using it elsewhere is a compile error (E0728)."},{"front":"What does Rust's standard library provide for async, and what does it deliberately NOT provide?","back":"It provides the `async`/`.await` syntax and the `Future` trait. It does NOT provide an executor — you need a runtime like Tokio to actually drive a future to completion."},{"front":"What warning appears if you create a Future and never `.await` or spawn it?","back":"`unused implementer of \\`Future\\` that must be used` — futures do nothing unless you `.await` or poll them."},{"front":"How does `.await` differ from `std::thread::sleep` while both are 'waiting'?","back":"`.await` yields the thread so other work can run while waiting; `std::thread::sleep` blocks the entire OS thread, so nothing else on it can run in the meantime."},{"front":"Roughly what does the compiler turn an `async fn`'s body into?","back":"A state machine implementing the `Future` trait, with a `poll` method that returns `Poll::Ready(value)` once done or `Poll::Pending` when it needs to wait."},{"front":"Why is async not automatically a speed boost for CPU-bound work?","back":"Async's win comes from yielding during I/O waits. A pure computation has nothing to yield during, so async adds overhead with no benefit — plain threads (or nothing) are usually better there."}]},"tokio-runtime-and-tasks":{"topic":"tokio-runtime-and-tasks","title":"The Tokio runtime and tasks","quiz":[{"q":"What does `#[tokio::main]` do to `async fn main`?","options":["Nothing at runtime — it's just documentation","Rewrites `main` into an ordinary function that builds a Tokio runtime and blocks on your async body","Spawns `main`'s body on a background thread and returns immediately","Converts `main` into a `macro_rules!` macro"],"answer":1,"explain":"The macro expands to a plain fn main that builds a Tokio runtime (e.g. Builder::new_multi_thread().build()) and calls .block_on(...) with your async body — that block_on is the executor driving your futures."},{"q":"What does `tokio::spawn(future)` return?","options":["The future's result directly, synchronously","A `JoinHandle` you can `.await` later to get the task's result","Nothing — spawned tasks are always fire-and-forget","A brand new Tokio runtime"],"answer":1,"explain":"tokio::spawn schedules the future to run concurrently and immediately hands back a JoinHandle. Awaiting the handle waits for the task and yields its return value."},{"q":"Why must a task passed to `tokio::spawn` be `'static` and `Send`?","options":["It's an arbitrary limitation with no real reason","The task can be moved between worker threads and may outlive the function that spawned it, so it can't safely borrow local data or hold non-thread-safe types","Because Tokio can only ever run on a single OS thread","Because async functions are never allowed to capture variables"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tokio's scheduler can migrate a task across worker threads at any .await point, and the task may run long after its spawning function returns — so it must fully own everything it touches, and that data must be thread-safe."},{"q":"`tokio::spawn(async { println!(\"{name}\"); })` fails to compile with error E0373 because it borrows `name` from the enclosing function. What's the fix?","options":["Change `async` to `async move` so the task owns its own copy of `name`","Change `name`'s type from `String` to `&str`","Remove the `println!` call entirely","Wrap the whole block in `unsafe { }`"],"answer":0,"explain":"async move captures name by value instead of by reference, so the spawned task no longer depends on the spawning function's stack frame — satisfying the 'static requirement."},{"q":"What does `tokio::join!(a, b)` do?","options":["Runs `a`, then runs `b` after `a` finishes","Runs `a` and `b` concurrently and returns once BOTH have finished","Runs whichever of `a` or `b` finishes first and drops the other","Spawns `a` and `b` onto independent OS threads with no coordination between them"],"answer":1,"explain":"join! drives all its futures concurrently on the same task and only completes once every one of them is done, returning a tuple of their results."},{"q":"In `tokio::select! { a => ..., b => ... }`, what happens to the branch that does NOT finish first?","options":["It keeps running in the background until it eventually finishes","It is dropped/cancelled immediately once the other branch wins","It causes a compile error","It automatically retries from the start"],"answer":1,"explain":"select! polls all branches concurrently; the instant one completes, its arm runs and every other branch is dropped mid-flight — it is not resumed or finished later."},{"q":"Why does calling `std::thread::sleep` inside an async task cause a real performance problem, even though the code compiles fine?","options":["It doesn't actually cause any problem","It freezes the entire OS worker thread it's running on, so every other task scheduled on that same thread is stalled until it returns","It only slows down the task that called it and nothing else","It silently crashes the whole program"],"answer":1,"explain":"std::thread::sleep never yields — it blocks the worker thread outright. On a runtime with limited worker threads, that stalls every other task sharing that thread, not just the caller."},{"q":"What's the right tool for running real blocking or CPU-heavy work inside an async program without stalling other tasks?","options":["`tokio::join!`","`tokio::select!`","`tokio::task::spawn_blocking`","Just calling it directly inside the `async fn`"],"answer":2,"explain":"spawn_blocking moves the closure onto a separate thread pool dedicated to blocking work, so it never occupies (and stalls) the async worker threads."},{"q":"Which of these is a telltale sign that async code has no runtime actually driving it?","options":["The program compiles and runs exactly as expected","A compile error about needing async context, or a runtime panic like 'there is no reactor running'","The program runs noticeably faster than a synchronous version","Nothing different happens at all — it's indistinguishable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Missing #[tokio::main]/the tokio dependency shows up either as a compile-time error (.await needs async context) or, if you tried to drive a future manually without a reactor, a runtime panic complaining no runtime/reactor is running."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does `#[tokio::main]` do?","back":"Rewrites `async fn main` into an ordinary `fn main` that builds a Tokio runtime (roughly `Builder::new_multi_thread().enable_all().build()`) and blocks on your async body with `.block_on(...)`."},{"front":"What does `tokio::spawn` do, and what does it return?","back":"Schedules a future to run concurrently on Tokio's worker pool. Returns a `JoinHandle` you can `.await` to get the task's result."},{"front":"What two bounds must a spawned future satisfy, and why?","back":"`'static` (owns everything it touches) and `Send` (safe to move across threads) — because the task can migrate between worker threads and may outlive the function that spawned it."},{"front":"How do you fix `tokio::spawn(async { ... })` erroring E0373 because it borrows a local?","back":"Add `move`: `tokio::spawn(async move { ... })`, so the task owns a copy of the data instead of borrowing it."},{"front":"`tokio::join!` vs `tokio::select!` — what's the core difference?","back":"`join!` runs all its futures concurrently and waits for ALL of them to finish. `select!` races them and proceeds with whichever finishes FIRST, cancelling the rest."},{"front":"Why does `std::thread::sleep` inside an async task hurt performance?","back":"It blocks the entire OS worker thread it runs on — every other task scheduled on that thread stalls until the sleep call returns. Use `tokio::time::sleep` instead."},{"front":"What should you use for real blocking or CPU-heavy work inside an async program?","back":"`tokio::task::spawn_blocking` — it moves the work onto a separate thread pool so it doesn't stall the async worker threads."},{"front":"What error appears when `tokio::spawn`'s future borrows a non-`'static` local?","back":"E0373: \"async block may outlive the current function, but it borrows `x`, which is owned by the current function.\" Fix with `async move`."}]},"declarative-macros":{"topic":"declarative-macros","title":"Declarative macros (macro_rules!)","quiz":[{"q":"What's the key difference between how a function and a macro receive their arguments?","options":["Functions receive values; macros receive tokens (code)","Macros receive values; functions receive tokens","Both receive fully evaluated values","There's no real difference"],"answer":0,"explain":"A function operates on already-evaluated values at runtime. A macro receives the raw tokens of the call and expands into new code before type checking runs."},{"q":"When does macro_rules! expansion happen relative to type checking?","options":["Before type checking","After type checking","During type checking, interleaved","Only if compilation fails"],"answer":0,"explain":"Macros expand fully into plain Rust source before the compiler starts checking types — a macro literally cannot see or reason about types."},{"q":"In the pattern `($msg:expr)`, what does `:expr` do?","options":["Declares $msg as a function parameter","A fragment specifier that limits $msg to matching an expression","Converts $msg into a string at compile time","Marks $msg as an optional argument"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fragment specifiers like :expr, :ident, :ty constrain what shape of code a captured $name is allowed to match."},{"q":"What does `$( $x:expr ),*` mean in a macro_rules! pattern?","options":["Match exactly one expression","Match zero or more comma-separated expressions","Match a single block of code","Match a type name"],"answer":1,"explain":"The $( ... ),* syntax captures a repeated, comma-separated list — the same mechanism vec! uses to accept any number of items."},{"q":"Given `macro_rules! twice { ($x:expr) => { { $x; $x } }; }`, what does `twice!(n += 1)` do to `n`, starting from 0?","options":["Increments n by 1, because $x is evaluated once and the result reused","Increments n by 2, because $x is pasted as raw tokens and both copies run","Leaves n at 0, because macros don't execute code","Fails to compile"],"answer":1,"explain":"A macro copies tokens, not values. $x appears twice in the expansion, so `n += 1` literally runs twice, adding 2 total."},{"q":"Why does `vec[1, 2, 3]` (without the `!`) fail to compile?","options":["vec is a reserved keyword in Rust","The compiler looks for a function named vec, and none exists — only the vec! macro does","Square brackets are invalid syntax in Rust","vec! requires parentheses instead of brackets"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without the !, Rust reads `vec[...]` as indexing into something named vec. No function or variable named vec exists — only the vec! macro."},{"q":"A macro rule `($x:expr) => { ... }` is called as `my_vec![1, 2, 3]` and fails with 'no rules expected the token ,'. What's the fix?","options":["Change :expr to :ty","Add repetition: `( $( $x:expr ),* )` in the pattern, and `$( ... )*` in the expansion","Remove the square brackets from the call site","Nothing — Rust automatically handles multiple arguments"],"answer":1,"explain":"A single $x:expr pattern only matches one expression. Repetition syntax is required to accept a variable-length, comma-separated list."},{"q":"Why should you generally prefer a plain function over a macro when both could work?","options":["Functions always run faster at runtime","Macros are harder to read and debug and tooling understands them less well; reach for one only when a function truly can't do the job","Macros can't accept any arguments","Functions can't be unit tested"],"answer":1,"explain":"Macros add real complexity — error messages point into the expansion, and editor tooling has weaker macro support. Use one only for variadic arguments or genuine code generation."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the `!` in `println!`, `vec!`, `format!` signal?","back":"That you're calling a macro, not a function. Macro calls always use `!`."},{"front":"When does a macro_rules! macro expand relative to type checking?","back":"Before type checking. Macros operate purely on tokens (syntax), so expansion happens first — the compiler only checks types on the code that comes out."},{"front":"What is a fragment specifier?","back":"The `:type` part after a captured name in a macro pattern (e.g. `:expr`, `:ident`, `:ty`, `:block`) — it constrains what shape of code that capture is allowed to match."},{"front":"Name four common fragment specifiers.","back":"`expr` (expression), `ident` (a bare name), `ty` (a type), `block` (a `{ }` block)."},{"front":"What does `$( $x:expr ),*` capture in a macro pattern?","back":"Zero or more expressions, separated by commas — the mechanism behind variadic macros like `vec!`."},{"front":"If `$x` appears twice in a macro's expansion and the caller passes `n += 1`, what happens when the macro runs?","back":"`n += 1` runs twice — macros paste raw tokens, not evaluated values, so repeating `$x` repeats the code."},{"front":"When should you reach for a macro instead of a function?","back":"Only when a function genuinely can't do the job — a variable number of arguments, or generating new code (structs, impls, match arms) that a function can't produce."},{"front":"What's the basic shape of a macro_rules! definition?","back":"`macro_rules! name { (pattern) => { expansion }; }` — the pattern captures pieces of the call, the expansion is the code generated in their place."}]},"procedural-macros-overview":{"topic":"procedural-macros-overview","title":"Procedural macros (derive macros you use)","quiz":[{"q":"What are the three kinds of procedural macros?","options":["Derive, attribute, and function-like","Struct, enum, and trait","Sync, async, and blocking","Public, private, and crate-level"],"answer":0,"explain":"Proc macros come in three flavors: derive (#[derive(X)]), attribute (#[tokio::main]), and function-like (sqlx::query!(...))."},{"q":"What does `#[derive(Serialize)]` actually do to your struct's own definition?","options":["Rewrites the struct's fields","Nothing to the definition itself — it generates a separate impl Serialize block next to it","Deletes any unused fields","Converts the struct into an enum"],"answer":1,"explain":"A derive macro adds new code (typically a trait impl) alongside your type. Your original struct or enum definition is never touched."},{"q":"Why can't `fn main()` directly contain top-level `async` code without help?","options":["Async functions can't return values","Something has to create an async runtime and drive the future — that's what an attribute macro like #[tokio::main] generates for you","main() can never be async in any language","It actually can, no macro is needed"],"answer":1,"explain":"#[tokio::main] rewrites your async fn main into a plain fn main that builds a Tokio runtime and blocks on your async body — Rust has no built-in async main."},{"q":"What makes `sqlx::query!(\"...\")` different from an ordinary macro_rules! macro, even though both are called as name!(...)?","options":["Nothing — they work identically","sqlx::query! is a function-like proc macro that can do real compile-time work, like checking your SQL against a live database schema","macro_rules! macros are always faster to compile","sqlx::query! can only be used inside tests"],"answer":1,"explain":"Function-like proc macros look like macro_rules! calls but are full Rust programs that can inspect external state (a DB schema, a file) at compile time — something pure token-matching can never do."},{"q":"What must a crate that defines a proc macro set in its Cargo.toml?","options":["edition = \"2021\"","`proc-macro = true` under [lib]","`crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`","Nothing special is required"],"answer":1,"explain":"Proc macro crates declare `proc-macro = true` under [lib] — which also means they can only export macros, not ordinary structs or functions."},{"q":"Why can't you define a proc macro and use it in the same crate?","options":["It's only a stylistic convention, not enforced","A proc macro must be compiled as its own artifact before the compiler can run it while compiling other code","Proc macros can only be written in a different language","Cargo doesn't allow more than one macro per crate"],"answer":1,"explain":"The compiler runs proc macro code as part of compiling other crates, so the macro crate has to already be built and available — it can't be defined and consumed in a single compilation pass."},{"q":"What does `cargo expand` do?","options":["Adds new dependencies to Cargo.toml automatically","Shows the fully macro-expanded source code, so you can see exactly what a derive or attribute macro generated","Increases your binary size for release builds","Converts macro_rules! macros into proc macros"],"answer":1,"explain":"cargo expand runs your crate through real macro expansion and pretty-prints the result — the fastest way to understand or debug generated code."},{"q":"This fails to compile: `use serde::Serialize; struct User { id: u64 } fn main() { serde_json::to_string(&User { id: 1 }).unwrap(); }`. What's missing?","options":["A semicolon after the struct definition","`#[derive(Serialize)]` above the struct — importing the trait doesn't implement it","serde_json needs to be imported separately","User needs a Debug impl"],"answer":1,"explain":"`use serde::Serialize;` only brings the trait name into scope; it doesn't implement it for User. Only `#[derive(Serialize)]` actually generates that implementation."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the three kinds of procedural macros.","back":"Derive macros (#[derive(X)]), attribute macros (#[tokio::main]), and function-like macros (sqlx::query!(...))."},{"front":"Does a derive macro modify your struct or enum's own definition?","back":"No — it generates separate code (usually a trait impl) alongside your type. The definition you wrote stays exactly as-is."},{"front":"What does #[tokio::main] actually do to an `async fn main`?","back":"It replaces it with a plain fn main() that builds a Tokio runtime and blocks on your async body — Rust has no native async main."},{"front":"What makes sqlx::query! different from a macro_rules! macro that looks the same at the call site?","back":"It's a proc macro that can do real compile-time work — like connecting to a database to check your SQL and column types — something pattern-matching macros can't do."},{"front":"What Cargo.toml setting marks a crate as a proc macro crate?","back":"`proc-macro = true` under [lib]. Such a crate can only export macros, not ordinary items."},{"front":"Why must a proc macro live in a different crate than the one that uses it?","back":"The compiler runs the macro's code while compiling other crates, so the macro crate must already be built as its own artifact first — it can't be defined and used in the same compilation."},{"front":"What's the fastest way to see what a derive macro actually generated?","back":"Run `cargo expand` — it prints the fully expanded, real Rust source."},{"front":"A struct imports `serde::Serialize` but fails `serde_json::to_string` with a 'trait bound not satisfied' error. What's the fix?","back":"Add `#[derive(Serialize)]` above the struct. Importing the trait only lets you refer to it; deriving it is what actually implements it."}]},"smart-pointers":{"topic":"smart-pointers","title":"Smart pointers","quiz":[{"q":"What does `Box<T>` give you?","options":["Shared ownership across many owners","A single-owner value stored on the heap","Thread-safe reference counting","Runtime-checked borrowing"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Box<T>` puts one value on the heap with a single owner. It's needed for large values and for recursive types whose size would otherwise be unknown."},{"q":"What does `Rc::clone(&value)` do?","options":["Makes a deep copy of the underlying data","Adds another owner and bumps the reference count (cheap, no data copy)","Moves the value to a new thread","Frees the value"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Rc::clone` is cheap: it creates another handle pointing at the same data and increments the owner count. The data itself is shared, not duplicated."},{"q":"You need several threads to share ownership of one value. Which do you use?","options":["`Rc<T>`","`Box<T>`","`Arc<T>`","A plain reference `&T`"],"answer":2,"explain":"`Arc<T>` is the thread-safe reference-counted pointer. `Rc<T>` isn't thread-safe and won't compile across threads; use `Arc` when threads are involved."},{"q":"Why is `Box` required for a recursive type like a linked-list node?","options":["Because recursion is not allowed in Rust","A Box is pointer-sized, giving the type a finite, known size the compiler can compute","Because Box makes it run faster","It isn't required; recursive types work without any indirection"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without indirection, a node containing itself would have infinite size. A `Box` is a fixed-size pointer, so the compiler can finally compute the type's size."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"`Box<T>` — what and when?","back":"Single owner, value on the heap. Use for large values and recursive types (like linked lists) where the size must be finite and known."},{"front":"`Rc<T>` — what and when?","back":"Multiple owners of one value, single-threaded. `Rc::clone` bumps an owner count cheaply; the value drops when the last owner goes away."},{"front":"`Rc` vs `Arc`?","back":"Same idea; `Arc` uses a thread-safe (atomic) counter so it can be shared across threads. `Rc` is single-threaded and slightly faster."},{"front":"Can you mutate through `Rc`/`Arc`?","back":"Not directly — they give shared read access. Combine with `RefCell` (single-thread) or `Mutex` (multi-thread) to mutate shared data."}]},"interior-mutability":{"topic":"interior-mutability","title":"Interior mutability","quiz":[{"q":"What is interior mutability?","options":["Turning off the borrow checker entirely","Mutating a value through a shared `&` reference, with the borrow rules checked at runtime","A way to make all fields public","Making a struct thread-safe automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"Interior mutability lets you change data even when you only hold a shared `&`. The type (like RefCell) enforces the borrow rules itself, at runtime instead of compile time."},{"q":"What happens if you hold a `RefCell` read borrow and call `borrow_mut()` at the same time?","options":["It's a compile error","It silently allows both","It panics at runtime ('already borrowed')","It returns None"],"answer":2,"explain":"RefCell enforces 'many readers or one writer' at runtime. Violating it compiles fine but panics with a BorrowMutError when it runs."},{"q":"Which combination gives shared ownership AND mutation in single-threaded code?","options":["`Box<RefCell<T>>`","`Rc<RefCell<T>>`","`Arc<Mutex<T>>`","`Rc<T>` alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Rc` provides multiple owners and `RefCell` provides interior mutability. Together, several owners can each mutate the shared value (single-threaded)."},{"q":"Why can't you use `RefCell` to share mutable state across threads?","options":["It's too slow","It isn't thread-safe (not Sync), so the compiler rejects it; use `Mutex`/`RwLock` instead","It only holds numbers","It requires unsafe code"],"answer":1,"explain":"RefCell's runtime borrow tracking isn't thread-safe, so it can't cross thread boundaries. The thread-safe equivalents are Mutex and RwLock."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does `RefCell<T>` let you do?","back":"Mutate through a shared `&` via `.borrow()` / `.borrow_mut()`, enforcing 'many readers or one writer' at runtime — panicking if you break it."},{"front":"`Cell<T>` vs `RefCell<T>`?","back":"`Cell` is for small `Copy` values via `get`/`set` and never panics; `RefCell` lends references and checks borrows at runtime."},{"front":"The single-thread combo for shared, mutable data?","back":"`Rc<RefCell<T>>` — Rc for multiple owners, RefCell for interior mutation."},{"front":"The multi-thread equivalents?","back":"`Arc` replaces `Rc`, and `Mutex`/`RwLock` replace `RefCell`. So `Arc<Mutex<T>>` is the thread-safe twin of `Rc<RefCell<T>>`."}]},"unsafe-rust":{"topic":"unsafe-rust","title":"Unsafe Rust","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `unsafe` keyword actually do?","options":["Turns off all safety checks for the whole program","Unlocks a small fixed set of extra operations the compiler can't prove safe","Makes the code run faster automatically","Disables the borrow checker everywhere"],"answer":1,"explain":"`unsafe` only unlocks five specific operations (like dereferencing raw pointers and calling unsafe functions). It does NOT disable safety for the rest of your program."},{"q":"Which of these requires an `unsafe` block?","options":["Creating a raw pointer with `&x as *const i32`","Dereferencing a raw pointer with `*p`","Declaring a variable","Calling a normal safe function"],"answer":1,"explain":"Creating a raw pointer is safe (it's just a number). Actually following it with `*p` could read invalid memory, so dereferencing requires `unsafe`."},{"q":"What is the recommended pattern for using unsafe code?","options":["Spread unsafe blocks throughout the codebase for speed","Wrap a small, checked unsafe core inside a safe API so callers never touch unsafe","Mark the whole program `unsafe`","Use unsafe to silence borrow-checker errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The standard library does this everywhere (e.g. Vec): a safe function checks the conditions, then a tiny unsafe block does the dangerous operation. Callers stay in safe Rust."},{"q":"What happens if you break the contract of an unsafe block (e.g. dereference a dangling pointer)?","options":["A clean compile error","Undefined behavior — crashes, corruption, or silent wrong results","The block is skipped automatically","Rust rolls it back safely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unsafe is a promise you make to the compiler. Break it and you get undefined behavior: the exact crashes and corruption Rust normally prevents. There's no partial credit."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Does `unsafe` disable the borrow checker?","back":"No. It only unlocks five operations (raw-pointer deref, unsafe fn calls, mutable statics, unsafe traits, unions). Ordinary ownership rules still apply."},{"front":"The golden pattern for unsafe code?","back":"Wrap a small, checked `unsafe` core inside a safe API — like `Vec` does — so callers never write `unsafe` themselves."},{"front":"What is undefined behavior?","back":"The result of breaking an unsafe promise (dangling pointer, bad FFI call, uninitialized memory): crashes, corruption, or code that works then fails later."},{"front":"How often should application code use `unsafe`?","back":"Almost never. It's mainly for FFI, low-level data structures, or a measured performance need. Prefer a safe alternative whenever one exists."}]},"ffi":{"topic":"ffi","title":"FFI","quiz":[{"q":"Why must every call to a C function declared in an `extern \"C\"` block be inside `unsafe`?","options":["Because C is an older language than Rust","Because Rust cannot verify the foreign function upholds its safety guarantees","Because `unsafe` makes FFI calls run faster","Because the compiler needs `unsafe` to link the program"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust's safety checks stop at the FFI border. The compiler can't verify what C does, so you take responsibility with `unsafe`."},{"q":"What does `#[repr(C)]` on a struct do?","options":["Makes the struct serialize to JSON","Forces C-compatible field layout so C reads the fields at the right offsets","Marks the struct as unsafe to use","Automatically frees the struct's memory"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without `#[repr(C)]`, Rust may reorder or pad fields differently than C, corrupting data across the boundary. `#[repr(C)]` fixes the layout to match C."},{"q":"Why do you add `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` (or `#[no_mangle]`) when exposing a Rust function to C?","options":["To make the function public","To keep the symbol name unscrambled so C can find it by name","To make the function async","To enable serde serialization"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust normally mangles function names into long unique symbols. `no_mangle` keeps the plain name so C's linker can find it."},{"q":"Why can't you pass a Rust `String` directly to a C function expecting a C string?","options":["Rust Strings are encrypted","A Rust String isn't zero-terminated and can contain interior nulls","C strings are always longer","Rust Strings can't leave the stack"],"answer":1,"explain":"C strings end in a `\\0` and Rust `String`s don't. You convert with `CString` (Rust->C) and `CStr` (C->Rust)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does FFI stand for and what is it for?","back":"Foreign Function Interface — calling code in another language (usually C) from Rust, or exposing Rust functions to C."},{"front":"What does `extern \"C\" { ... }` do?","back":"Declares foreign functions (name and signature) so Rust can call them. The \"C\" names the ABI. Calls are always unsafe."},{"front":"What attribute forces C-compatible struct layout, and why is it needed?","back":"`#[repr(C)]`. Without it, Rust may reorder/pad fields differently than C, corrupting data that crosses the boundary."},{"front":"Who frees memory that Rust allocates and hands to C?","back":"There's no borrow checker across FFI — you must decide and document the ownership rule. Freeing on both sides causes double-free crashes."}]},"date-and-time":{"topic":"date-and-time","title":"Dates and time","quiz":[{"q":"Why is `Instant` preferred over `SystemTime` for measuring elapsed time?","options":["Instant is a calendar-aware type","Instant is monotonic and immune to system clock changes; SystemTime can jump","SystemTime doesn't exist in std","Instant returns a Result while SystemTime doesn't"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instant only ever moves forward and ignores clock adjustments, making it the right tool for timing code."},{"q":"What does `.elapsed()` on an `Instant` return?","options":["A SystemTime","A DateTime<Utc>","A Duration","A String"],"answer":2,"explain":"elapsed() gives you a Duration — how much time has passed since the Instant was captured."},{"q":"Why does `SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH)` return a `Result` instead of a bare `Duration`?","options":["Because UNIX_EPOCH might not exist on some platforms","Because a Duration can never be negative, but the wall clock could theoretically be set before the reference time","Because duration_since always panics if called twice","Because Result is required for any function used in main"],"answer":1,"explain":"Duration can't represent negative time, and a real wall clock can go backward relative to a reference point — so the API returns Err instead of pretending."},{"q":"Which crate provides real calendar dates, timezones, and formatting in Rust?","options":["std::time","chrono","regex","rand"],"answer":1,"explain":"std has no calendar type at all — chrono is the ecosystem standard for actual dates, timezones, and formatting/parsing."},{"q":"What's wrong with calling `.format(\"%Y-%m-%d\")` on an `Instant`?","options":["Nothing, it works fine","Instant has no .format method — it's an opaque stopwatch reading with no calendar meaning","It only works on chrono::Local values","\"%Y-%m-%d\" is invalid format syntax everywhere"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instant isn't tied to any calendar date at all, so it has no .format method. Formatting is a chrono DateTime operation."},{"q":"What's the difference between `%m` and `%M` in a chrono format string?","options":["No difference, they're interchangeable","%m is month, %M is minutes","%m is minutes, %M is month","Both mean 'month' in different locales"],"answer":1,"explain":"One letter's case flips the whole field — %m is the month, %M is minutes. Worth double-checking against real output."},{"q":"Why be careful comparing `DateTime<Utc>` and `DateTime<Local>` directly?","options":["They can never be compared — it's a compile error","They represent the same instant differently depending on machine timezone, causing off-by-hours bugs if not converted first","Local time is always computed faster","chrono has deprecated the Local type"],"answer":1,"explain":"Comparing across timezones without normalizing to the same one first is a classic source of subtle bugs."},{"q":"Which type would you reach for to time how long a function call takes?","options":["SystemTime","chrono::Utc","Instant","DateTime<Local>"],"answer":2,"explain":"Instant + .elapsed() is built specifically for measuring durations, immune to wall-clock adjustments."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does Instant::now() + .elapsed() give you?","back":"A monotonic measurement of elapsed time, immune to system clock changes — use it for timing code."},{"front":"What does SystemTime represent?","back":"Real wall-clock time, which can jump if the system clock is adjusted — use it for timestamps, not measuring durations."},{"front":"Why does duration_since return a Result?","back":"Because a Duration can never be negative, and a wall clock can genuinely go backward relative to a reference point."},{"front":"What calendar features does std::time provide?","back":"None — no year, month, day, or timezone. For real calendar dates, use the chrono crate."},{"front":"What does chrono::Utc::now() return?","back":"A DateTime<Utc> — a real calendar timestamp that knows its own timezone."},{"front":"How do you format a chrono DateTime as text?","back":"Call .format(\"%Y-%m-%d\") (or any strftime-style pattern) on it."},{"front":"What's a classic timezone bug to watch for?","back":"Comparing a Utc time to a Local time directly without converting one to match the other first."}]},"regex-and-text-processing":{"topic":"regex-and-text-processing","title":"Regex and text processing","quiz":[{"q":"Does Rust's standard library include regex support?","options":["Yes, in std::regex","No — the regex crate is the ecosystem standard","Yes, but only for ASCII text","Only on the nightly compiler"],"answer":1,"explain":"std has no regex engine at all. The regex crate, backed by a linear-time engine, is what the ecosystem reaches for."},{"q":"Why should you compile a `Regex` once and reuse it, instead of calling `Regex::new` inside a loop?","options":["Regex::new is deprecated","Compiling the pattern is the expensive part; recompiling every iteration wastes that work over and over","Rust doesn't allow Regex::new inside loops","A Regex value can only be used once and then must be discarded"],"answer":1,"explain":"Compiling a pattern into a matching engine costs meaningfully more than running a match — always compile once and reuse."},{"q":"Why does `Regex::new` need a raw string like `r\"\\d{4}\"` instead of `\"\\d{4}\"`?","options":["Raw strings execute faster at runtime","Without r\"\", Rust tries to interpret \\d as its own escape sequence and rejects it at compile time","Regex patterns must be written in all uppercase","It's purely a style convention with no real effect on compilation"],"answer":1,"explain":"\\d isn't a valid Rust string escape, so a plain string literal fails to compile before the regex engine ever sees the pattern. r\"\" makes backslashes literal."},{"q":"What does a named capture group like `(?<year>\\d{4})` let you do?","options":["Nothing extra — it behaves identically to an unnamed group","Pull out the match by name (&caps[\"year\"]) instead of a fragile numeric index","Make the regex engine run measurably faster","Force the match to be case-insensitive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Named groups read better and stay correct if the pattern's groups are later reordered — a numeric index like &caps[1] would silently break."},{"q":"What does `.is_match()` return?","options":["The matched text itself","A bool","A Vec of every match","An Option<Captures>"],"answer":1,"explain":"is_match() only answers yes/no. Use .find() for the matched text or .captures() for its labeled pieces."},{"q":"Why might a regex pattern from Python or JavaScript fail to port directly to Rust's `regex` crate?","options":["Rust's regex crate uses an entirely different syntax for digits and letters","Rust's regex crate deliberately doesn't support backreferences or lookaround, to guarantee linear-time matching","Rust regexes can only match ASCII text","Rust's regex crate requires every pattern to use named groups"],"answer":1,"explain":"Leaving out backreferences/lookaround avoids catastrophic backtracking, guaranteeing linear-time matching — but it means some patterns from other engines need rewriting."},{"q":"Which is the more idiomatic way to check if a path ends with \".rs\"?","options":["Regex::new(r\"\\.rs$\").unwrap().is_match(path)","path.ends_with(\".rs\")","Both are equally idiomatic — pick whichever","Neither approach actually works for this"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fixed suffix check doesn't need a pattern-matching engine — str::ends_with is clearer and needs no dependency."},{"q":"What lets a `Regex` be compiled exactly once and reused everywhere without threading it through every function call?","options":["A static std::sync::LazyLock<Regex> (or compiling once at startup)","Calling regex::Regex::clone() before each use","Creating a fresh Regex::new() call inside each function","Wrapping every call site in an unsafe block"],"answer":0,"explain":"LazyLock builds the Regex the first time it's touched and reuses it for every call after that, avoiding the need to pass it around manually."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Does std have regex support?","back":"No — the regex crate is the ecosystem standard, built on a linear-time (non-backtracking) engine."},{"front":"What's the expensive part of using a Regex?","back":"Compiling the pattern with Regex::new — always compile once and reuse the same Regex, never per-call or per-loop-iteration."},{"front":"Why do regex patterns need a raw string (r\"...\")?","back":"So backslashes reach the regex engine literally instead of being interpreted as Rust's own string escapes first."},{"front":"What does .captures() with a named group let you do?","back":"Extract a match by name, e.g. &caps[\"year\"], instead of a fragile numeric index like &caps[1]."},{"front":"What does the regex crate deliberately not support, and why?","back":"Backreferences and lookaround — leaving them out guarantees linear-time matching with no catastrophic backtracking."},{"front":"When should you reach for a plain str method instead of regex?","back":"When you're matching a fixed substring or doing a simple split — split_once, trim, starts_with, contains are clearer and need no dependency."},{"front":"How can you compile a Regex once and share it across your whole program?","back":"Store it in a static std::sync::LazyLock<Regex>, which builds it lazily on first use and reuses it after that."}]},"random-numbers":{"topic":"random-numbers","title":"Random numbers","quiz":[{"q":"Does Rust's standard library include a random number generator?","options":["Yes, std::random","No — the rand crate is the ecosystem standard","Yes, but only for integers, not floats","Only on the nightly compiler"],"answer":1,"explain":"Generating good randomness is its own algorithmic problem, so it lives in the rand crate rather than std."},{"q":"What does `..=` do in `rng.random_range(1..=6)`?","options":["It's required syntax with no special meaning","It makes the range inclusive, so 6 is actually a possible result (like a real die)","It marks the range as 'random' to the compiler","It excludes the first value from being generated"],"answer":1,"explain":"A plain 1..6 would only ever produce 1 through 5. The inclusive ..= is what makes 6 reachable."},{"q":"Why does calling `.choose()` on a slice fail with 'no method named `choose` found' if you've only written `use rand::Rng;`?","options":["choose() doesn't exist anywhere in the rand crate",".choose() lives on the rand::seq::SliceRandom trait, which must be imported separately","You must call rand::rng() before importing anything","Slices in Rust can never be randomized"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rng and SliceRandom are two different traits for two different jobs — generating numbers vs. doing something random with a collection."},{"q":"What does `slice.choose(&mut rng)` return?","options":["T, by value","&T directly, panicking if the slice is empty","Option<&T> — None if the slice is empty","Result<&T, Error>"],"answer":2,"explain":"It returns an Option because an empty slice genuinely has nothing to pick — you must handle the None case."},{"q":"Why would you use `StdRng::seed_from_u64(42)` instead of `rand::rng()`?","options":["It's faster to generate numbers with","It's the only RNG type that supports random_range","It produces the exact same sequence every run — useful for tests and simulations","It's required before you can call .shuffle()"],"answer":2,"explain":"A seeded RNG is fully reproducible, which is exactly what you want when a test or simulation needs to be deterministic."},{"q":"Which trait must be in scope to call `.shuffle()` on a `Vec`?","options":["rand::Rng","rand::seq::SliceRandom","std::cmp::Ord","serde::Serialize"],"answer":1,"explain":"shuffle() and choose() both live on SliceRandom, a trait separate from Rng."},{"q":"Why shouldn't you assume the default `rand::rng()` is safe for generating security tokens?","options":["It's too slow for that kind of use case","Its default algorithm is built for speed and statistical quality, not guaranteed unpredictability against an attacker","It can only generate numbers between 0 and 100","rand doesn't support any security-related use case, period"],"answer":1,"explain":"Statistical randomness and cryptographic unpredictability are different properties — check rand's docs before using it for tokens or secrets."},{"q":"What risk does a test built directly on `rand::rng()` (the default thread RNG) carry?","options":["It will fail to compile inside a #[test] function","Flaky results, since different runs can draw different random values","It always runs noticeably slower than other tests","It can never be used alongside assert! macros"],"answer":1,"explain":"The default RNG is intentionally unpredictable — great for real use, bad for a test that needs to behave the same way every run."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Does std have a random number generator?","back":"No — the rand crate is the ecosystem standard."},{"front":"How do you get a random number from 1 to 6, inclusive?","back":"rng.random_range(1..=6) — the ..= makes the range inclusive so 6 is actually reachable."},{"front":"What trait do .choose() and .shuffle() require in scope?","back":"rand::seq::SliceRandom — without importing it, those methods don't exist on a slice."},{"front":"What does slice.choose(&mut rng) return, and why?","back":"Option<&T> — None when the slice is empty, since there's nothing to pick."},{"front":"How do you get a reproducible sequence of 'random' numbers?","back":"Seed a specific generator, e.g. StdRng::seed_from_u64(42), instead of using the default rand::rng()."},{"front":"Is the default rand::rng() safe for generating security tokens?","back":"Not guaranteed — check rand's docs for a CSPRNG before using randomness for tokens, session ids, or secrets."},{"front":"Why seed an RNG inside a test?","back":"So the test's random input is identical every run, avoiding flakiness from whatever values the default RNG happened to draw."}]},"serde-and-json":{"topic":"serde-and-json","title":"Serde and JSON","quiz":[{"q":"What do the two halves of the name 'Serde' mean?","options":["Server and Debug","Serialize (Rust -> text) and Deserialize (text -> Rust)","Serial and Decode","Secure and Decrypt"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serde = Serialize + Deserialize: turning Rust values into text/bytes and reading them back into Rust types."},{"q":"What does `#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]` above a struct do?","options":["Marks the struct as unsafe","Generates the conversion code between the struct and formats like JSON at compile time","Sends the struct over the network","Makes all fields public"],"answer":1,"explain":"The derive generates all the serialize/deserialize code for you at compile time, based on the struct's fields."},{"q":"Why does `serde_json::from_str` return a `Result`?","options":["Because JSON is always invalid","Because deserializing untrusted input can fail (missing fields, wrong types, malformed text)","Because Result is required for all functions","Because it needs to return two values"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deserializing reads outside data you don't control. Bad input becomes an Err you can handle instead of a crash."},{"q":"The JSON sends a key `displayName` but your Rust field is `display_name`. What fixes the mismatch?","options":["Rename your Rust field to displayName","Add `#[serde(rename = \"displayName\")]` above the field","Nothing — serde ignores case","Wrap the field in Result"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serde matches by field name. `#[serde(rename = ...)]` (or `rename_all`) maps between the JSON key and your Rust field name."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which two crates do you add for JSON with serde, and what feature does serde need?","back":"`serde` (with the `derive` feature) and `serde_json`. e.g. `cargo add serde --features derive` then `cargo add serde_json`."},{"front":"How do you convert a struct to a JSON string and back?","back":"`serde_json::to_string(&value)` -> JSON String; `serde_json::from_str(text)` -> your type. Both return a Result."},{"front":"How do you model a JSON field that may be missing?","back":"Use `Option<T>`. Present -> Some(value), absent -> None, instead of a deserialization error."},{"front":"What does `#[serde(rename = \"...\")]` do?","back":"Maps a JSON key name to a differently-named Rust field, so your Rust can stay snake_case while matching the wire format."}]},"http-clients-reqwest":{"topic":"http-clients-reqwest","title":"HTTP clients with reqwest","quiz":[{"q":"Why should you build a reqwest::Client once and reuse it, rather than creating one per request?","options":["It's required by the compiler","Client pools connections internally, so reusing it avoids repeating expensive connection setup","A new Client always fails to compile","It automatically retries failed requests"],"answer":1,"explain":"Client keeps connections open/pooled internally; building a new one per request throws that pool away and repeats DNS/TLS setup costs."},{"q":"What does `.send().await?` return for a request whose server responds 404 Not Found?","options":["Always an Err, because 404 means failure","Ok(Response) — a 404 is still a valid HTTP response as far as reqwest is concerned","It panics","None"],"answer":1,"explain":"reqwest only treats network-level failures as Err from send(); a non-2xx status code is still a successful HTTP exchange until you check it."},{"q":"How do you turn a non-2xx response into an Err you can propagate with `?`?","options":["Call `.unwrap()` on the response","Call `.error_for_status()` on the response","It happens automatically","Check `if response == 200`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.error_for_status()` converts a 4xx/5xx response into an Err(...) so `?` can bubble it up like any other failure."},{"q":"What does `.json::<T>().await?` do?","options":["Prints the response as JSON","Reads the response body and deserializes it into your type T via serde","Converts T into JSON text","Validates that the URL is JSON-compatible"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.json::<T>()` reads the body text and uses serde's Deserialize to build a T from it, returning a Result since parsing can fail."},{"q":"Why should you set a timeout on your reqwest client or request?","options":["It's required for `.json()` to work","Without one, a slow or unresponsive server can make your request hang indefinitely","It makes requests faster","It's only needed for POST requests"],"answer":1,"explain":"With no timeout, a request has no upper bound on how long it waits for a response — a stalled server can hang your program forever."},{"q":"What's cheap about cloning a `reqwest::Client`?","options":["Nothing, cloning creates all-new connections","Client is internally an Arc, so cloning shares the same connection pool","Cloning a Client is not allowed","It resets the timeout"],"answer":1,"explain":"reqwest::Client wraps its internals in an Arc, so .clone() just hands out another handle to the same pool rather than duplicating it."},{"q":"In `client.get(url).send().await?.json::<Repo>().await?`, why might `.json::<Repo>()` fail even though `send()` succeeded?","options":["It never fails once send() succeeds","The response body might not match Repo's shape (e.g. it's an error JSON, not the expected struct)","json() always fails for GET requests","Only POST requests can be parsed as JSON"],"answer":1,"explain":"A successful send() just means you got an HTTP response — if that response is an error body (e.g. 404 JSON), deserializing into your success struct fails on shape mismatch."},{"q":"Which crate feature do you need on `reqwest` to use `.json()` for deserializing responses?","options":["\"blocking\"","\"json\"","\"cookies\"","No feature needed, it's built in unconditionally"],"answer":1,"explain":"reqwest's JSON support (serializing/deserializing bodies) is gated behind the \"json\" feature: `cargo add reqwest --features json`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why build one reqwest::Client instead of one per request?","back":"A Client pools connections internally; reusing it avoids repeating DNS lookups and TLS handshakes on every call. A new Client per request throws that pooling away."},{"front":"Does reqwest treat a 404 or 500 response as an Err automatically?","back":"No. .send().await only errors on network failures. A non-2xx status is still Ok(Response) until you check .status() or call .error_for_status()."},{"front":"How do you convert a non-2xx response into a propagatable Err?","back":"Call .error_for_status() on the Response — it turns 4xx/5xx into an Err so `?` can bubble it up like any other failure."},{"front":"What does .json::<T>().await do?","back":"Reads the response body and deserializes it into T via serde, returning a Result since the body might not match T's shape."},{"front":"How do you set a timeout for reqwest requests?","back":"`reqwest::Client::builder().timeout(Duration::from_secs(10)).build()` — applies to every request made through that client (or set it per-request)."},{"front":"Is reqwest::Client expensive to clone?","back":"No — it's an Arc internally, so .clone() shares the same connection pool rather than creating new connections."},{"front":"What crates/features do you typically need for a reqwest + serde JSON setup?","back":"`reqwest` with feature \"json\", `serde` with feature \"derive\", and an async runtime like `tokio` with feature \"full\"."}]},"databases-and-sqlx":{"topic":"databases-and-sqlx","title":"Databases with sqlx","quiz":[{"q":"What's the key difference between sqlx and a traditional ORM?","options":["sqlx only supports SQLite","sqlx has you write real SQL, which it checks against your schema, instead of hiding SQL behind method chains","sqlx doesn't support async","ORMs are always faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unlike ORMs that build queries from method calls, sqlx has you write actual SQL strings and verifies them against your real database."},{"q":"What do sqlx's `query!`/`query_as!` macros check, and when?","options":["Nothing — they're identical to query()/query_as()","They connect to a live database (or offline cache) at compile time to verify SQL and infer result types","They check your SQL at runtime only, like `query()`","They validate your Cargo.toml"],"answer":1,"explain":"The macro versions connect to a real database (via DATABASE_URL) or a saved .sqlx cache during `cargo build`, catching typos and type mismatches as compile errors."},{"q":"Why create one PgPool at startup instead of connecting per query?","options":["PgPool doesn't support reconnecting","Opening a connection is expensive, and per-query connections can exhaust the database's connection limit under load","It's required by the Rust compiler","PgPool can only be created once per program by design"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each new connection costs a TCP handshake and auth round trip; sharing a pool avoids repeating that cost and avoids exhausting the database's max connections."},{"q":"What is the purpose of `$1` in a sqlx query string?","options":["It's a comment marker","A bound parameter placeholder — the value is sent to the database separately from the SQL text via .bind()","It denotes the first row returned","It's Postgres syntax for a wildcard"],"answer":1,"explain":"`$1`, `$2`, ... are placeholders filled in via .bind(value); the driver sends the query text and values as separate pieces, which is what prevents SQL injection."},{"q":"What's wrong with `format!(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '{}'\", name)` used as a query?","options":["Nothing, it's a normal way to build SQL","It's a SQL injection vulnerability — untrusted input becomes part of the SQL syntax itself","format! doesn't work with SQL strings","It's slower than using query!"],"answer":1,"explain":"Formatting user input directly into SQL text lets that input escape the intended string and rewrite the query's logic — the classic SQL injection hole that bound parameters prevent."},{"q":"Is `PgPool` expensive to `.clone()`?","options":["Yes, cloning opens a whole new set of connections","No, PgPool is a handle to the shared pool — cloning it doesn't open new connections","PgPool cannot be cloned","Cloning it closes the original pool"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cloning PgPool just hands out another reference to the same underlying pool of connections, not a new pool."},{"q":"Why might a CI build using sqlx's `query!` macro fail with a \"DATABASE_URL not set\" error even though the SQL is correct?","options":["query! always requires general internet access","query!/query_as! need a live database (or offline cache) available at compile time to verify the SQL, and CI often forgets to provide one","The SQL syntax must be wrong","CI never supports sqlx"],"answer":1,"explain":"The compile-time-checked macros need to reach a real database or a saved .sqlx offline cache while building — a CI pipeline that skips this setup fails even on correct SQL."},{"q":"What does `#[derive(sqlx::FromRow)]` on a struct do?","options":["Marks the struct as unsafe","Lets sqlx map a database row's columns onto the struct's fields automatically","Converts the struct into SQL DDL","Adds a primary key to the struct"],"answer":1,"explain":"FromRow generates the code that reads a returned row's columns into the struct's fields, used by functions like query_as::<_, User>(...)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Is sqlx an ORM?","back":"No — sqlx is an async SQL toolkit. You write real SQL strings yourself; sqlx checks them against your schema instead of generating queries from method calls."},{"front":"What do query!/query_as! check at compile time?","back":"They connect to a live database (via DATABASE_URL) or a saved offline cache to verify the SQL is valid and infer the result's column types."},{"front":"Where should a PgPool be created?","back":"Once, at application startup — then shared (via reference or Arc) across the whole app, instead of opening a new connection per query."},{"front":"What does $1 mean in a sqlx query?","back":"A bound parameter placeholder. The value is passed via .bind(...) and sent to the database separately from the SQL text — this is what prevents SQL injection."},{"front":"Why is format!()-ing values into SQL dangerous?","back":"It lets untrusted input become part of the SQL syntax itself (SQL injection) — e.g. a value like `' OR '1'='1` can rewrite the query's logic."},{"front":"Is PgPool cheap to clone?","back":"Yes — cloning it shares a handle to the same pool of connections rather than opening new ones."},{"front":"What's a common CI pitfall with sqlx's compile-time-checked macros?","back":"They need a live database or a saved .sqlx offline cache reachable during `cargo build` — CI setups that skip this fail to build even with correct SQL."}]},"cli-apps":{"topic":"cli-apps","title":"CLI apps","quiz":[{"q":"In `std::env::args()`, what is the first element (`args[0]`)?","options":["The first flag the user passed","The program's own name","Always an empty string","The current directory"],"answer":1,"explain":"args[0] is the program name. The first real user argument is args[1]."},{"q":"Why prefer `args.get(1)` over `args[1]` to read the first argument?","options":["It's faster","`args[1]` panics if the argument is missing, while `.get(1)` returns an Option you can handle","`.get(1)` returns a reference and `args[1]` doesn't","There is no difference"],"answer":1,"explain":"Indexing out of range panics. `.get(1)` gives Some/None so a missing argument becomes a case you handle, not a crash."},{"q":"Where should a CLI print error and status messages?","options":["stdout, using println!","stderr, using eprintln!","A log file only","It doesn't matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"stdout is for the real result (so it can be piped/redirected cleanly); errors and status go to stderr via eprintln!."},{"q":"What exit code should a CLI return when it fails?","options":["0","Any non-zero value","Always 200","It can't set an exit code"],"answer":1,"explain":"0 means success; non-zero means failure. Scripts rely on this, e.g. `myapp && echo ok` only runs if myapp exited 0."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you read command-line arguments with just the standard library?","back":"`let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();` — args[0] is the program name, real args start at args[1]."},{"front":"stdout vs stderr — what goes where in a CLI?","back":"stdout (println!) = the real result/data. stderr (eprintln!) = status, warnings, errors. Keep them separate so piping works."},{"front":"How does a program report success or failure to the shell?","back":"Its exit code: 0 = success, non-zero = failure. Set it with std::process::exit(code) or return Result from main."},{"front":"When should you use the `clap` crate?","back":"For anything beyond one or two arguments. clap parses flags/options and generates --help, --version, and error messages for you."}]},"logging-and-tracing":{"topic":"logging-and-tracing","title":"Logging and tracing","quiz":[{"q":"You call `tracing::info!` everywhere but nothing prints. What's the most likely cause?","options":["You need to restart your computer","No subscriber is installed — the macros emit events but nobody is listening","info! only works in release builds","You must call info! twice"],"answer":1,"explain":"In tracing, macros only emit events. A separate subscriber (e.g. tracing_subscriber::fmt::init()) must be installed once at startup or nothing prints."},{"q":"Which list shows log levels from least to most severe?","options":["error, warn, info, debug, trace","trace, debug, info, warn, error","info, trace, warn, debug, error","debug, info, warn, error, trace"],"answer":1,"explain":"From noisiest/least severe to most severe: trace, debug, info, warn, error. You set a threshold and everything below it is dropped."},{"q":"Why prefer `info!(user_id = id, \"logged in\")` over `info!(\"user {id} logged in\")`?","options":["It's shorter to type","The value becomes a searchable structured field instead of unsearchable text","String formatting is not allowed in tracing","It changes the log level"],"answer":1,"explain":"Structured fields are labeled key/values that log tools can filter on (e.g. user_id = 7). Values glued into the message are just text."},{"q":"What does a tracing `span` give you that a single `info!` event does not?","options":["Colored output","A duration of work whose context tags every log inside it (e.g. request_id)","Automatic error handling","Faster logging"],"answer":1,"explain":"Events are single points; a span wraps a chunk of work so every log inside it carries shared context, letting you follow one request end to end."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"In the `tracing` crate, what actually decides where log events go?","back":"A subscriber. The info!/warn!/error! macros only emit events; you must install a subscriber (e.g. tracing_subscriber::fmt::init()) once at startup or nothing prints."},{"front":"Name the five log levels from least to most severe.","back":"trace, debug, info, warn, error. Set a threshold; everything below it is dropped."},{"front":"What is a structured field in tracing?","back":"A labeled key/value attached to a log event, e.g. info!(user_id = 7, \"...\"), that log tools can search and filter on — unlike text buried in the message."},{"front":"What is a span, and how does it differ from an event?","back":"A span represents a duration of work; every log inside it is tagged with the span's context (like request_id). Events are single points in time."}]},"cfg-and-feature-flags":{"topic":"cfg-and-feature-flags","title":"cfg and Cargo feature flags","quiz":[{"q":"What happens to code under a `#[cfg(...)]` attribute whose condition is false for this build?","options":["It runs but is wrapped in an implicit `if false`","It is not compiled at all — as if it were deleted","It compiles but prints a warning every time it runs","It runs only in debug builds"],"answer":1,"explain":"`#[cfg(...)]` is evaluated at compile time. A false condition means the item is never compiled in, not skipped at runtime."},{"q":"Which `#[cfg(...)]` condition is true only when compiling with `cargo test`?","options":["#[cfg(debug_assertions)]","#[cfg(test)]","#[cfg(target_os = \"test\")]","#[cfg(feature = \"test\")]"],"answer":1,"explain":"`#[cfg(test)]` is true specifically when the crate is being compiled for testing, keeping test-only code out of normal binaries."},{"q":"You write `#[cfg(feature = \"json\")]` in your code but never add `json` to `[features]` in Cargo.toml. What happens?","options":["Cargo automatically declares the feature for you","It's a compile error immediately","The code is silently excluded forever, since the feature can never be turned on, and modern Cargo warns about it","The feature defaults to enabled"],"answer":2,"explain":"An undeclared feature can never be enabled, so the gated code is permanently compiled out. Modern Cargo also emits an `unexpected cfg condition value` warning."},{"q":"What does `optional = true` on a Cargo dependency do?","options":["Makes the dependency's version requirement optional","Prevents the dependency from being compiled in unless something enables it (typically via a matching feature)","Downloads the dependency lazily at runtime","Makes the dependency's API calls return Option instead of the real type"],"answer":1,"explain":"`optional = true` means the dependency is only compiled in when a feature (usually `dep:name` in a `[features]` entry) turns it on — otherwise it's left out entirely."},{"q":"In `[features] json = [\"dep:serde_json\"]`, what does the `dep:` prefix accomplish that plain `\"serde_json\"` would not?","options":["It enables the serde_json dependency without also creating a public feature literally named serde_json","It marks serde_json as a dev-dependency","It pins serde_json to a specific version","It has no effect — dep: and no prefix are identical"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without `dep:`, Cargo also creates an implicit public feature named after the dependency itself, which is usually not intended. `dep:name` enables the dependency directly."},{"q":"Two crates in the same dependency graph both depend on `logging-lib`. One enables its `colors` feature; the other doesn't request it either way. What happens in the final build?","options":["Each crate gets its own separately-compiled copy of logging-lib, with different features","The build fails because of a feature conflict","logging-lib is compiled once with `colors` enabled, since features are additive/unioned across the whole graph","colors is enabled only for the crate that requested it"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cargo builds each dependency once per build with the union of every feature any crate in the graph requested — features are additive, not per-crate."},{"q":"Why should a Cargo feature only ever add capability, never change existing behavior in a conflicting way?","options":["Because Cargo compiles a separate copy of the crate for every feature combination","Because features are unioned across the whole dependency graph, so one crate's needs can't be isolated from another's in the same build","Because features are resolved at runtime, not compile time","Because rustc rejects any feature that changes behavior"],"answer":1,"explain":"Since features unify per build rather than per crate, a feature that changes behavior (instead of only adding to it) can silently affect a completely different crate that happens to share the same dependency."},{"q":"What's a risk of never testing your crate with `--no-default-features`?","options":["The crate won't compile at all without default features","Code behind non-default features can silently rot, since it's never actually exercised","Cargo will refuse to publish the crate","It has no real risk — default features are always identical to no features"],"answer":1,"explain":"If your own builds always include the default features, feature-gated code paths outside that default combination can break without anyone noticing until a user hits them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does #[cfg(...)] do, conceptually?","back":"It's an if-statement the compiler evaluates before your program exists. If the condition is false, the item is not compiled in at all — it's gone, not just skipped."},{"front":"Name three common #[cfg(...)] conditions besides feature checks.","back":"#[cfg(target_os = \"linux\")] (or macos/windows), #[cfg(test)] (true only under cargo test), and #[cfg(debug_assertions)] (true in debug builds, false in --release)."},{"front":"How do you declare a Cargo feature, and how do you check it in code?","back":"Declare it in Cargo.toml under [features], e.g. `json = []`. Check it in code with `#[cfg(feature = \"json\")]`."},{"front":"How do you make a dependency opt-in behind a feature?","back":"Mark the dependency `optional = true`, then add a feature that enables it via `dep:` syntax, e.g. `json = [\"dep:serde_json\"]`."},{"front":"What does the `dep:` prefix in a feature's dependency list do?","back":"It enables the named optional dependency without also implicitly creating a separate public feature with that same name."},{"front":"Are Cargo features additive or exclusive across a dependency graph?","back":"Additive. Each dependency is compiled once per build with the union of every feature any crate in the graph enabled — there's no per-crate isolation."},{"front":"Why must a feature never change existing behavior in a conflicting way?","back":"Because features are unioned across the whole build, a behavior-changing feature turned on by one crate would silently affect every other crate sharing that dependency in the same build."},{"front":"What warning does modern Cargo give for an undeclared feature checked with #[cfg(feature = \"...\")]?","back":"`unexpected cfg condition value` — and the gated code is permanently excluded, since a feature that was never declared can never be turned on."}]},"build-scripts":{"topic":"build-scripts","title":"Build scripts (build.rs)","quiz":[{"q":"How does Cargo know to run a build script?","options":["You register it in [build-dependencies] in Cargo.toml","It automatically compiles and runs any file named build.rs at the crate root before the rest of the build","You pass --build-script build.rs to cargo build","You call it manually from main.rs before any other code"],"answer":1,"explain":"A build.rs file at the crate root (next to Cargo.toml) is found and run automatically by Cargo before the main build — no explicit wiring is needed."},{"q":"How does a build script communicate instructions back to Cargo?","options":["By writing to a file named cargo.json","By printing specially-formatted lines to stdout, like cargo::rerun-if-changed=...","By returning a special exit code for each instruction","By setting environment variables before it exits"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cargo parses the build script's stdout for lines starting with the cargo:: (or legacy cargo:) prefix, treating each one as an instruction."},{"q":"What is `cargo::rerun-if-changed=PATH` for?","options":["It deletes PATH before the build script runs","It tells Cargo to only re-run the build script when PATH changes, instead of on every build","It copies PATH into OUT_DIR","It sets PATH as an environment variable for the main crate"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without it, Cargo's default heuristics may re-run (or fail to re-run) the script incorrectly; declaring the exact file(s) it depends on keeps rebuilds correct and fast."},{"q":"A build script does `println!(\"cargo::rustc-env=GIT_HASH={}\", hash)`. How does the main crate read that value at compile time?","options":["std::env::var(\"GIT_HASH\") at runtime","env!(\"GIT_HASH\") — resolved at compile time and baked into the binary","It's automatically available as a global constant named GIT_HASH","include!(\"GIT_HASH\")"],"answer":1,"explain":"cargo::rustc-env sets a compile-time environment variable; env!(\"KEY\") in the crate's own code reads it and bakes the value directly into the binary."},{"q":"Why does Cargo only parse a build script's stdout, and not stderr, for instructions?","options":["stderr is reserved for compiler errors only","That's just the convention — printing an instruction via eprintln! is silently ignored, shown only as plain diagnostic text","stderr output isn't captured by Cargo at all","Instructions can be printed to either stream interchangeably"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only stdout lines are parsed as Cargo instructions. Anything sent to stderr is just displayed as ordinary text (e.g. with -vv), never acted on."},{"q":"When cross-compiling, on which machine does build.rs itself actually run?","options":["The target platform being compiled for","The host machine doing the compiling — never the target","Both, once for each","Neither — it runs inside a sandboxed emulator of the target"],"answer":1,"explain":"build.rs is always compiled for, and executed on, the host machine, even when the crate itself is being cross-compiled for a different target."},{"q":"Name one common real-world use of a build script.","options":["Running the crate's unit tests","Generating Rust code from a schema (e.g. protobuf), compiling a bundled C library, or embedding metadata like a git commit hash","Publishing the crate to crates.io","Formatting the crate's source code"],"answer":1,"explain":"Typical build.rs uses include codegen from an external schema, compiling/linking native C code (often via the `cc` crate), and stamping in build-time metadata."},{"q":"Why is a slow or network-dependent build script a real problem?","options":["It only affects the very first build, never subsequent ones","It makes every single `cargo build` slower and less reproducible, since it runs before the crate compiles","Cargo automatically caches its result forever regardless of changes","It has no real downside since it runs in parallel with compilation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The build script runs before compilation on every build that triggers it, so anything slow or flaky inside it (like a network call) taxes every build and can break offline/reproducible builds."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Where does a build script live, and how does Cargo find it?","back":"A file named build.rs at the crate root, next to Cargo.toml (not inside src/). Cargo automatically compiles and runs it before the rest of the crate builds — no configuration needed."},{"front":"How does a build script send instructions to Cargo?","back":"By printing specially-formatted lines to stdout, such as `cargo::rerun-if-changed=...` or `cargo::rustc-env=KEY=VALUE`. Cargo parses stdout for these; stderr is just shown as plain text."},{"front":"What does cargo::rerun-if-changed=PATH do, and why does it matter?","back":"It tells Cargo to only re-run the build script when PATH changes. Omitting it risks Cargo not noticing a dependency changed and reusing stale generated output."},{"front":"How do you get a build-script-set value into your crate's compiled code?","back":"The build script prints `cargo::rustc-env=KEY=VALUE`; the crate's own code reads it at compile time with `env!(\"KEY\")`, which bakes the value into the binary."},{"front":"On which machine does build.rs run when cross-compiling?","back":"Always the host machine doing the compiling, never the target. Reading cfg!(target_os = ...) inside build.rs reflects the host, not the target you're building for."},{"front":"List three common real uses of a build script.","back":"Generating Rust code from a schema (e.g. protobuf/GraphQL), compiling and linking a bundled C library (often via the `cc` crate), and embedding build metadata like a git commit hash."},{"front":"What's the downside of a slow or network-dependent build script?","back":"It runs before every triggered `cargo build`, so it slows down every build and can make builds flaky or non-reproducible if it depends on network access."},{"front":"What happens if a build script panics because required input is missing?","back":"The entire build aborts. A bare .unwrap() gives whoever hits it an unhelpful backtrace instead of a clear explanation of what was missing and why."}]},"docs-and-rustfmt":{"topic":"docs-and-rustfmt","title":"Docs and rustfmt","quiz":[{"q":"Which comment style becomes documentation that `cargo doc` collects?","options":["// two slashes","/// three slashes (a doc comment)","/* block comment */","# hash comment"],"answer":1,"explain":"`///` is a doc comment attached to the item below it, which cargo doc turns into documentation. `//` is a normal comment and is ignored by cargo doc."},{"q":"What does `cargo fmt` do?","options":["Runs your tests","Reformats your code to the standard Rust style automatically","Publishes your crate","Checks for logic bugs"],"answer":1,"explain":"cargo fmt rewrites your code in the official style (spacing, indentation, wrapping) so everyone's code looks the same. It's not about logic."},{"q":"What happens to code examples written inside doc comments when you run `cargo test`?","options":["They are ignored","They actually run as doctests and their assertions are checked","They are deleted","They cause a compile error"],"answer":1,"explain":"Code blocks in doc comments are doctests: cargo test runs them and checks their assertions, so documentation examples can't silently go stale."},{"q":"What does the `//!` comment style document?","options":["The item directly below it","The enclosing module or file it sits inside","Nothing — it's a syntax error","Only test functions"],"answer":1,"explain":"`//!` documents the module/file it's inside (put at the top of a file), while `///` documents the item that follows it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the difference between `//` and `///`?","back":"`//` is a normal comment (ignored by cargo doc). `///` is a doc comment that cargo doc turns into documentation for the item below it."},{"front":"What does `cargo fmt` do, and why use it?","back":"Reformats your whole project to the standard Rust style automatically. It ends style debates and keeps version-control diffs about real changes."},{"front":"What are doctests?","back":"Code examples inside doc comments. `cargo test` runs them and checks their assertions, so docs stay correct."},{"front":"How do you generate and view your project's docs?","back":"`cargo doc --open` builds a browsable HTML site for your crate and dependencies and opens it in your browser."}]},"workspaces-and-crates":{"topic":"workspaces-and-crates","title":"Workspaces and crates","quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between a binary crate and a library crate?","options":["A binary crate is faster","A binary crate has a `main` function and runs; a library crate has no `main` and is used by other crates","A library crate can't have dependencies","There is no difference"],"answer":1,"explain":"A binary crate produces a runnable program (has main). A library crate produces code for other crates to use (no main), like serde or axum."},{"q":"What do all crates in a Cargo workspace share?","options":["One main function","One Cargo.lock and one target/ build folder","The same source file","Nothing"],"answer":1,"explain":"A workspace shares a single Cargo.lock (so everyone uses the same dependency versions) and one target/ directory (so dependencies compile once)."},{"q":"What should a pure workspace-root `Cargo.toml` contain?","options":["A `[package]` section describing the program","A `[workspace]` section with a `members` list and (usually) no `[package]`","Only `[dependencies]`","A `main` function"],"answer":1,"explain":"The workspace root coordinates crates; it has `[workspace]` with members and no `[package]`. Each member has its own [package] Cargo.toml."},{"q":"How do you depend on a local crate in the same workspace?","options":["You can't — only crates.io crates work","Point at its folder: `core = { path = \"../core\" }`","Copy its source into your crate","List it under [workspace] only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cargo looks up crates.io by default, so a local crate needs an explicit path. You can then use its `pub` items."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a crate, and what are the two kinds?","back":"A crate is one Rust compile unit. A binary crate has `main` and runs; a library crate has no main and is used by other crates."},{"front":"What is a workspace and what does it share?","back":"A workspace groups multiple crates under one root. They share one Cargo.lock (same dependency versions) and one target/ folder (compile once)."},{"front":"What goes in a pure workspace-root Cargo.toml?","back":"A `[workspace]` section with a `members` list and (usually) no `[package]`. Each member crate has its own [package] Cargo.toml."},{"front":"How do you depend on another crate in the same workspace?","back":"Give it a path: `other = { path = \"../other\" }`. You can only use its `pub` items — the compiler enforces the boundary."}]},"clippy-and-formatting":{"topic":"clippy-and-formatting","title":"Clippy and formatting","quiz":[{"q":"How is clippy different from the compiler?","options":["Clippy is faster than the compiler","The compiler stops code that's wrong; clippy suggests how to improve code that already compiles","Clippy replaces the compiler","Clippy only checks formatting"],"answer":1,"explain":"The compiler answers 'does this work?'. Clippy answers 'is this the idiomatic way?' — it lints code that already compiles fine."},{"q":"Which clippy suggestion is correct?","options":["`x.is_empty()` should become `x.len() == 0`","`x.len() == 0` should become `x.is_empty()`","`for x in &v` should become an index loop","`if flag` should become `if flag == true`"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clippy prefers `.is_empty()` over `.len() == 0` — it's clearer and can be faster. The other options are backwards."},{"q":"What jobs do `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy` handle, respectively?","options":["Both do the same thing","cargo fmt handles layout/spacing; cargo clippy handles style and logic smells","cargo fmt runs tests; cargo clippy compiles","cargo fmt lints; cargo clippy formats"],"answer":1,"explain":"They don't overlap: cargo fmt makes code look standard (spacing, indentation); cargo clippy makes it read better (idiomatic patterns)."},{"q":"You disagree with one specific clippy lint in one place. What's the right move?","options":["Stop using clippy entirely","Understand why it fired, then silence just that lint with `#[allow(clippy::lint_name)]` if it truly doesn't fit","Delete the code","Ignore all warnings forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clippy is usually right, but occasionally a lint doesn't fit. Understand it first, then use #[allow(...)] deliberately for that spot only."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does clippy do that the compiler doesn't?","back":"It lints code that already compiles, suggesting more idiomatic/clearer/faster versions — often with the exact fix. The compiler only stops wrong code."},{"front":"What do `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy` each handle?","back":"cargo fmt = layout/spacing/indentation (how code looks). cargo clippy = style and logic smells (how code reads). Different, non-overlapping jobs."},{"front":"Give two classic clippy catches.","back":"`x.len() == 0` -> `x.is_empty()`; `if flag == true` -> `if flag`; manual index loop -> `for item in &vec`."},{"front":"How do you silence a single clippy lint you intentionally disagree with?","back":"`#[allow(clippy::lint_name)]` on the item. Do it deliberately after understanding why the lint fired."}]},"web-services":{"topic":"web-services","title":"Web services","quiz":[{"q":"In an Axum web server, what is a handler?","options":["A configuration file","An async function whose return value becomes the HTTP response","A database table","A special kind of macro"],"answer":1,"explain":"A handler is an async fn mapped to a route. You return data (like &str or Json<T>) and the framework turns it into the HTTP response."},{"q":"Why do web handlers use `async`?","options":["To make the code shorter","So a handler can pause on slow work (like a database call) while the server serves other requests","Because Axum requires all functions to be async","To avoid using structs"],"answer":1,"explain":"async lets a handler yield while waiting, so one server can juggle thousands of connections. The Tokio runtime drives this."},{"q":"What must be true of a type `T` for a handler to return `Json<T>`?","options":["T must be an integer","T must implement `Serialize` (usually via `#[derive(Serialize)]`)","T must be async","T must be a String"],"answer":1,"explain":"Json<T> serializes T to JSON, so T must implement Serialize — commonly by adding #[derive(Serialize)] to the struct."},{"q":"Why is doing slow blocking work inside an async handler a problem?","options":["It uses too much disk","It freezes the thread so other requests can't run","It changes the HTTP status code","It's actually fine and recommended"],"answer":1,"explain":"Blocking work stalls the async thread, starving other requests. Use async equivalents or offload heavy work to a dedicated thread."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the shared shape of every web server?","back":"A loop of request in, response out. A route (path + method) maps to a handler function that produces the response."},{"front":"What is an Axum handler, and how does it produce a response?","back":"An `async fn` mapped to a route. Its return value (e.g. &str, String, Json<T>) becomes the HTTP response — the framework serializes it."},{"front":"What starts the async runtime for an Axum server?","back":"`#[tokio::main]` on `async fn main`. Without a runtime, async code never runs."},{"front":"How do handlers share a resource like a database connection pool?","back":"Build it once at startup, attach with `.with_state(state)`, and receive it in handlers via `State<AppState>` — never one connection per request."}]},"wasm-basics":{"topic":"wasm-basics","title":"Rust and WebAssembly","quiz":[{"q":"Which compile target is used to build Rust for the browser / generic WASM hosts?","options":["wasm32-unknown-unknown","x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","wasm32-wasi","browser-wasm32"],"answer":0,"explain":"wasm32-unknown-unknown is the standard target for browser and generic WASM hosts — 32-bit WASM with no specific vendor or OS underneath it."},{"q":"What does #[wasm_bindgen] on a function actually generate?","options":["A unit test for the function","The marshalling/interop glue that lets JavaScript call the function and converts values between Rust and JS types","A benchmark harness","Documentation comments"],"answer":1,"explain":"#[wasm_bindgen] generates the code that converts values (like String <-> JS string) across the boundary and emits a JS wrapper, so the function is directly callable from JavaScript."},{"q":"What does `wasm-pack build` do that plain `cargo build --target wasm32-unknown-unknown` does not?","options":["Nothing extra — they're identical commands","It runs the wasm-bindgen CLI over the compiled output and assembles a ready-to-import pkg/ directory with JS glue and a package.json","It installs the wasm32-unknown-unknown target automatically","It publishes the crate to crates.io"],"answer":1,"explain":"cargo build alone produces a raw .wasm file. wasm-pack build additionally runs wasm-bindgen's post-processing step and packages everything into an importable pkg/ directory."},{"q":"Why can't Rust code compiled to wasm32-unknown-unknown open a raw TCP socket?","options":["It's a Rust language limitation unrelated to WASM","The browser sandbox doesn't expose raw sockets to WASM code at all — networking only happens through JS APIs like fetch or WebSocket","TCP sockets require a feature flag that's always disabled by default","It can, as long as std::net is imported"],"answer":1,"explain":"The browser sandbox has no concept of raw sockets for WASM — any networking has to go through JavaScript-provided APIs like fetch() or WebSocket, which Rust code must call into."},{"q":"What does console_error_panic_hook::set_once() do, and when should you call it?","options":["It prevents panics from happening at all","It makes a panic's real message and location show up in the browser console instead of an opaque JS exception; call it once at startup during development","It disables all console output for performance","It converts panics into Result::Err automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without a panic hook, a Rust panic in WASM surfaces as an unhelpful, message-free JS runtime error. Installing the hook once at startup restores a real, readable panic message."},{"q":"In Cargo.toml, what crate-type does wasm-bindgen's tooling need to produce a .wasm module plus JS bindings?","options":["bin","cdylib","proc-macro","staticlib only, with no rlib"],"answer":1,"explain":"wasm-bindgen's CLI post-processes a cdylib (C-compatible dynamic library) artifact. A plain rlib, the default library format, isn't something it can turn into a WASM+JS package."},{"q":"What's the practical difference between `wasm-pack build --target web` and the default `--target bundler`?","options":["There is no difference — both flags produce identical output","--target web generates an ES module you initialize yourself; --target bundler assumes a tool like webpack is handling that initialization step","--target web only works in Firefox","--target bundler skips generating a .wasm file entirely"],"answer":1,"explain":"The generated JS glue differs by target. Using the wrong one breaks the import/init pattern your JS code expects, since web and bundler targets structure the output differently."},{"q":"Why should you avoid making many small calls across the JS/Rust boundary with large or complex data?","options":["wasm-bindgen has a hard limit of 10 calls per second","Each boundary crossing has real conversion/serialization cost, so many small calls with big payloads add up; typed arrays or serde-wasm-bindgen are better for bulk data","JavaScript can only call one Rust function per page load","It causes a compile error, not a performance issue"],"answer":1,"explain":"Crossing the WASM/JS boundary isn't free — every call converts values between representations. For large data, prefer typed arrays or serde-wasm-bindgen over many small calls."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is Rust an unusually good fit for compiling to WebAssembly?","back":"It has no garbage collector and a very small runtime, so compiled WASM stays small and fast without needing a language runtime shipped alongside it."},{"front":"What target do you compile Rust to for the browser, and how do you install it?","back":"wasm32-unknown-unknown, installed once per machine with `rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`."},{"front":"What does #[wasm_bindgen] generate?","back":"The interop glue that lets JavaScript call a Rust function directly — converting values like String <-> JS string automatically and emitting a matching JS/TypeScript wrapper."},{"front":"What does `wasm-pack build` do beyond a plain cargo build?","back":"It compiles to wasm32-unknown-unknown AND runs the wasm-bindgen CLI over the result, producing a pkg/ directory with the .wasm file, JS glue, types, and a package.json — ready to import or publish."},{"front":"Name three capabilities a browser-targeted WASM module does NOT have access to.","back":"A real filesystem (std::fs has nothing to act on), normal OS threads (std::thread::spawn doesn't work the usual way), and raw TCP/UDP sockets (only fetch/WebSocket via JS)."},{"front":"What does an un-hooked panic look like in the browser console, and how do you fix it?","back":"An opaque, message-free error like 'RuntimeError: unreachable executed'. Call console_error_panic_hook::set_once() at startup (e.g. in a #[wasm_bindgen(start)] function) to get the real panic message and location."},{"front":"What crate-type must Cargo.toml declare for wasm-bindgen's tooling to work, and why?","back":"crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"] in [lib]. wasm-bindgen needs the cdylib (C-compatible dynamic library) artifact to post-process into a .wasm module plus JS bindings; rlib alone isn't enough."},{"front":"Why does crossing the JS/Rust boundary with large data need care?","back":"Each call across the boundary has real conversion cost. For big payloads, use typed arrays or serde-wasm-bindgen rather than many small individual calls."}]}};
